const SEARCH_INDEX = [{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"What a Thesis Is and How It Works","x":"1 Chapter 1 of 24 · Part A · The research process What a Thesis Is and How It Works Research versus development, the contributions a thesis can make, kinds of research, the research cycle, working with a supervisor, planning the year and what examiners look for. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters Two students build the same face-recognition app. One gets a B on the project. The other writes a thesis that other researchers cite. The code was almost identical. The second student asked a question — does it still work for faces with masks, and why not? — measured it fairly against existing methods and explained the answer. That is research. This chapter explains what a thesis is, what it contributes, how the year runs and how to work with your supervisor."},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 1 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. What research is What a thesis contributes The research process Planning the year Habits and pitfalls"},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"What a thesis is","x":"Section 1 of 5 What a thesis is A thesis is a written report of your own research : you ask a question nobody has fully answered, collect evidence in a planned way, and explain what the evidence shows. It ends with a contribution — something the field knows or can do now that it could not before. It starts from a question , not from a product idea. It builds on existing work : you must show what is already known. It uses a method others could repeat. It reports evidence honestly, including what did not work. It is judged by examiners on originality, rigour and clarity. In one sentence: a thesis answers a research question with evidence, and shows why the answer matters."},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Research versus development","x":"Section 1 of 5 Research versus development Development project Goal: a working product Success: “it works and users like it” Novelty: not required Comparison with others: optional Example: build a Bangla OCR mobile app Research thesis Goal: new knowledge Success: the question is answered with evidence Novelty: required — something new, or new evidence Comparison with existing work: essential Example: does fine-tuning on synthetic handwriting improve Bangla OCR on old documents? Most good computer science theses also build something — a model, a tool, a system. The difference is that it is built to answer a question , and it is measured against what already exists."},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Thesis or project?","x":"Interactive Thesis or project?"},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"What a thesis can contribute","x":"Section 2 of 5 What a thesis can contribute A new method An algorithm, model or technique that beats or simplifies the current best. A new dataset or benchmark Data that did not exist, with labels, documentation and baseline results — very valuable for local languages and problems. An empirical study A careful comparison of existing methods on a new setting, explaining when each works. A system with evaluation A tool or prototype, measured with users or experiments."},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"What a thesis can contribute (cont.)","x":"Section 2 of 5 What a thesis can contribute (cont.) A survey or systematic review A structured map of a field that identifies trends and open problems. A replication Repeating a published study on new data to test whether its findings hold. An undergraduate thesis does not need to change the world: a clear, honest, well-measured contribution in a narrow area is a success."},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Kinds of research","x":"Section 2 of 5 Kinds of research Design Question it answers Typical CS example Experimental Does X cause a better Y? compare a new model with baselines on benchmark data Design science Can we build an artefact that solves the problem? build and evaluate a phishing-detection browser extension Empirical / observational What is happening, and how often? mine 10,000 GitHub projects for security misconfigurations Survey / user study What do people think or do? questionnaire and interviews on how students use AI tools Case study How does it work in one real setting? deploying a clinic queue system in one hospital Simulation What would happen under conditions we cannot test live? simulate a 500-node IoT network in ns-3 Theoretical Can we prove a property? prove a bound on an algorithm’s running time Research is quantitative (numbers, statistics), qualitative (interviews, themes) or mixed . Chapter 7 shows how to choose."},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"The research cycle","x":"Section 3 of 5 The research cycle"},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"The research cycle (cont.)","x":"Section 3 of 5 The research cycle (cont.) Research is a loop, not a straight line. You will go round it several times: early experiments change your questions, and new papers change your method. Question → what exactly do you want to know? Literature → what is already known? Method → how will you find out? Experiment → collect the evidence. Analysis → what does it show, and how sure are you? Writing → explain it so others can check and build on it. Rest on a step of the cycle for a short description."},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Who does what","x":"Section 3 of 5 Who does what Person Role What they expect from you You (and your partner) do the research and write the thesis — Supervisor guides direction, checks quality, signs off milestones regular progress, honest reports, questions asked early Co-supervisor (sometimes) adds expertise in one area the same, on their part Examiners / committee judge the proposal, the thesis and the defence a clear contribution and convincing evidence Department sets deadlines, format and rules forms submitted on time, the format followed Your supervisor is a guide, not a manager. You own the thesis: plan the work, propose next steps and bring evidence to every meeting."},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Working with your supervisor","x":"Section 3 of 5 Working with your supervisor Agree a meeting rhythm (weekly or fortnightly) and keep it. Send a short progress report the day before: done, problems, plan, questions. Bring evidence : numbers, plots, draft pages — not just “I am working on it”. Write down the decisions and actions at the end of each meeting and email them back. Ask early when you are stuck for more than a few days. Never disappear: a short “no progress, because…” email is better than silence. Before each meeting What did I do since last time? What did I learn (results)? What blocked me? What do I plan next? Which decision do I need from my supervisor?"},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Planning the year","x":"Section 4 of 5 Planning the year"},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Milestones and deliverables","x":"Section 4 of 5 Milestones and deliverables Topic agreed with your supervisor Proposal document + presentation Literature review a chapter draft Methodology data, method, metrics fixed Experiments results with analysis Thesis full report Defence presentation + viva Paper submitted to a venue Each milestone has a checklist on the milestones page . Your department sets the dates; work backwards from the final deadline."},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"What examiners look for","x":"Section 4 of 5 What examiners look for Criterion Good evidence of it Originality a clear statement of what is new, and a comparison showing it Rigour a sound method, fair baselines, repeated runs, statistics Knowledge of the field a literature review that is critical and up to date Critical analysis results explained, not just listed; failures discussed Clarity a logical structure, readable writing, good figures Honesty limitations and threats to validity stated openly"},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Habits of good researchers","x":"Section 5 of 5 Habits of good researchers Read every week Two or three papers a week, with notes. Reading is part of the work, not a phase that ends. Keep a research diary Date, what you tried, the result, what you will try next. You will need it when writing. Start small Run a tiny experiment in week one. A small working pipeline beats a big plan. Version everything Code and LaTeX in Git, data with a README, results with the command that produced them. Write as you go A paragraph about every experiment, the day you run it. The thesis is then mostly editing. Back up Three copies, two places, one off your laptop. A lost laptop must not cost the thesis."},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Why theses go wrong — and how to prevent it","x":"Section 5 of 5 Why theses go wrong — and how to prevent it Problem Prevention The topic is too big narrow it to one question you can answer in the time (chapter 2) No data, or data arrives late secure the data before committing to the topic Months of reading, no experiments build a baseline by the end of the first month Results “do not work” a negative result, analysed honestly, is still a result Writing left to the last month write a paragraph per experiment as you go Lost contact with the supervisor fixed meeting rhythm and weekly reports Copying text or code cite everything; write in your own words (chapter 9)"},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 3 Worked example 1 Classify each as development (D) or research (R): (a) “Build a website for our department’s alumni.” (b) “Which of three Bangla sentiment models generalises best to social-media text from a different year?” (c) “Develop a library management app with barcode scanning.” (d) “Does adding traffic-light timing data reduce the error of bus-arrival prediction in Dhaka?” Show the solution (a) D — the goal is a working product. (b) R — a comparison question answered with evidence. (c) D — a product. (d) R — a question about an effect, measured against a baseline."},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 3 Worked example 2 Turn this project into a research question: “An app that detects plant diseases from leaf photos.” Show the solution Find what is unknown. For example: “How much does accuracy drop when a model trained on lab photos is used on farmers’ phone photos in the field, and does data augmentation close the gap?” Now there is a question, a comparison (lab vs field, with vs without augmentation) and a measurable answer."},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Worked example 3","x":"Worked example 3 of 3 Worked example 3 A pair of students has 12 months. Their data collection is delayed by one month. What do they change? Show the solution They do not move the final deadline. They (1) start the literature review and method on a public dataset in the meantime, (2) shorten the experiment phase by running fewer variants, (3) write the background chapters during the delay, and (4) tell their supervisor at once and agree the new plan in writing."},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Name three things that make a thesis different from a software project. Show the answer It answers a research question; it compares with existing work; it reports evidence from a planned method (and is judged on originality and rigour, not only on whether the software works). 2. List four kinds of contribution an undergraduate thesis can make. Show the answer A new method, a new dataset or benchmark, an empirical comparison of existing methods in a new setting, a system with evaluation, a survey, or a replication study. 3. Put the steps of the research cycle in order: analysis, question, writing, experiment, literature, method. Show the answer Question → literature → method → experiment → analysis → writing, and then back to new questions."},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=23","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. What should a weekly report to your supervisor contain? Show the answer What you did, what you learned (results, with numbers or plots), what blocked you, the plan for next week and the decisions you need. 5. Your experiments show your idea does not beat the baseline. Is the thesis failed? Show the answer No. A negative result that is measured fairly and explained (why it did not work, in which cases) is a valid contribution. Report it honestly and analyse it. 6. Why should you build a simple baseline in the first month? Show the answer It tests the data and the pipeline early, gives a number to beat, reveals problems while there is still time, and gives you something concrete to discuss with your supervisor."},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=24","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=25","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"For your next supervisor meeting","x":"Bring this to your supervisor For your next supervisor meeting Bring this A half-page note: three areas that interest you, why each one does, and the questions you want to ask your supervisor about how theses work in your department (deadlines, groups, assessment). See an acceptable example Areas: (1) NLP for Bangla — I use Bangla daily online and see how badly tools handle it; (2) medical imaging — a relative’s X-ray was misread; (3) software testing — I enjoyed the testing course. Questions: How many students per thesis group? When is the proposal defence? Is a paper expected? See this step in the worked thesis Milestone checklist"},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=26","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Essay You and Your Research — Richard Hamming ↗ A classic talk on choosing important problems and working on them. Guide How to do Research at the MIT AI Lab ↗ Practical advice from graduate students on reading, writing, supervisors and getting unstuck. Guide How to write a great research paper — Simon Peyton Jones ↗ Short talk and slides on starting from the idea and writing early. Book How to Write a Better Thesis — Evans, Gruba & Zobel Springer, 2014. Planning and writing each part of a thesis. Book The Craft of Research — Booth et al. University of Chicago Press, 4th ed. 2016. From a topic to a question to an argument. Tool Thesis milestone checklists Seven milestones with checklists you can tick; saved in your browser."},{"u":"topics/01-what-a-thesis-is.html#s=27","w":"Chapter 1 · What a Thesis Is and How It Works","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary A thesis answers a research question with evidence and states its contribution. Research differs from development: it needs a question, a method, a comparison with existing work and honest evidence. Contributions include new methods, datasets, empirical studies, evaluated systems, surveys and replications. Research is a cycle: question, literature, method, experiment, analysis, writing — repeated. You own the thesis; meet your supervisor regularly with evidence and a plan. Start small, read every week, write as you go, version and back up everything. Next: Choosing a Topic and Research Questions More practice All resources Milestone checklist All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","x":"2 Chapter 2 of 24 · Part A · The research process Choosing a Topic and Research Questions Where topics come from, narrowing an area to a problem and a research question, the FINER check, hypotheses and objectives, scoping the work, and getting the topic agreed. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters “Sir, I want to do something with AI.” The supervisor smiles; it is the fifth time this week. It is an interest, not a thesis. A thesis needs a question narrow enough to answer in a year, with data you can get and a result you can measure. The topic decides more about your thesis than anything you do later. This chapter turns an interest into a precise research question and shows how to test it before you commit."},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 2 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. Finding a topic From topic to question Writing research questions Scoping and agreeing"},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Where good topics come from","x":"Section 1 of 4 Where good topics come from Your interests A course you enjoyed, a project you are proud of, a problem you meet every day. You will live with the topic for a year. Recent papers The “future work” and “limitations” sections of recent papers list open problems, often stated by the authors themselves. Local problems Bangla language, local agriculture, Dhaka traffic, local health data: problems the big labs ignore, where your data and context are an advantage. Your supervisor Supervisors often have ideas, data or ongoing projects. A topic inside their expertise gets better guidance."},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Where good topics come from (cont.)","x":"Section 1 of 4 Where good topics come from (cont.) Industry and society A company or NGO problem with real data and real users can become a strong thesis — if you can publish the results. Replication “Does this published result hold on our data?” is a modest but honest and useful starting point."},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"From area to objectives","x":"Section 2 of 4 From area to objectives"},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"From area to objectives (cont.)","x":"Section 2 of 4 From area to objectives (cont.) A topic becomes researchable only when it is narrow enough to answer. Each step narrows the one before: Area Natural language processing Topic Sentiment analysis of Bangla text Problem Models trained on product reviews fail on social-media comments with mixed Bangla and English Research question Does continued pre-training on code-mixed text improve sentiment F1 on Banglish comments? Hypothesis It improves macro-F1 by at least 3 points over the base model Objectives Collect and label 5,000 comments; fine-tune two models; compare; analyse errors"},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"A good topic is…","x":"Section 2 of 4 A good topic is… Criterion Ask yourself Interesting Will I still care about it in ten months? Feasible Can I finish it with my skills, time and hardware (or a free GPU)? Data-ready Does the data exist, or can I collect and label it in a few weeks? Novel Is there something not yet done — a new setting, data, method or comparison? Supervised Does a supervisor know the area? Measurable Is there a clear way to tell whether I succeeded? Useful Who benefits if I answer it?"},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Score your candidate topics","x":"Interactive Score your candidate topics"},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Research questions","x":"Section 3 of 4 Research questions A research question (RQ) states exactly what you want to find out. Most theses have one main question and two or three sub-questions. It is a question , not a task (“Does…?”, “How…?”, “Which…?”, “To what extent…?”). A comparative question names the population or data , the method , the comparison and the outcome . An exploratory question (interviews, observation) names the people and setting and the experience or behaviour to understand; it has no comparison. It can be answered with evidence within your time. Its answer is not obvious in advance. Templates Does X improve Y for Z compared with W ? Which of A , B , C works best for task on data ? How can we design a system that does T under constraint C ? Why does method M fail on case K ?"},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"The FINER check","x":"Section 3 of 4 The FINER check Letter Meaning Fails when… F Feasible : time, data, skills, compute “train a new large language model from scratch” I Interesting : to you and to the field a toy problem nobody uses N Novel : confirms, extends or challenges earlier findings it repeats a tutorial exactly E Ethical : no harm, consent, privacy respected scraping private health data without consent R Relevant : matters for science, users or society the answer would change nothing FINER comes from clinical research and works well for computing too."},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Check a research question","x":"Interactive Check a research question"},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Weak and strong questions","x":"Section 3 of 4 Weak and strong questions Weak Why Strong Can AI detect fake news? too broad; already answered “yes, sometimes” Do transformer models trained on English fake-news data transfer to Bangla news, and how much does fine-tuning on 2,000 labelled Bangla articles help? Make a better spam filter. a task, not a question; “better” is undefined Does adding URL features reduce false positives of an SMS spam classifier at equal recall? Is blockchain good for voting? vague; no measurable outcome What throughput and latency can a permissioned blockchain achieve for a 10,000-voter election simulation? Study students’ use of ChatGPT. no question, no population How do CSE undergraduates at one university use AI assistants for programming tasks, and which uses do they consider dishonest?"},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Hypotheses, objectives and contributions","x":"Section 4 of 4 Hypotheses, objectives and contributions Hypothesis A testable prediction: “Method A will have higher F1 than B on dataset D.” Experiments either support or reject it. Not every thesis needs one (design and exploratory studies often do not). Objectives The concrete steps to answer the questions: collect…, implement…, compare…, analyse… . Make them SMART: specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound. Contributions are written last, as results: “We release a dataset of…”, “We show that…”, “We propose… which improves… by…”."},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Scoping: small enough to finish","x":"Section 4 of 4 Scoping: small enough to finish Fix one main task, dataset family and metric. Prefer depth (one problem studied well) to breadth (five problems done badly). Write down what is out of scope . Plan a minimum result (must have) and stretch goals (nice to have). Example Too big: “AI for Bangladeshi agriculture.” Still big: “Detect diseases of all crops from images.” Right size: “Detect five common rice-leaf diseases from phone photos; compare three CNNs; test robustness to field lighting.”"},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Getting the topic agreed","x":"Section 4 of 4 Getting the topic agreed Shortlist two or three topics, scored One-page idea problem, question, data, method, why it matters Meet discuss with a supervisor Read 5–10 key papers on the chosen topic Refine rewrite the question Agree topic and plan confirmed in writing The one-page idea is your first research document. Keep it: it becomes the start of your proposal."},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 3 Worked example 1 Narrow the interest “cybersecurity” to a research question for a one-year thesis. Show the solution Area: cybersecurity → topic: phishing detection → problem: URL-based detectors trained on English sites miss phishing pages in Bangla → question: “Do character-level URL models trained on global phishing data detect Bangla-language phishing sites, and does adding page-text features improve recall?” → objectives: collect 3,000 labelled Bangla URLs/pages, train two models, compare recall at a fixed false-positive rate, analyse misses."},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 3 Worked example 2 Apply FINER to: “Build a general AI that diagnoses all diseases from medical images.” Show the solution F: fails — impossible data and compute. I: interesting. N: as stated, not novel enough to define. E: risky without clinical oversight and consent. R: relevant. Fix: “Detect diabetic retinopathy grade from fundus images in a public dataset, and test performance on images from a local smartphone fundus camera.”"},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Worked example 3","x":"Worked example 3 of 3 Worked example 3 Write a hypothesis and two objectives for: “Does data augmentation help a small Bangla handwritten-digit dataset?” Show the solution Hypothesis: “Training with rotation and elastic-distortion augmentation increases test accuracy by at least 2 percentage points compared with no augmentation.” Objectives: (1) train the same CNN five times with and without augmentation on a fixed split; (2) compare mean accuracy with a paired test and analyse which digits improve."},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Name four sources of thesis topics. Show the answer Your interests, the future-work sections of recent papers, local problems and data, your supervisor’s projects, industry or NGO problems, replications of published results. 2. What makes a research question precise? Answer for a comparative study and for an exploratory one. Show the answer A comparative question (method A against B) names the population or data, the method, the comparison and the outcome measure: “Does continued pre-training improve macro-F1 of BanglaBERT on code-mixed comments compared with fine-tuning alone?” An exploratory question, for an interview or observation study, names the people, the setting and what you want to understand: “How do first-year CSE students at one university use AI assistants when debugging, and what problems do they report?” It is precise because who, where and what are fixed, even though nothing is compared. 3. Expand FINER. Show the answer Feasible, Interesting, Novel, Ethical, Relevant."},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. Improve: “Can machine learning predict exam results?” Show the answer For example: “Do features from LMS activity in the first four weeks predict which students will fail a course better than prior GPA alone, measured by recall of at-risk students?” — population, method, comparison and outcome are now explicit. 5. What is the difference between an objective and a contribution? Show the answer An objective is a planned step (“compare three models”); a contribution is a result you can claim afterwards (“we show that model A is 4 points better on code-mixed text”). 6. Why write down what is out of scope? Show the answer It protects the plan from growing, tells examiners you made a deliberate choice, and gives you natural future work."},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=23","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"For your next supervisor meeting","x":"Bring this to your supervisor For your next supervisor meeting Bring this One paragraph with your research question and its scope: what is in, what is out, the data you will use and how you will measure the answer. See an acceptable example RQ: Does continued pre-training of BanglaBERT on unlabelled code-mixed comments improve sentiment classification of Banglish Facebook comments, compared with fine-tuning alone, measured by macro-F1? In scope: three classes (positive, negative, neutral), 5,000 labelled comments. Out of scope: sarcasm detection as a separate task, other platforms. See this step in the worked thesis Milestone checklist"},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=24","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Essay You and Your Research — Richard Hamming ↗ On choosing important problems. Search Google Scholar ↗ Search “future work” together with your topic to find open problems. Map Connected Papers ↗ Start from one paper and see the neighbourhood of a topic. Code Hugging Face Papers ↗ What is trending in machine learning this week, with code. Book The Craft of Research — Booth et al. Chapters 3–4: from topics to questions to problems. Guide Quick reference: research question templates Templates and the FINER check on one page."},{"u":"topics/02-topics-and-research-questions.html#s=25","w":"Chapter 2 · Choosing a Topic and Research Questions","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary Topics come from interests, recent papers’ future work, local problems, supervisors, industry and replications. Narrow step by step: area → topic → problem → research question → hypothesis → objectives. A good research question names the data, the method, the comparison and the outcome. Check it with FINER: feasible, interesting, novel, ethical, relevant. Scope tightly: one main task and metric; write down what is out of scope. Secure the data and agree the topic with your supervisor in writing. Next: Searching the Literature More practice All resources Milestone checklist All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Searching the Literature","x":"3 Chapter 3 of 24 · Part A · The research process Searching the Literature Where to search (Google Scholar, Semantic Scholar, IEEE Xplore, ACM, arXiv, DBLP, PubMed), building Boolean search strings, filters, snowballing, getting full text legally and keeping a search log. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters Three months into your brilliant new method, you find a paper from last year that did exactly the same thing. Better. With a nicer figure. A systematic search prevents that heartbreak and hands you the datasets, baselines and metrics you need. It also shows examiners that your claim of novelty rests on evidence. This chapter shows where to search, how to write search strings, how to follow citations and how to record what you did."},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 3 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. Where to search Search strings Following citations Recording the search"},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Why search systematically","x":"Section 1 of 4 Why search systematically A literature search is not “type a few words into Google”. You need to find the important papers, the recent ones and the ones that do almost what you plan — before your examiners do. It shows what is already known, so you do not reinvent it. It gives you baselines, datasets and metrics. It reveals the gap your thesis fills. A recorded search can be repeated and defended."},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Search engines and digital libraries","x":"Section 1 of 4 Search engines and digital libraries Source Best for Notes Google Scholar everything; citation counts; “Cited by” widest coverage, little filtering; set up alerts Semantic Scholar AI-assisted search, TL;DR summaries, influential citations free; good for CS and biomedicine IEEE Xplore networks, security, signal and image processing, IoT full text often through the university library ACM Digital Library software engineering, HCI, systems, data many papers now open access arXiv the newest ML, AI, CV and NLP work preprints: not yet peer-reviewed DBLP an author’s or venue’s full list of papers bibliographic index, very accurate Scopus / Web of Science curated, quality-filtered search; journal metrics through the university subscription PubMed biomedical and health papers essential for biomedical theses"},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Discovery tools","x":"Section 1 of 4 Discovery tools Connected Papers Enter one good paper; see a graph of similar papers, older “prior works” and newer “derivative works”. Hugging Face Papers Trending ML papers with code and models. (Papers with Code closed in 2025; its archive is no longer updated.) Unpaywall Finds the legal free copy of a paywalled paper: the author’s version or a repository copy. Survey papers Search “ topic survey” or “ topic review”: one good survey maps hundreds of papers for you. Alerts Google Scholar alerts and arXiv daily listings bring new papers on your keywords to your inbox. Your library The university library gives access to IEEE, ACM, Springer and Elsevier full text; ask about remote access."},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Building a search string","x":"Section 2 of 4 Building a search string Write the RQ as concepts : e.g. sentiment · Bangla · social media . For each concept list synonyms : Bangla / Bengali; sentiment / opinion / emotion; social media / Facebook / YouTube comments. Join synonyms with OR inside brackets, concepts with AND . Use \"quotes\" for exact phrases and * for word endings where supported. Run it, read the first results, refine the words — and record each version. (\"Bangla\" OR \"Bengali\") AND (sentiment OR opinion OR emotion) AND (\"social media\" OR Facebook OR YouTube) Google Scholar ignores brackets and wildcards in some cases; use fewer, more specific terms there, and the full string in IEEE Xplore, ACM, Scopus."},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Build a search query","x":"Interactive Build a search query"},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Filters and search fields","x":"Section 2 of 4 Filters and search fields Filter Use it to… Year range keep the last 5 years for methods; keep older seminal papers too Title / abstract only cut noise from papers that mention a word once Publication type separate journals, conferences, surveys and preprints Venue restrict to the top conferences of your field Author follow a leading group’s work Sort by citations vs date citations finds classics; date finds the frontier"},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Snowballing","x":"Section 3 of 4 Snowballing"},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Snowballing (cont.)","x":"Section 3 of 4 Snowballing (cont.) Start from a few strong “seed” papers and follow citations both ways: Backward: read the reference list of the seed — what it builds on. Forward: use “Cited by” in Google Scholar or Semantic Scholar — who built on it since. Repeat with the best new papers until no new relevant papers appear. Keyword search plus snowballing finds far more of the relevant work than either alone."},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Getting the full text legally","x":"Section 3 of 4 Getting the full text legally Open the link from the university network or library proxy (IEEE, ACM, Springer, Elsevier). Look for an open-access version: arXiv, the author’s homepage, a university repository, Unpaywall. Check ResearchGate or the author’s profile for an accepted version. Email the author politely — most are happy to send their paper. Ask your supervisor or the library for an inter-library request. Do not use pirate sites. Besides being illegal, they often serve wrong versions and malware."},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Keep a search log","x":"Section 4 of 4 Keep a search log Date Database Query Filters Results Kept 3 Feb IEEE Xplore (\"Bangla\" OR \"Bengali\") AND sentiment 2019–2025, title/abstract 142 18 3 Feb Google Scholar Bangla sentiment social media since 2020 ≈ 2,400 12 (first 100 screened) 5 Feb Snowball backward from Sharma et al. 2023 — 61 refs 7 The log makes your review reproducible and lets you write the “search method” paragraph of your literature review in minutes. A spreadsheet is enough."},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"How much is enough?","x":"Section 4 of 4 How much is enough? You keep meeting the same papers and authors — the search is saturating . You can name the 3–5 most important papers and the current best result. You know the standard datasets, baselines and metrics. You can state the gap in one paragraph. Typical numbers Screen a few hundred titles, read 40–80 abstracts, read 20–40 papers properly, and cite 30–60 in an undergraduate thesis. Quality beats quantity."},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 2 Worked example 1 Turn the RQ “Does data augmentation improve rice-leaf disease classification on smartphone photos?” into a search string. Show the solution Concepts and synonyms: (rice OR paddy) · (leaf disease OR plant disease) · (classification OR detection OR recognition) · (augmentation OR “synthetic data”). String: (rice OR paddy) AND (\"leaf disease\" OR \"plant disease\") AND (classif* OR detect* OR recogni*) AND (augmentation OR \"synthetic data\") . In Google Scholar, simplify to: rice leaf disease detection augmentation smartphone ."},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 2 Worked example 2 A seed paper has 45 references and is cited by 120 papers. How do you snowball efficiently? Show the solution Backward: scan the 45 titles, keep those on your problem (say 8). Forward: in “Cited by”, sort by date or search within citing articles for your keywords; scan titles of the 120, keep about 10. Read abstracts of the 18 and repeat from the 3–4 best. Record each step in the search log."},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Name three databases and say what each is best for. Show the answer For example: Google Scholar (widest coverage and citations), IEEE Xplore (networks, signal processing, IoT), arXiv (the newest ML/AI preprints); also ACM DL, DBLP, Semantic Scholar, PubMed. 2. What do AND, OR and quotes do in a search string? Show the answer AND requires all concepts (narrower); OR allows any synonym (wider); quotes keep an exact phrase. 3. Explain backward and forward snowballing. Show the answer Backward: follow a paper’s reference list to older work. Forward: follow “Cited by” to newer papers that build on it."},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. Why is a preprint on arXiv treated with care? Show the answer It has not necessarily been peer-reviewed; check whether a reviewed version was later published, and read its claims critically. 5. List four legal ways to get a paywalled paper. Show the answer University library access or proxy; arXiv or other open-access copy; author homepage or repository (Unpaywall finds it); emailing the author; inter-library request. 6. What should a search log record? Show the answer Date, database, exact query, filters, number of results, number kept — so the search can be repeated and described in the thesis."},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"For your next supervisor meeting","x":"Bring this to your supervisor For your next supervisor meeting Bring this Your search log: databases, the exact query strings, dates, filters and the number of results, plus the 10 most relevant papers found so far. See an acceptable example 12 Mar · Google Scholar · (“code-mixed” OR “code-switched” OR Banglish) AND sentiment AND (Bangla OR Bengali) · 2018–2026 · 412 results, first 100 screened · 14 kept. 12 Mar · IEEE Xplore · same string · 37 results · 6 kept (3 duplicates). Snowballing from the two most cited papers: 5 more. See this step in the worked thesis Milestone checklist"},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Guide Google Scholar search help ↗ Operators, alerts, “Cited by” and library links. Search Semantic Scholar ↗ AI-assisted search with summaries and citation context. Library IEEE Xplore ↗ Advanced search with command search for Boolean strings. Library ACM Digital Library ↗ Computing literature from ACM conferences and journals. Preprints arXiv ↗ Browse cs.CL, cs.CV, cs.LG, cs.CR and other categories daily. Map Connected Papers ↗ Graph of related prior and derivative work. Access Unpaywall ↗ Legal open-access copies. Index DBLP ↗ Complete publication lists for authors and venues."},{"u":"topics/03-searching-the-literature.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 3 · Searching the Literature","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary Search systematically: it finds the key, recent and closest work, plus datasets, baselines and metrics. Use several sources: Google Scholar, Semantic Scholar, IEEE Xplore, ACM DL, arXiv, DBLP (PubMed for biomedicine). Build strings from concepts and synonyms: OR within a concept, AND between concepts, quotes for phrases. Snowball backward (references) and forward (“Cited by”) from strong seed papers. Get full text legally: library access, open-access copies, authors. Keep a search log; stop when results saturate and you can state the gap. Next: Selecting and Judging Papers More practice All resources Milestone checklist All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Selecting and Judging Papers","x":"4 Chapter 4 of 24 · Part A · The research process Selecting and Judging Papers Screening search results in stages with inclusion and exclusion criteria, the PRISMA flow, judging venues (CORE ranks, journal quartiles, predatory venues) and judging the quality of a paper itself. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters Your search returned 1,800 papers. You have one month and a limited supply of tea. You cannot read them all — and you shouldn’t. Screening in stages with written criteria, judging the venue and judging the paper itself turns a mountain into a reading list. Done well, it saves weeks and keeps weak or fake sources out of your thesis. This chapter turns a long list of results into a short list you can trust and defend."},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 4 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. Screening Judging quality Judging the paper"},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"From hundreds of results to the papers that matter","x":"Section 1 of 3 From hundreds of results to the papers that matter Identify all results from every database Remove duplicates the same paper found twice Screen titles clearly off-topic? drop Screen abstracts against your criteria Read full text decide finally Include the papers you will review Selection is a funnel. Do it in stages, with written criteria, so that the decision for every paper can be explained — and repeated by someone else."},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Inclusion and exclusion criteria","x":"Section 1 of 3 Inclusion and exclusion criteria Include if… addresses Bangla or Bengali text performs sentiment or emotion classification reports quantitative results published 2018 or later peer-reviewed, or a preprint from a known group Exclude if… only English or other languages no evaluation (position papers) not available in full text a duplicate or an earlier version of the same work published in a predatory venue Write the criteria before screening. If you change them, write down when and why."},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Count your screening with PRISMA","x":"Interactive Count your screening with PRISMA"},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Is the venue trustworthy?","x":"Section 2 of 3 Is the venue trustworthy? Signal What it tells you Where to check Conference rank (A*, A, B, C) how selective a CS conference is CORE portal Journal quartile (Q1–Q4) the journal’s standing in its subject area SCImago (SJR) , Scopus, Web of Science Indexed in Scopus / Web of Science / DBLP meets basic quality and regularity criteria search the index for the venue Publisher ACM, IEEE, Springer, Elsevier, ACL, AAAI, PMLR, MDPI, Wiley… the venue’s website Peer review experts checked the work before publication the venue’s review policy In machine learning, NLP and vision the top conferences (NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR, ACL, EMNLP, CVPR, ICCV) matter as much as journals. In many other areas journals carry more weight."},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Predatory journals and conferences","x":"Section 2 of 3 Predatory journals and conferences Promise acceptance in days, or “guaranteed publication”. Ask for fees before review, or hide the fees. Send flattering spam emails inviting you to submit or to join the editorial board. Have a very broad scope (“engineering, science and management”). Show fake or invented impact factors. Have no real peer review, and editors you cannot verify. A paper in a predatory venue is weak evidence; publishing your own thesis work there can harm your record. Check with Think. Check. Submit. and ask your supervisor."},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Check a venue","x":"Interactive Check a venue"},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Judging the paper itself","x":"Section 3 of 3 Judging the paper itself Question Good sign Warning sign Is the problem clear? precise task, data and metric vague goals, buzzwords Is the comparison fair? strong, recent baselines on the same data only weak or old baselines Is the data described? source, size, splits, licence “we collected data” with no detail Are results reliable? several runs, variance, significance tests one run, tiny test set, no variance Can it be reproduced? code and data released, settings listed no code, missing hyperparameters Are limitations discussed? honest limitations section only claims of success Has it been retracted or corrected? none check Retraction Watch"},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Citations and recency","x":"Section 3 of 3 Citations and recency Citation count shows attention, not correctness. Old papers have more; new strong papers have few. Compare a paper with others of the same age in the same field. Seminal papers (the ones that started a line of work) are worth citing even if old. For methods and results, prefer the last 3–5 years ; the state of the art moves fast. Balanced reading list 2–3 seminal papers · 2–3 recent surveys · 10–20 recent papers close to your problem · the papers that introduced your datasets and metrics · a few from your local context."},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Preprints, blogs and grey literature","x":"Section 3 of 3 Preprints, blogs and grey literature Source Use it for Be careful because… arXiv preprint the newest methods not yet reviewed; check for a later published version and cite that Technical reports, theses details and negative results quality varies Blog posts, tutorials understanding and code not citable as evidence for a scientific claim Company white papers industry context may be marketing Wikipedia, ChatGPT and other AI tools orientation and keywords never cite as a source; AI tools can invent papers — verify every reference"},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 3 Worked example 1 A search found 412 records (IEEE 140, ACM 72, Scopus 200). 96 were duplicates. Title screening removed 210, abstract screening 70, and full-text reading 14. How many papers are included? Show the solution 412 − 96 = 316 screened by title; 316 − 210 = 106 abstracts; 106 − 70 = 36 full texts; 36 − 14 = 22 included . Report these numbers in a PRISMA flow diagram."},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 3 Worked example 2 An email invites you to publish in the “International Journal of Advanced Science, Engineering and Management”, with review in 72 hours and a fee of 150 USD. What do you do? Show the solution Treat it as predatory: very broad scope, unrealistic review time, unsolicited email and up-front fee. Check it on Scopus and in the Think. Check. Submit. list, ask your supervisor, and do not submit or cite it as strong evidence."},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Worked example 3","x":"Worked example 3 of 3 Worked example 3 Paper A (2017, 900 citations) and paper B (2024, 15 citations) both address your problem. Which do you cite? Show the solution Both, for different reasons: A as the seminal work that defined the approach, B as the current state of the art and a likely baseline. Citation counts must be compared within the same age."},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Name the stages of screening. Show the answer Identify, remove duplicates, screen titles, screen abstracts, read full texts, include. 2. Give two inclusion and two exclusion criteria for a review of phishing-detection papers. Show the answer Include: detects phishing URLs or pages; reports quantitative evaluation. Exclude: only email spam (not phishing); no evaluation; predatory venue; duplicate version. 3. Where do you check a conference’s rank and a journal’s quartile? Show the answer Conference rank: the CORE portal. Journal quartile: SCImago (SJR), Scopus or Web of Science."},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. List four signs of a predatory venue. Show the answer Guaranteed or very fast acceptance; fees before review; spam invitations; very broad scope; fake impact factors; no verifiable editors or peer review. 5. Why is a high citation count not proof that a paper is correct? Show the answer Citations measure attention (and age); a paper can be cited often for being criticised or simply for being early. Correctness comes from method and evidence. 6. Can you cite a blog post or ChatGPT as evidence? Show the answer No. Use them for understanding or keywords, then find and cite the peer-reviewed source; verify every reference an AI tool gives you, because it can invent papers."},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"For your next supervisor meeting","x":"Bring this to your supervisor For your next supervisor meeting Bring this Your inclusion and exclusion criteria and a PRISMA-style count: found, screened, read in full, included. See an acceptable example Include: peer-reviewed or arXiv with code, 2018 or later, sentiment or emotion on Bangla or Bangla–English text. Exclude: no evaluation, no dataset description, predatory venues. Counts: 549 found → 418 after duplicates → 61 after titles and abstracts → 24 read in full → 17 included. See this step in the worked thesis Milestone checklist"},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Standard PRISMA 2020 statement and flow diagram ↗ Templates for reporting screening and selection. Ranking CORE conference portal ↗ Search a conference to see its rank. Ranking SCImago Journal Rank ↗ Journal quartiles by subject category. Integrity Think. Check. Submit. ↗ Checklist to identify trustworthy journals. Integrity Retraction Watch Database ↗ Check whether a paper has been retracted. Book Guidelines for Systematic Literature Reviews in Software Engineering — Kitchenham & Charters Technical report, 2007. The standard method for systematic reviews in computing."},{"u":"topics/04-selecting-and-judging-papers.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 4 · Selecting and Judging Papers","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary Screen in stages — duplicates, titles, abstracts, full text — against criteria written in advance. Report the numbers with a PRISMA flow diagram. Judge the venue: CORE rank for conferences, SJR quartile for journals, indexing and peer review. Avoid predatory venues: fast guaranteed acceptance, up-front fees, spam, vague scope. Judge the paper: clear problem, fair baselines, described data, reliable results, reproducibility, honest limitations. Balance seminal and recent work; use preprints with care; never cite AI chatbots or unverified references. Next: Reading Papers and Managing Notes More practice All resources Milestone checklist All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"Reading Papers and Managing Notes","x":"5 Chapter 5 of 24 · Part A · The research process Reading Papers and Managing Notes How a paper is built, the three-pass reading method, questions to ask, reading results critically, one-paper summaries, the literature matrix, reference managers and getting through hard papers. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters You read a 12-page paper carefully, word by word, for three hours. A week later, all you remember is that Figure 2 was blue. Researchers don’t read papers like novels. They read in passes, with questions, and write notes they can use later — that is how they get through dozens of papers without drowning. This chapter gives you a reading method, a note template and a literature matrix."},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 5 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. Reading strategy Reading critically Notes and the literature matrix Getting unstuck"},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"How a research paper is built","x":"Section 1 of 4 How a research paper is built Section What it tells you Read it… Title and abstract the problem, method and main result in 200 words always, first Introduction motivation, the gap, the contributions (often a bulleted list) in pass 1 Related work how it differs from earlier papers to find more papers Method what exactly they did in pass 2–3 Experiments / results data, baselines, metrics, tables, figures in pass 2; check carefully Discussion and limitations what the results mean and where they fail always — gold for thesis ideas Conclusion and future work summary and open problems in pass 1"},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"The three-pass method","x":"Section 1 of 4 The three-pass method"},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"The three-pass method (cont.)","x":"Section 1 of 4 The three-pass method (cont.) Pass 1 — 5 to 10 minutes. Title, abstract, introduction, section headings, figures, conclusion. Decide: relevant? read on? Pass 2 — about an hour. Read everything except the hardest proofs and details. Study figures and tables. Mark references to read. Summarise it in your own words. Pass 3 — several hours. Only for the papers your thesis builds on. Re-derive, or re-implement; question every assumption; note strengths, weaknesses and ideas. After S. Keshav, “How to Read a Paper”, ACM SIGCOMM Computer Communication Review, 2007."},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"Questions to ask while reading","x":"Section 1 of 4 Questions to ask while reading Problem What exactly is the problem, and why does it matter? Novelty What is new compared with earlier work? Is the claim supported? Method What did they do? Could I explain it to a classmate in two minutes? Evidence Which data, baselines and metrics? Do the numbers support the conclusion? Any statistics? Limits What assumptions? Where would it fail? What did they not test? For me What can I reuse (dataset, baseline, metric, code)? What would I do differently?"},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"Reading results tables critically","x":"Section 2 of 4 Reading results tables critically Model Accuracy F1 Baseline (2019) 81.2 0.62 Strong baseline (2023) 86.9 0.74 Proposed 87.4 0.75 Is +0.5 accuracy significant , or within run-to-run noise? Look for standard deviations or tests. Why does accuracy (87%) differ so much from F1 (0.75)? Probably class imbalance . Were all models tuned equally, on the same split ? Is the test set large enough? 0.5% of 200 samples is one example. Which classes or cases does the method still get wrong?"},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"Taking notes that you can use","x":"Section 3 of 4 Taking notes that you can use One-paper summary Citation author, year, venue (with BibTeX key) Problem one sentence Method two or three sentences Data datasets, sizes Results the main numbers Limitations what it does not do Relevance how it relates to my RQ Ideas what I could try Write notes in your own words ; copied sentences in notes become accidental plagiarism later. Mark exact quotes with quotation marks and the page. Give every paper a tag (theme, method, dataset). Keep notes in one place: Zotero notes, a spreadsheet or Obsidian."},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"The literature matrix","x":"Interactive The literature matrix"},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"Reference managers","x":"Section 3 of 4 Reference managers Tool Strengths Zotero (free, open source) save papers from the browser in one click, annotate PDFs, groups for sharing with a partner, Better BibTeX for LaTeX Mendeley (free) PDF library and annotation; Word plugin JabRef (free) BibTeX-native; good with Overleaf Overleaf + .bib file the end point: export BibTeX from the manager into the thesis Choose one tool on day one and put every paper in it. Re-finding a paper you read three months ago costs far more than saving it now."},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"Organising your library","x":"Section 3 of 4 Organising your library Folders or tags by theme (e.g. datasets, transformer models, augmentation, evaluation). A tag for reading status : to read, pass 1, pass 2, key paper. Consistent citation keys (author + year + first word: sharma2023bangla ). One shared group library if you work in a pair. A weekly reading rhythm Monday: pass 1 on 10 new titles · Wednesday: pass 2 on the best 2–3 · Friday: update the matrix and write one paragraph comparing what you read."},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"Understanding hard papers","x":"Section 4 of 4 Understanding hard papers Read a survey or tutorial on the topic first; then the paper makes sense. Look up unknown terms in a textbook, not only in a chatbot — and check the answer. Find the paper’s code and run it; code removes ambiguity. Find a talk or blog by the authors: they often explain the idea more simply. Discuss it in a reading group with classmates — explaining is the best test of understanding. It is normal to read a key paper three or four times."},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 2 Worked example 1 After pass 1 of a paper you know: it proposes a lightweight CNN for skin-lesion classification, tested on HAM10000, with 2% higher balanced accuracy than MobileNetV2. Write the matrix row. Show the solution Problem: skin-lesion classification on mobile devices. Method: lightweight CNN with … (fill after pass 2). Data: HAM10000 (≈10,000 dermatoscopic images, 7 classes). Results: +2% balanced accuracy over MobileNetV2. Limitations: (pass 2) single dataset, no external test. Relevance: candidate baseline for my mobile skin-cancer thesis."},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 2 Worked example 2 A table shows the proposed model at 92.1% accuracy and the baseline at 91.8%, one run each, on 300 test images. What do you conclude? Show the solution 0.3% of 300 images is about one image. With one run and no variance, the difference is within noise. You cannot conclude the proposed model is better; you would need several runs, variance and a significance test (chapter 8)."},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. What do you read in pass 1? Show the answer Title, abstract, introduction, section headings, figures, conclusion (and glance at the references). 2. Which papers deserve pass 3? Show the answer Only the few your thesis builds on directly: your main baselines and the methods you extend. 3. Name five fields of a one-paper summary. Show the answer Citation, problem, method, data, results, limitations, relevance to my question, ideas."},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. Why must notes be in your own words? Show the answer Copied sentences end up in the thesis without quotation marks and become plagiarism; paraphrasing also tests your understanding. 5. What is a literature matrix? Show the answer A table with one row per paper and columns such as problem, data, method, metric, results and limitations; it shows patterns and gaps across papers. 6. Give three ways to understand a paper you find too hard. Show the answer Read a survey or tutorial first; run the authors’ code; watch the authors’ talk; discuss in a reading group; reread it several times."},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"For your next supervisor meeting","x":"Bring this to your supervisor For your next supervisor meeting Bring this Three completed rows of your literature matrix: problem, data, method, results and limitations for each paper. See an acceptable example Paper 1 (2021): Bangla sentiment, 8k formal news comments, BiLSTM, F1 0.71; limitation: formal script only. Paper 2 (2022): code-mixed hate speech, 5k comments, mBERT, F1 0.76; limitation: no Banglish sentiment. Paper 3 (2023): BanglaBERT fine-tuning on 6 tasks, strong on formal text; limitation: not tested on Latin-script Bangla. See this step in the worked thesis Milestone checklist"},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Paper How to Read a Paper — S. Keshav ↗ The three-pass method in three pages. Tool Zotero documentation ↗ Saving, annotating, groups and BibTeX export. Tool Better BibTeX for Zotero ↗ Stable citation keys and automatic .bib export for LaTeX. Search Semantic Scholar ↗ TL;DR summaries help in pass 1. Book Doing a Literature Review — Chris Hart SAGE, 2nd ed. 2018. Reading critically and analysing arguments."},{"u":"topics/05-reading-papers.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 5 · Reading Papers and Managing Notes","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary Read in passes: a 10-minute scan, a one-hour read, and a deep read only for key papers. Ask the same questions of every paper: problem, novelty, method, evidence, limits, use for me. Read results critically: significance, imbalance, fair tuning, test-set size, failure cases. Write one-paper summaries in your own words; mark quotes with quotation marks. Keep a literature matrix: it reveals patterns and the gap. Use one reference manager from day one; tag papers by theme and status. Next: Writing the Literature Review More practice All resources Milestone checklist All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"Writing the Literature Review","x":"6 Chapter 6 of 24 · Part A · The research process Writing the Literature Review The purpose of a literature review, synthesis instead of summary, organising by theme, method or time, comparison tables, stating the research gap, writing well, and systematic versus narrative reviews. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters Thirty paragraphs, each starting “X et al. proposed…”. The examiner sighs: “This is a shopping list, not a literature review.” A literature review is an argument: here is what the field knows, here is how it fits together, and here is the missing piece — which my thesis supplies. This chapter shows how to turn your notes and literature matrix into that argument."},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 6 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. Purpose Synthesis Structure The gap Writing"},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"What a literature review is for","x":"Section 1 of 5 What a literature review is for Shows you know the field: the key ideas, methods, datasets and results. Organises earlier work so the reader sees its structure and trends . Evaluates it critically: strengths, weaknesses, contradictions. Ends with the gap — what is missing — which your thesis fills. Justifies your choices of method, baselines, data and metrics. A literature review is an argument that leads to your research question. It is not a list of paper summaries."},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"Summary versus synthesis","x":"Section 2 of 5 Summary versus synthesis Summary (weak) “Rahman et al. [3] used an SVM and got 78% accuracy. Islam et al. [4] used an LSTM and got 82%. Das et al. [5] used BERT and got 88%.” Paper by paper; the reader must find the pattern. Synthesis (strong) “Accuracy on Bangla sentiment rose from below 80% with feature-based models [3] to 82–88% with neural and transformer models [4, 5]. All three, however, use product reviews; none evaluates code-mixed social-media text, where informal spelling is common.” Grouped, compared, and leading to a gap."},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"Summary or synthesis?","x":"Interactive Summary or synthesis?"},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"Ways to organise the review","x":"Section 3 of 5 Ways to organise the review Organisation Use when Example headings Thematic the field has clear sub-problems (most common) Datasets · Feature-based methods · Deep learning · Code-mixed text Methodological you compare families of methods Rule-based · Classical ML · Transformers · LLM prompting Chronological the history explains the present 2010–2015 lexicons · 2016–2019 RNNs · 2020– transformers By problem dimension several aspects must be covered Accuracy · Robustness · Efficiency · Fairness Within each section, move from broad to specific : the general approach, the closest work to yours, and finally what is missing."},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"The shape of the review","x":"Section 3 of 5 The shape of the review"},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"The shape of the review (cont.)","x":"Section 3 of 5 The shape of the review (cont.) Introduce the scope: what the review covers and how papers were found (your search log). Sections by theme, each comparing and evaluating the work. Comparison table of the closest papers (from your literature matrix). Gap : one clear paragraph on what has not been done and why it matters. Bridge : how your research questions address the gap."},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"The comparison table","x":"Section 3 of 5 The comparison table Work Data Method Code-mixed? Metric Result [3] 2019 product reviews, 8k SVM + TF-IDF no accuracy 78% [4] 2021 product reviews, 12k BiLSTM no accuracy 82% [5] 2023 news comments, 20k BanglaBERT partly macro-F1 0.81 This thesis Facebook comments, 5k BanglaBERT + continued pre-training yes macro-F1 — A good table makes the gap visible : the last row has a “yes” where every earlier row has “no”."},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"Stating the gap","x":"Section 4 of 5 Stating the gap Not yet studied: no work has evaluated X on Y. Contradiction: [4] and [7] report opposite effects of Z. Limitation: existing methods need large labelled data, which low-resource languages lack. Not compared fairly: methods were evaluated on different splits, so it is unclear which is better. Context: results from English or Western hospitals may not transfer to local data. “No one has done it” is not enough: also say why it matters that it is done."},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"Writing it well","x":"Section 5 of 5 Writing it well Do Do not Group papers and compare them write one paragraph per paper Use your own words and cite every idea copy sentences from abstracts Report numbers with their setting (data, metric) compare numbers from different datasets as if equal Evaluate: strengths and limitations only praise Use reporting verbs precisely: shows, proposes, claims, reports say “proves” for an experiment Keep it focused on your question cover everything you read"},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"Systematic versus narrative reviews","x":"Section 5 of 5 Systematic versus narrative reviews Narrative review the usual thesis chapter searches widely but informally organised by the author’s judgement faster; risk of missing work Systematic review (SLR) a research method in itself protocol, search strings, criteria written in advance PRISMA flow, quality assessment, data extraction reproducible; takes months — can be a whole thesis Even in a narrative review, describe your search and criteria briefly. It costs one paragraph and adds a lot of credibility."},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 2 Worked example 1 Rewrite as synthesis: “A [1] used CNNs on PlantVillage (99%). B [2] used ResNet on PlantVillage (99.5%). C [3] used MobileNet on field photos (81%).” Show the solution “On the lab-photo PlantVillage dataset, CNN models reach 99% accuracy or more [1, 2]. On field photos, however, accuracy falls to around 81% [3], suggesting that background, lighting and occlusion — absent in lab images — remain the main obstacle. Few studies test models trained in the lab on field images directly.”"},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 2 Worked example 2 Choose an organisation for a review on intrusion detection with three families of methods (signature-based, classical ML, deep learning) and two concerns (accuracy and explainability). Show the solution Methodological sections for the three families, each evaluated on accuracy, followed by a cross-cutting section on explainability, a comparison table, and the gap (e.g. deep models are accurate but rarely explained to analysts)."},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. State three purposes of a literature review. Show the answer Show knowledge of the field; organise and critically evaluate earlier work; identify the gap and justify your research question and methods. 2. What is the difference between summary and synthesis? Show the answer Summary describes papers one by one; synthesis groups and compares them to reveal patterns, contradictions and gaps. 3. Name four ways to organise a review. Show the answer Thematic, methodological, chronological, by problem dimension."},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. Give three kinds of research gap. Show the answer Not yet studied in this setting; contradictory results; limitations of current methods; no fair comparison; results not tested in a local context. 5. Why is it wrong to compare 92% on dataset A with 85% on dataset B? Show the answer Different data (size, difficulty, splits, metrics) makes the numbers incomparable; compare only on the same benchmark and protocol. 6. What makes a review systematic? Show the answer A protocol written in advance: research questions, search strings, databases, inclusion/exclusion criteria, screening with PRISMA, quality assessment and structured data extraction."},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"For your next supervisor meeting","x":"Bring this to your supervisor For your next supervisor meeting Bring this A one-page outline of your literature review organised by theme, ending in one paragraph that states the gap. See an acceptable example Themes: (1) sentiment for formal Bangla; (2) code-mixed South Asian text; (3) domain-adaptive pre-training. Gap: models for formal Bangla are strong, and domain-adaptive pre-training helps other languages, but no study tests it for sentiment in Latin-script Banglish, and no labelled Banglish sentiment dataset is public. See this step in the worked thesis Milestone checklist"},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Book Doing a Literature Review — Chris Hart SAGE, 2nd ed. 2018. Analysis, synthesis and argument. Standard PRISMA 2020 ↗ Reporting guideline for systematic reviews. Guide Purdue OWL: quoting, paraphrasing and summarising ↗ When to quote, paraphrase or summarise. Book Writing for Computer Science — Justin Zobel Springer, 3rd ed. 2014. Chapters on reading and describing related work. Book Guidelines for Systematic Literature Reviews in Software Engineering — Kitchenham & Charters Technical report, 2007. Protocol, search, selection and extraction."},{"u":"topics/06-writing-the-literature-review.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 6 · Writing the Literature Review","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary A literature review is an argument that ends in the gap your thesis fills. Synthesise: group, compare and evaluate papers instead of summarising them one by one. Organise by theme (most common), method, time or problem dimension; go from broad to specific. Use a comparison table to make the gap visible. State the gap and why it matters; bridge to your research questions. Describe your search briefly; a full systematic review is a method of its own. Next: Designing Your Methodology More practice All resources Milestone checklist All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Designing Your Methodology","x":"7 Chapter 7 of 24 · Part A · The research process Designing Your Methodology Choosing a research design, the experimental pipeline, describing a proposed method, choosing baselines and metrics, threats to validity and writing the research proposal. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters “My model gets 94% accuracy!” The examiner asks: “Compared with what, on which data, and would it be 94% tomorrow?” The room goes quiet. A methodology answers those questions before you start, so that your result means something. Without it, even a correct result cannot convince anyone. This chapter shows how to choose a design, describe your method, pick fair baselines and metrics, and anticipate threats to validity."},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 7 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. Designs The proposed method Comparison and measurement Validity The proposal"},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"What a methodology is","x":"Section 1 of 5 What a methodology is The methodology is the plan that turns your research question into evidence: what data, what method, compared with what, measured how. It must be detailed enough that another student could repeat your study. It follows from the research question — not from the tool you want to use. It is decided before the experiments, then refined. It names its own weaknesses (threats to validity). A methodology answers Which research design? What data, and how is it prepared? What is the proposed method? What is it compared with (baselines)? How is success measured (metrics)? How is the evaluation set up? What could make the results wrong?"},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Choosing a research design","x":"Section 1 of 5 Choosing a research design"},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Choosing a research design (cont.)","x":"Section 1 of 5 Choosing a research design (cont.) If your question is… Use… Does X improve Y? controlled experiment Can we build something that solves P? design science: build, then evaluate How often / how much happens in practice? empirical study, data mining What do people think or do? survey, interviews, user study What happens at a scale we cannot test? simulation How does it work in one real setting? case study Rest on a branch of the tree for a description."},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Which design fits?","x":"Interactive Which design fits?"},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"The experimental pipeline","x":"Section 2 of 5 The experimental pipeline Data : source, size, labels, licence, ethics. Pre-processing : cleaning, normalisation, tokenisation, augmentation — applied the same way to every method. Proposed method : what is new, with a diagram and every setting. Baselines : the methods you compare against. Evaluation : split, metrics, number of runs, statistics."},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Describing your proposed method","x":"Section 2 of 5 Describing your proposed method A block diagram of the whole system, then each block in detail. Equations or pseudocode for the new parts; cite the parts you reuse. All hyperparameters and how they were chosen (on validation data). Hardware and software: GPU, library versions, training time. Why each design choice was made — and the alternatives you rejected. Write it so a classmate could re-implement it without asking you. If something is “standard”, cite the source that defines it."},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Baselines","x":"Section 3 of 5 Baselines Kind of baseline Why include it Example Trivial shows the task is not trivial always predict the majority class; random Classic a simple, well-known method logistic regression or SVM on TF-IDF State of the art the method you must beat or match the best recent model on this benchmark Ablation of your own method shows what each part contributes your model without the new component Tune baselines as carefully as your own method, on the same data and split. A weak, untuned baseline makes any method look good — and examiners know it."},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Choosing metrics","x":"Section 3 of 5 Choosing metrics Task Common metrics Note Balanced classification accuracy, F1 — Imbalanced classification macro-F1, balanced accuracy, AUC-PR, recall at fixed precision accuracy misleads Detection / segmentation mAP, IoU, Dice report per class Regression / forecasting MAE, RMSE, MAPE MAE is easier to explain Ranking / retrieval precision@k, MRR, nDCG — Text generation BLEU, ROUGE, BERTScore + human evaluation automatic metrics are rough Systems latency, throughput, memory, energy report the hardware User studies task time, success rate, SUS, errors with enough participants Choose the metric that matches the question : if missing a disease is worse than a false alarm, report recall (sensitivity)."},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Pick the right metric","x":"Interactive Pick the right metric"},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Threats to validity","x":"Section 4 of 5 Threats to validity Threat Question Mitigation Internal validity Is the improvement really caused by my method? same data and tuning for all methods; ablation; several seeds External validity Do results hold beyond this dataset? test on a second dataset or real-world data Construct validity Does the metric measure what I care about? choose metrics carefully; add human evaluation Conclusion validity Is the difference statistically reliable? enough data, variance, significance tests Every thesis has threats. Naming them, with what you did about them, is a strength, not a weakness."},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"The research proposal","x":"Section 5 of 5 The research proposal Title and one-paragraph summary Introduction: problem, motivation, research questions, objectives Literature review: key work and the gap (shorter than in the thesis) Methodology: design, data, proposed method, baselines, metrics, evaluation setup Expected contributions and risks (with fallback plans) Timeline (Gantt chart) and resources needed References The proposal is a contract with your supervisor and committee: once approved, the methodology is the plan you follow — and changes are discussed, not hidden."},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 3 Worked example 1 RQ: “Does continued pre-training on code-mixed text improve sentiment classification of Banglish comments?” Design the methodology in six lines. Show the solution Design: controlled experiment. Data: 5,000 labelled comments, 70/15/15 stratified split, plus a public Bangla dataset for external testing. Proposed: BanglaBERT with continued pre-training on 1M unlabelled code-mixed comments, then fine-tuning. Baselines: majority class; TF-IDF + logistic regression; BanglaBERT fine-tuned only; multilingual XLM-R. Metrics: macro-F1 (primary), per-class F1. Setup: 5 seeds, mean ± sd, paired test on macro-F1; error analysis of 100 misclassified comments."},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 3 Worked example 2 A student plans to compare their model with a 2016 SVM baseline only. What do you advise? Show the solution Add a trivial baseline (majority class), a strong recent baseline (the current best on that dataset) and an ablation of their own model. Tune all of them with the same budget on the same split. Otherwise the improvement is not convincing."},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Worked example 3","x":"Worked example 3 of 3 Worked example 3 A cancer-screening model has 98% accuracy on data with 2% positive cases. Which metrics should be reported? Show the solution Accuracy is misleading (predicting “healthy” always gives 98%). Report sensitivity (recall), specificity, precision (PPV), F1 or AUC-PR, and the confusion matrix — with sensitivity as the primary metric if missing a cancer is the main risk."},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. What are the main parts of a methodology? Show the answer Research design, data, pre-processing, proposed method, baselines, metrics, evaluation setup, and threats to validity. 2. Which design for: “How do software developers in Dhaka use code-review tools?” Show the answer A survey and/or interviews (qualitative or mixed methods), possibly with mining of repository data. 3. Name four kinds of baseline. Show the answer Trivial (majority/random), classic simple method, state of the art, ablations of your own method."},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. Why is accuracy a poor metric for imbalanced data? Show the answer Predicting the majority class gives high accuracy while missing the minority class completely; use macro-F1, recall, balanced accuracy or AUC-PR. 5. Explain external validity with an example. Show the answer Whether results generalise beyond the study: a model tested only on lab photos may fail on field photos; testing on a second, real-world dataset checks it. 6. List the sections of a research proposal. Show the answer Title and summary, introduction (problem, RQs, objectives), literature review and gap, methodology, expected contributions and risks, timeline, references."},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"For your next supervisor meeting","x":"Bring this to your supervisor For your next supervisor meeting Bring this A one-page evaluation plan: research design, data and split, baselines, metrics, the statistical comparison and the main threats to validity. See an acceptable example Controlled experiment. 5,000 comments, 70/15/15 split stratified by class and grouped by Facebook page. Baselines: majority class, TF-IDF + logistic regression, BanglaBERT fine-tuned. Metric: macro-F1 (classes are imbalanced). 5 seeds; mean ± sd; paired t-test over seeds plus a bootstrap CI on the test set. Threats: label noise (two annotators, κ), one platform only. See this step in the worked thesis Milestone checklist"},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Guide scikit-learn: metrics and scoring ↗ Definitions and code for classification, regression and ranking metrics. Checklist NeurIPS paper checklist ↗ What a sound ML experiment should report. Book Writing for Computer Science — Justin Zobel Springer, 3rd ed. 2014. Chapters on experiments, hypotheses and evidence. Book Experimentation in Software Engineering — Wohlin et al. Springer, 2nd ed. 2024. Designs, validity threats and analysis for empirical studies. Guide Thesis milestone 2: research proposal Checklist for the proposal."},{"u":"topics/07-designing-your-methodology.html#s=23","w":"Chapter 7 · Designing Your Methodology","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary The methodology turns the research question into evidence and must be repeatable. Choose the design from the question: experiment, design science, empirical study, survey, simulation or case study. Describe the proposed method fully: diagram, settings, choices and alternatives. Compare with trivial, classic, state-of-the-art and ablation baselines, tuned equally. Pick metrics that match the question; accuracy misleads on imbalanced data. Name threats to validity and what you did about them; the proposal fixes the plan. Next: Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility More practice All resources Milestone checklist All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","x":"8 Chapter 8 of 24 · Part A · The research process Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility Preparing data, train/validation/test splits and cross-validation, data leakage, running and logging experiments, metrics from the confusion matrix, p-values, confidence intervals and significance tests, ablation and error analysis, presenting results and reproducibility. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters Two months of work: your model beats the baseline by 0.4%. The examiner asks: “Would it still win with a different random seed?” You check. It doesn’t. Experiments are only as convincing as their setup: clean splits, no leakage, several runs, the right metrics and a test of whether differences are real. This chapter turns “I trained a model” into “I can show, with evidence, that the method works — and why”."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 8 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. Data Avoiding leakage Experiments Measuring Is the difference real? Analysis Reproducibility"},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Getting and preparing data","x":"Section 1 of 7 Getting and preparing data Public datasets : cite the paper that introduced them; check the licence. Your own data : plan collection, consent and storage (chapter 9); write a labelling guide. Labelling : at least two annotators on a sample; report agreement (Cohen’s κ). Clean : remove duplicates and broken items; document every rule. Describe : size, classes, class balance, sources, time period. A datasheet for your data Who collected it, when, how, for what purpose; what each label means; known gaps and biases; licence and how to access it. Put it in an appendix."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Train, validation and test","x":"Section 1 of 7 Train, validation and test Train : the model learns from it. Validation : you tune hyperparameters and choose models with it. Test : used once , at the end, to report the result. Split before any analysis; keep class balance (stratify). For small data use k-fold cross-validation : every item is tested exactly once."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Data leakage","x":"Section 2 of 7 Data leakage Leak How it happens Fix Duplicates across splits the same image or sentence is in train and test remove duplicates before splitting Same subject in both photos of one patient or one user in train and test split by patient / user / device Future information random split of time-series data split by time: train on the past, test on the future Pre-processing on all data normalisation or feature selection fitted on train + test fit on train only, then apply Tuning on the test set choosing the best epoch by test score tune on validation; test once Leakage makes results look excellent and then fail in practice. Kapoor and Narayanan (2023) found leakage errors in hundreds of published studies across many fields that use machine learning — see the Princeton leakage catalogue ."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Leak or no leak?","x":"Interactive Leak or no leak?"},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Running experiments","x":"Section 3 of 7 Running experiments Start with the simplest baseline end to end; then add complexity. Change one thing at a time and record it. Fix and record random seeds ; run each setting at least 5 times (10 if you may need the Wilcoxon test). Give every method the same tuning budget . Log every run: config, commit hash, metrics, time (a spreadsheet, or MLflow / Weights & Biases). An experiment log row 2026-03-04 · run 17 · commit a3f9c1 · bert-base, lr 2e-5, 4 epochs, seed 3 · val F1 0.812 · test — · note: augmentation on"},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Measuring results: the confusion matrix","x":"Section 4 of 7 Measuring results: the confusion matrix"},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"What a p-value says","x":"Section 5 of 7 What a p-value says Suppose A and B were really equally good . The p-value is how often a difference at least as large as the one you saw would appear just from random variation. A small p means your result would be surprising if there were no real difference. p < 0.05 does not mean… because… “there is a 95% chance A is better” p assumes no difference; it is not the probability of a hypothesis “the difference is large” with enough data a tiny difference becomes significant “p = 0.20, so A and B are equal” a non-significant result is no evidence either way, not proof of equality Report three things: the size of the difference, its 95% confidence interval and the p-value with the test used. “A beat B by 2.0 F1 points (95% CI 1.1 to 2.9; paired t-test over 5 seeds, p = 0.003).”"},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Choose the test by what one pair is","x":"Section 5 of 7 Choose the test by what one pair is Your pairs are… Use Watch out seeds on one fixed split paired t-test; Wilcoxon signed-rank if far from normal shows only training randomness, not how the score varies with other test data folds of one (repeated) cross-validation corrected resampled t-test (Nadeau and Bengio), or the 5×2 cv test folds share most training data, so the ordinary paired t-test and Wilcoxon overstate the evidence separate, independent test sets or datasets paired t-test or Wilcoxon; with many datasets, Friedman + post-hoc use at least 6 pairs for Wilcoxon two classifiers on one test set McNemar’s test , plus a bootstrap confidence interval over the test items one split only: say so With 5 pairs the Wilcoxon test can never give p < 0.05 — its smallest possible p is 2 ÷ 2 5 = 0.0625. Plan the number of runs before you choose the test."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Beyond p: size, uncertainty, many comparisons","x":"Section 5 of 7 Beyond p: size, uncertainty, many comparisons Effect size Give the difference in the metric’s own units (“+2.0 F1 points”), and if useful a standardised size such as Cohen’s d. Confidence interval The range of differences compatible with your data. A bootstrap interval: resample the test items with replacement 1,000 times, recompute the difference, take the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles. Many comparisons Testing your method against 6 baselines at 0.05 gives about a 26% chance of at least one false “win”. Correct with Holm–Bonferroni . Assumptions The t-test assumes roughly normal differences, which you cannot check with 5 values. More runs help more than any test."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Significance-test calculator","x":"Interactive Significance-test calculator"},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Ablation and error analysis","x":"Section 6 of 7 Ablation and error analysis Ablation study Remove or replace one component at a time and measure the change. It shows which part of your method gives the improvement. Variant Macro-F1 (5 seeds) Full model 0.812 ± 0.006 – continued pre-training 0.781 ± 0.009 – augmentation 0.804 ± 0.007 – both 0.769 ± 0.011 Error analysis Look at real mistakes: sample 50–100 errors, group them by cause, count each group. It explains why the method fails and gives you future work. Error type Count sarcasm 31 mixed sentiment 24 spelling variants 18 label noise 9"},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Presenting results","x":"Section 6 of 7 Presenting results A main results table: methods × metrics, mean ± sd, best in bold , the test, its p-value and a confidence interval noted. Plots: learning curves, per-class bars, confusion matrices, examples. Every figure and table numbered, captioned, and discussed in the text. Say what the numbers mean , not only what they are. Method Macro-F1 Accuracy Majority class 0.21 ± 0.00 0.46 ± 0.00 TF-IDF + LR 0.68 ± 0.01 0.73 ± 0.01 BanglaBERT 0.78 ± 0.02 0.81 ± 0.01 Ours 0.81 ± 0.01 † 0.84 ± 0.01 † Better than BanglaBERT in all 5 seeds on the same split: +3.0 points, paired t-test p = 0.004; 95% bootstrap CI of the test-set difference [0.01, 0.05]."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Reproducibility","x":"Section 7 of 7 Reproducibility Code in Git Commit often with clear messages; tag the version used for the thesis. Environment requirements.txt or environment.yml with versions; note the GPU and OS. Seeds and configs Every run from a config file and a recorded seed. Scripts for every number Each table and figure regenerated by one script from saved results. Data and models Release them if allowed (GitHub, Zenodo, Hugging Face), or explain why not. README How to install, run and reproduce the main table."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 4 Worked example 1 A model for detecting pneumonia is trained and tested on X-ray images split randomly. Many patients have several images. Is there a problem? Show the solution Yes: images of the same patient appear in both train and test, so the model can recognise the patient rather than the disease — leakage. Split by patient ID so that each patient’s images are all in one split, and report the result again."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 4 Worked example 2 Confusion matrix for “phishing” as positive: TP = 90, FN = 10, FP = 30, TN = 870. Compute precision, recall, F1 and accuracy. Show the solution Precision = 90 ÷ (90 + 30) = 0.75. Recall = 90 ÷ (90 + 10) = 0.90. F1 = 2 × 0.75 × 0.90 ÷ 1.65 = 0.818. Accuracy = (90 + 870) ÷ 1000 = 0.96. Accuracy looks excellent; precision shows one in four alarms is false."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Worked example 3","x":"Worked example 3 of 4 Worked example 3 Method A: F1 over 5 seeds 0.80, 0.82, 0.81, 0.79, 0.83. Method B on the same seeds: 0.78, 0.80, 0.80, 0.77, 0.80. How do you report and test it? Show the solution Means: A 0.810 ± 0.016, B 0.790 ± 0.014. Differences A − B: 0.02, 0.02, 0.01, 0.02, 0.03; mean 0.02, sd 0.0071; t = 0.02 ÷ (0.0071 ÷ √5) ≈ 6.3 with 4 degrees of freedom, p ≈ 0.003. 95% confidence interval: 0.02 ± 2.776 × 0.0071 ÷ √5 = [0.011, 0.029]. Report: “A beat B by 2.0 F1 points (95% CI 1.1 to 2.9; paired t-test over 5 seeds, p = 0.003).” Two cautions: the seeds share one test set, so this shows the gain is not training luck, not that it holds on other data; and a Wilcoxon test on these 5 pairs gives p = 0.0625, its smallest possible value, so use more seeds if you need it."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Worked example 4","x":"Worked example 4 of 4 Worked example 4 Two models are compared with 10-fold cross-validation. The fold differences A − B are 0.020, −0.005, 0.010, 0.015, −0.010, 0.020, 0.005, 0.000, 0.018, 0.007. Is A better? Show the solution Mean difference 0.008, sd 0.0106. An ordinary paired t-test gives t = 0.008 ÷ (0.0106 ÷ √10) = 2.39, p = 0.041 — apparently significant. But each pair of folds shares 8 of its 9 training parts, so the differences are not independent. The corrected resampled t-test (Nadeau and Bengio) multiplies the variance by (1/n + n test /n train ) = 1/10 + 1/9 instead of 1/10: standard error = 0.0106 × √0.211 = 0.0049, t = 1.65, df = 9, p = 0.13. Conclusion: no reliable evidence that A is better. Try it in the calculator with “Cross-validation folds” selected."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. What is each split for: train, validation, test? Show the answer Train: fit the model. Validation: tune and select models. Test: report the final result, used once. 2. Give three examples of data leakage. Show the answer Duplicates across splits; the same patient/user in train and test; random splits of time series; normalisation fitted on all data; tuning on the test set. 3. Why run each experiment with several seeds? Show the answer Results vary with initialisation and data order; several runs give a mean and standard deviation and allow a significance test."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. Which test compares two methods evaluated on the same 10 folds of one cross-validation, and why not the ordinary paired t-test? Show the answer The corrected resampled t-test (Nadeau and Bengio), or the 5×2 cv test. The folds share most of their training data, so their differences are not independent; the ordinary paired t-test — and the Wilcoxon test — treat them as independent and overstate the evidence. 5. What is the difference between an ablation study and error analysis? Show the answer Ablation removes components to measure their contribution; error analysis studies the mistakes to understand why the method fails. 6. Why can a Wilcoxon signed-rank test on 5 seeds never be significant at 0.05? Show the answer With 5 pairs there are only 2 5 = 32 equally likely sign patterns; even when all 5 differences favour A, the two-sided p is 2 ÷ 32 = 0.0625. Use at least 6 pairs, or the t-test if the differences look roughly normal."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=23","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=24","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"For your next supervisor meeting","x":"Bring this to your supervisor For your next supervisor meeting Bring this Your main results table (mean ± sd over seeds), one ablation and a first error analysis of 50 mistakes. See an acceptable example Majority 0.21 ± 0.00; TF-IDF + LR 0.68 ± 0.01; BanglaBERT 0.78 ± 0.02; ours 0.81 ± 0.01. Ablation: without continued pre-training 0.781. Errors (50): sarcasm 16, mixed sentiment 12, spelling variants 9, label noise 5, other 8. See this step in the worked thesis Milestone checklist"},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=25","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Guide scikit-learn: cross-validation ↗ Splits, k-fold, grouped and time-series splits. Paper Statistical Comparisons of Classifiers over Multiple Data Sets — J. Demšar ↗ JMLR, 2006. Which tests to use when comparing ML methods. Project Leakage and the reproducibility crisis in ML-based science ↗ A catalogue of leakage types found in published studies. Checklist NeurIPS paper checklist ↗ Reporting seeds, variance, compute and data."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=26","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Further learning (cont.)","x":"Resources Further learning (cont.) Paper Inference for the Generalization Error — C. Nadeau and Y. Bengio ↗ Machine Learning, 2003. The corrected resampled t-test for cross-validation. Paper Approximate Statistical Tests for Comparing Supervised Classification Learning Algorithms — T. Dietterich ↗ Neural Computation, 1998. McNemar and the 5×2 cv test. Statement The ASA Statement on p-Values — Wasserstein and Lazar ↗ The American Statistician, 2016. Six principles for reading p-values correctly. Tool MLflow tracking ↗ Free experiment tracking: parameters, metrics, artefacts."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=27","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Further learning (cont.)","x":"Resources Further learning (cont.) Guide Datasheets for Datasets — Gebru et al. ↗ Questions every dataset description should answer."},{"u":"topics/08-experiments-and-evaluation.html#s=28","w":"Chapter 8 · Experiments, Statistics and Reproducibility","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary Describe and document your data; measure label agreement; check licences. Split into train, validation and test (or use cross-validation); test once, at the end. Prevent leakage: no duplicates or subjects across splits, time-based splits, fit pre-processing on train only. Run several seeds, log every run, give every method the same tuning budget. Report the size of a difference with its confidence interval, and test it with a method that matches what one pair is (seeds, folds, test sets); add ablation and error analysis. Make every number reproducible: Git, environment, seeds, scripts, README. Next: Research Ethics and Integrity More practice All resources Milestone checklist All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Research Ethics and Integrity","x":"9 Chapter 9 of 24 · Part A · The research process Research Ethics and Integrity Fabrication, falsification and plagiarism; quoting, paraphrasing and citing; using AI tools responsibly; consent and privacy in research with people; data licences; fairness and harm; authorship. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters A thesis is submitted with excellent results. A year later, someone notices that the test set was used for tuning and two paragraphs came from a blog. The degree suddenly feels very nervous. Ethics is not a form signed at the end; it shapes how you collect data, run experiments, use AI tools and write. Most problems are not deliberate cheating but shortcuts and carelessness. This chapter lists the rules that protect you, your participants and the trust in your results."},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 9 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. Integrity Plagiarism and citing AI tools People and data Responsible research"},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Research integrity","x":"Section 1 of 5 Research integrity Research is useful only if others can trust it. Integrity means reporting what you really did and found, and giving credit for every idea and word that is not yours. Misconduct Meaning Fabrication inventing data or results Falsification changing data, removing inconvenient runs, editing images, picking only good seeds Plagiarism presenting others’ words, ideas, code or figures as your own Misconduct can lead to a failed thesis, a cancelled degree, or a retracted paper years later. No result is worth it."},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Plagiarism in all its forms","x":"Section 2 of 5 Plagiarism in all its forms Form Example Do instead Copy-paste sentences from a paper without quotes paraphrase in your own words and cite Patchwriting swapping a few words of the original understand, close the source, write Idea theft using a method without citing it cite the source of every method and idea Code copying code from GitHub without credit respect the licence; credit the repository Figures reusing a figure from a paper redraw and cite (“adapted from [x]”), or ask permission Self-plagiarism reusing your own earlier text without saying so cite your earlier work Contract cheating paying someone, or an AI, to write it never"},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Quote, paraphrase, summarise","x":"Section 2 of 5 Quote, paraphrase, summarise Original “Transformer models pretrained on large multilingual corpora transfer poorly to code-mixed text because their tokenisers fragment romanised words into many subword pieces.” Acceptable paraphrase Multilingual pretrained models struggle with code-mixed text, partly because romanised words are split into many small subword units by their tokenisers [12]. Quote rarely, with quotation marks and the citation — only when the exact words matter. Paraphrase : the same idea, your structure and words, with the citation. Summarise : the main point of a longer passage, with the citation."},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Acceptable or not?","x":"Interactive Acceptable or not?"},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Using AI tools responsibly","x":"Section 3 of 5 Using AI tools responsibly Usually acceptable (if allowed) Not acceptable brainstorming keywords and search terms generating thesis text and submitting it as yours explaining a concept you then verify in a textbook citing references the AI produced without checking them grammar and spelling suggestions on your own text inventing or “cleaning” data or results help debugging your own code uploading confidential or personal data to a public AI service Follow your department’s policy and agree the rules with your supervisor. Keep a short log of what you used and for what. Declare it in the thesis (a short statement in the methodology or acknowledgements). You are responsible for every word and number, whoever helped."},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Research with people","x":"Section 4 of 5 Research with people Informed consent : participants know the purpose, what they do, risks, and that they can stop at any time. Voluntary : no pressure — be careful with your own classmates or students. Privacy : collect only what you need; anonymise; store securely; delete when done. Approval : ask whether your university needs ethics-committee approval for surveys, interviews, user studies or health data. Vulnerable groups (children, patients) need extra protection and usually formal approval. A consent form contains who you are and your supervisor the purpose of the study what the participant will do, and how long it takes risks and benefits how data is stored, used and anonymised the right to withdraw contact details"},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Data, licences and privacy","x":"Section 4 of 5 Data, licences and privacy Situation What to check Public dataset licence terms (research only? attribution?) and cite the dataset paper Web scraping site terms of service, robots.txt, personal data in the content, rate limits Social-media posts platform rules; remove user names; do not quote identifiable posts in the thesis Medical data de-identification, approval, data-use agreement (e.g. PhysioNet credentialing) Company data written permission to use and to publish results Code and models licences (MIT, Apache, GPL, CC) and model-use restrictions"},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Fairness, bias and harm","x":"Section 5 of 5 Fairness, bias and harm Does the data represent the people the system affects (gender, region, language, skin tone, age)? Report results per group where it matters, not only an average. Could the system be misused (surveillance, deepfakes, attacks)? Say so, and how you limit it. In security research, follow responsible disclosure : tell the vendor before publishing a vulnerability. State the limitations so nobody uses the work beyond what it supports."},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Authorship and credit","x":"Section 5 of 5 Authorship and credit Situation Fair practice Thesis pair agree who does what; both can explain every part Paper from the thesis authors are those who contributed substantially; agree the order early (often student first, supervisor last) Help from others acknowledge it: annotators, a friend who shared code, a lab that lent a GPU Funding or data providers acknowledge as their agreement requires"},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 3 Worked example 1 Your model gives F1 0.70, 0.71, 0.84, 0.69 and 0.70 on five seeds. You are tempted to report only 0.84. Why not, and what do you report? Show the solution Reporting only the best seed is falsification by selection: it misrepresents the expected performance. Report the mean ± sd (0.728 ± 0.063), mention the unusual run, and investigate why that run differs."},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 3 Worked example 2 You plan a survey of 200 students about how they use AI for assignments. List the ethical steps. Show the solution Check whether ethics approval is needed; write a consent statement (purpose, voluntary, anonymous, right to stop); collect no names or IDs; store responses securely; avoid pressure (do not survey your own juniors as their tutor); report only aggregated results; delete raw data after the thesis as stated."},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Worked example 3","x":"Worked example 3 of 3 Worked example 3 You found a SQL-injection vulnerability in a local e-commerce site during your security thesis. What should you do? Show the solution Do not exploit it further or publish it immediately. Inform your supervisor, report it privately to the company (responsible disclosure) with enough detail to fix it, give them time, and describe it in the thesis without enabling attacks — ideally after it is fixed."},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Define fabrication, falsification and plagiarism. Show the answer Fabrication: inventing data or results. Falsification: manipulating or selectively reporting data or results. Plagiarism: presenting others’ words, ideas, code or figures as your own. 2. Is changing a few words of a sentence and citing it acceptable? Show the answer No — that is patchwriting. Understand the idea, write it in your own structure and words, and cite it; or quote it exactly with quotation marks. 3. Give two acceptable and two unacceptable uses of AI tools. Show the answer Acceptable (if allowed): brainstorming search terms; grammar suggestions on your own text. Unacceptable: submitting generated text as yours; citing unverified AI-produced references; uploading personal data."},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. What does informed consent require? Show the answer Participants understand the purpose, what they will do, risks, how data is used and stored, and that participation is voluntary and they can withdraw; they agree before taking part. 5. What must you check before using a public dataset? Show the answer Its licence and terms (e.g. research only, attribution), any personal data it contains, and the paper to cite. 6. What is responsible disclosure? Show the answer Reporting a security vulnerability privately to the affected vendor and allowing time to fix it before publishing details."},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"For your next supervisor meeting","x":"Bring this to your supervisor For your next supervisor meeting Bring this A short ethics note: where the data comes from, consent or terms of use, how you anonymise and store it, and how you will declare AI-tool use. See an acceptable example Public Facebook comments collected through the platform’s permitted tools; usernames and profile links removed before labelling; stored on a university drive; only comment text and labels released, under a research-only licence. AI use: Grammarly for grammar; no AI-written text; declared in the acknowledgements. See this step in the worked thesis Milestone checklist"},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Code ACM Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct ↗ Principles for computing professionals, including researchers. Guide COPE — Committee on Publication Ethics ↗ Guidance on authorship, misconduct and AI in publishing. Guide Purdue OWL: quoting, paraphrasing and summarising ↗ Examples of good paraphrasing. Report The Belmont Report ↗ Respect for persons, beneficence and justice in research with people. Integrity Retraction Watch ↗ Real cases of retracted papers and why. Guide Choose a licence ↗ What common code licences allow."},{"u":"topics/09-research-ethics-and-integrity.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 9 · Research Ethics and Integrity","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary Never fabricate, falsify or plagiarise; report all runs and every source. Paraphrase in your own words and cite; quote rarely, with quotation marks. Use AI tools only as your department allows; verify everything; declare how you used them. Research with people needs informed consent, privacy and often ethics approval. Check dataset, scraping and code licences; protect personal and medical data. Consider bias, misuse and responsible disclosure; agree authorship and acknowledge help. Next: Writing the Thesis More practice All resources Milestone checklist All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Writing the Thesis","x":"10 Chapter 10 of 24 · Part A · The research process Writing the Thesis Thesis structure and front matter, writing the introduction, methodology, results and discussion, the abstract, academic style, figures and tables, LaTeX, references and the writing and feedback process. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters Excellent experiments, explained in tangled sentences, read like a weak thesis. Examiners can only grade what they can understand — and they often read it late at night, tired. Writing is how you organise your thinking and show your evidence. A clear structure, a strong introduction and well-explained results can lift a thesis by a whole grade. This chapter covers structure, the abstract, academic style, figures, LaTeX, references and a writing process that finishes on time."},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 10 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. Structure The chapters Writing well LaTeX and references Process and feedback"},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"The structure of a thesis","x":"Section 1 of 5 The structure of a thesis"},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"The structure of a thesis (cont.)","x":"Section 1 of 5 The structure of a thesis (cont.) Chapter Answers 1 Introduction What problem, why it matters, what you asked, what you contribute 2 Literature review What is known, and the gap 3 Methodology How you answered the questions 4 Results and discussion What you found and what it means 5 Conclusion The answers, limitations, future work"},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Front and back matter","x":"Section 1 of 5 Front and back matter Before chapter 1 Title page (title, authors and IDs, supervisor, department, date) Declaration of originality, signed Approval page Acknowledgements Abstract Table of contents, lists of figures and tables, abbreviations After the last chapter References, in one consistent style Appendices: extra results, questionnaires, consent forms, datasheet, code listings Follow your department’s template for margins, fonts and page order; the milestones page has a LaTeX starting point."},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Writing the introduction","x":"Section 2 of 5 Writing the introduction Context the field and why it matters Problem what is difficult or missing — with evidence Gap what existing work does not do Research questions what exactly you ask Contributions what you deliver, as a bulleted list Outline one line per chapter Readers decide in the introduction whether your thesis is worth reading. The contributions list should be checkable: “We release a dataset of 5,000 labelled comments”, not “we study sentiment”."},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Methodology and results chapters","x":"Section 2 of 5 Methodology and results chapters Methodology data: source, size, cleaning, splits proposed method with a diagram baselines and why metrics and evaluation setup implementation details (in an appendix if long) Results and discussion main results table first answer each RQ in turn, with evidence ablation and error analysis compare with earlier work: agree or disagree, and why limitations and threats to validity Results say what happened; discussion says why and so what . Do not leave the interpretation to the reader."},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Writing the abstract","x":"Interactive Writing the abstract"},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Academic style","x":"Section 3 of 5 Academic style Prefer Avoid short, direct sentences long chains of clauses “We trained the model for 4 epochs.” “The model was trained by us for a duration of four epochs.” precise numbers: “improved macro-F1 by 3.1 points” “significantly better” without a test one term for one thing, defined once switching between “dataset”, “corpus” and “data collection” hedging where evidence is limited: “suggests”, “may” “proves”, “always”, “perfect” present tense for facts, past for what you did random tense changes Both “we” and the passive are accepted in computing; be consistent and follow your supervisor’s preference."},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Figures and tables","x":"Section 3 of 5 Figures and tables Every figure and table is numbered , has a caption , and is referred to in the text before it appears. The caption says what to notice: “Our model (blue) needs half the training data to reach the baseline’s F1.” Label axes with units; use readable font sizes; colour-blind-safe colours. Tables: best values in bold, mean ± sd, units in headers. Prefer vector graphics (PDF/SVG) for plots and diagrams. A reader skimming only the figures and captions should understand your main result."},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Try LaTeX for a thesis chapter","x":"Section 4 of 5 Try LaTeX for a thesis chapter"},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"References","x":"Section 4 of 5 References Cite every idea, method, dataset, figure and quotation that is not yours. Use one style (IEEE numeric is common in computing) through the whole thesis. Let the tools format it: Zotero → BibTeX → \\cite{key} → \\printbibliography . Check every entry: authors, title, venue, year, pages, DOI. Remove arXiv versions when a published version exists. IEEE style [1] S. Keshav, “How to read a paper,” ACM SIGCOMM Comput. Commun. Rev. , vol. 37, no. 3, pp. 83–84, 2007. [2] J. Devlin, M.-W. Chang, K. Lee, and K. Toutanova, “BERT: Pre-training of deep bidirectional transformers for language understanding,” in Proc. NAACL-HLT , 2019, pp. 4171–4186."},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"The writing process","x":"Section 5 of 5 The writing process Outline headings + one sentence each, agreed with the supervisor Draft write fast; do not polish yet Figures first the story is in the results Revise structure, then paragraphs, then sentences Feedback supervisor and a classmate Check references, numbering, similarity report Proofread on paper or read aloud Plan about three months for writing, with drafts sent chapter by chapter. Write the methodology and results first, the introduction and abstract last."},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Handling supervisor feedback","x":"Section 5 of 5 Handling supervisor feedback Read all comments before changing anything; group them by type. Fix the big ones (structure, missing analysis) before the small ones (wording). Reply in a short list: comment → what you changed (or why not). Do not argue in silence: if you disagree, explain with evidence. Send the next version with changes highlighted (latexdiff, or Word track changes)."},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 3 Worked example 1 Write a contributions list for a thesis on code-mixed Bangla sentiment analysis. Show the solution (1) A dataset of 5,000 Facebook comments in Banglish, labelled for three sentiment classes, with an annotation guide (κ = 0.78). (2) A comparison of six models, showing that continued pre-training on code-mixed text improves macro-F1 by 3.1 points. (3) An error analysis showing that sarcasm accounts for 31% of the remaining errors. (4) Code and data released for research use."},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 3 Worked example 2 Improve the caption “Figure 4.2: Results.” Show the solution “Figure 4.2: Macro-F1 against the number of training examples. Our model (blue) reaches the baseline’s best score (dashed line) with 40% of the training data.” — it now says what is shown and what to notice."},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Worked example 3","x":"Worked example 3 of 3 Worked example 3 Rewrite: “It was found by us that the accuracy of the proposed model was significantly better.” Show the solution “Our model improved accuracy from 84.2% to 86.9% (mean of five runs; p = 0.01, paired t-test).” — active, precise, and “significantly” is backed by a test."},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. List the five main chapters of a typical thesis. Show the answer Introduction; literature review; methodology; results and discussion; conclusion and future work. 2. What goes in the introduction? Show the answer Context, problem with evidence, the gap, research questions, contributions, and an outline of the chapters. 3. Give the five moves of an abstract. Show the answer Context, problem, approach, main results (with numbers), conclusion/implication."},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. What is the difference between results and discussion? Show the answer Results report what happened (numbers, tables, figures); discussion explains why, compares with earlier work, and states the implications and limitations. 5. Three rules for figures and tables. Show the answer Numbered and captioned; referred to in the text before they appear; readable labels with units (and best values marked in tables). 6. Which chapters should you write first, and which last? Show the answer Methodology and results first (they are what you did); introduction, conclusion and abstract last (they describe what you found)."},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"For your next supervisor meeting","x":"Bring this to your supervisor For your next supervisor meeting Bring this A full draft of one chapter (usually the methodology) and your abstract of 150–300 words. See an acceptable example Methodology draft of 9 pages: data collection, labelling guide and agreement (κ = 0.74), split, models, training details, evaluation and statistics. Abstract: context, problem, approach, results with numbers, conclusion — 212 words. See this step in the worked thesis Milestone checklist"},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=23","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Guide Overleaf: How to write a thesis in LaTeX ↗ Structure, front matter and chapters in LaTeX. Guide How to write a great research paper — Simon Peyton Jones ↗ Start with the idea; contributions up front. Book Writing for Computer Science — Justin Zobel Springer, 3rd ed. 2014. Style, structure, figures and editing. Book How to Write a Better Thesis — Evans, Gruba & Zobel Springer, 3rd ed. 2014. Chapter by chapter advice. Tool LaTeX thesis template A starting template with front matter and chapters. Tool Zotero + Better BibTeX ↗ Automatic .bib export with stable keys."},{"u":"topics/10-writing-the-thesis.html#s=24","w":"Chapter 10 · Writing the Thesis","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary A thesis follows introduction, literature review, methodology, results and discussion, conclusion — plus front and back matter. The introduction gives context, problem, gap, questions, contributions and an outline. Results say what happened; discussion says why and so what, including limitations. Write clearly and precisely; back every claim with numbers or tests. Number, caption and refer to every figure and table; use one reference style via BibTeX. Outline, draft, revise with feedback, check and proofread; write the abstract last. Next: Presenting, Defending and Publishing More practice All resources Milestone checklist All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Presenting, Defending and Publishing","x":"11 Chapter 11 of 24 · Part A · The research process Presenting, Defending and Publishing Proposal, progress and final presentations, slides that work, rehearsing, viva questions and how to answer them, posters, choosing a conference or journal, peer review and releasing code and data. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters One year of work. Fifteen minutes of slides. Then a professor leans forward and says, “Just one small question…” A defence is not a quiz on facts; it is a conversation in which you show that you understand your work, its strengths and its limits. Good slides and honest answers make it easy. This chapter prepares you for the presentation, the viva and turning your thesis into a published paper."},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 11 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. Presenting The defence Posters and publishing"},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Presentations during a thesis","x":"Section 1 of 3 Presentations during a thesis Presentation Goal Typical focus Proposal defence approval of the plan problem, questions, gap, methodology, timeline, risks Progress presentation show the work is on track data, first results, problems, the remaining plan Final defence judge the thesis contributions, results, analysis, limitations — then questions Poster session explain the work to visitors in minutes one clear message, big figures, little text"},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Structure of a defence talk","x":"Section 1 of 3 Structure of a defence talk Hook the problem in one slide, with an example Gap why existing work is not enough Questions your RQs and contributions Method one diagram Results the main table or plot, then the key insight Analysis ablation, errors, limitations Conclusion answers to the RQs; future work Timing for 15 minutes Problem and gap 3 · method 3 · results 5 · analysis and limitations 2 · conclusion 1 · buffer 1. About one slide per minute."},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Slides that work","x":"Section 1 of 3 Slides that work Do Avoid one message per slide, stated in the title titles like “Results” or “Methodology 2” large figures and diagrams paragraphs of text read aloud highlight the number that matters full tables with 40 numbers the same terms and colours as the thesis new names for the same thing slide numbers (examiners refer to them) animations that slow you down backup slides for expected questions saying “I did not have time”"},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Rehearsing","x":"Section 1 of 3 Rehearsing Rehearse aloud with a timer at least three times; cut until you finish with a minute to spare. Present to classmates who do not know your topic, and ask what was unclear. Record yourself once: watch for filler words and turning your back to the audience. Prepare the laptop, adapter, a PDF copy on a USB drive and in the cloud. Know your first two sentences by heart; the rest follows."},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"The viva: questions to expect","x":"Section 2 of 3 The viva: questions to expect"},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Answering questions","x":"Section 2 of 3 Answering questions Listen to the whole question; pause; thank the examiner if it helps you think. Repeat or rephrase it if unsure: “Do you mean…?” Answer directly first, then give the evidence (a backup slide helps). If you do not know, say so honestly, then say how you would find out. Agree with fair criticism and explain the limitation; do not become defensive. Examiners test whether you understand your work. An honest “we did not test that; I expect … because …” scores better than a confident guess."},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Posters","x":"Section 3 of 3 Posters A0 or A1, portrait or as required; readable from 2 metres. Title that states the finding, not only the topic. Sections: problem · method (diagram) · results (big figure) · conclusion · QR code to the paper or code. About 300–500 words in total. Prepare a 1-minute and a 3-minute explanation. Poster layout Three columns read left to right: why (problem, gap) → how (method) → what (results, conclusion). The key figure is the largest element."},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Publishing your thesis work","x":"Section 3 of 3 Publishing your thesis work Conference 8–10 page paper, peer-reviewed in 2–4 months presented in person or online the main venue type in ML, AI, CV, NLP deadlines fixed; plan backwards Journal longer article, several review rounds months to a year more weight in many fields and for promotions check quartile and that it is not predatory Good first venues: workshops at major conferences, regional IEEE conferences with proper peer review, and student research competitions. Your supervisor knows which are respected."},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Peer review","x":"Section 3 of 3 Peer review Desk reject : out of scope or badly formatted — check the call for papers. Reviews : usually 2–4 experts, often anonymous. Decision : accept, minor revision, major revision or reject. Rebuttal / response letter : answer every point politely, say what changed and where. A rejection with good reviews is useful: improve and submit elsewhere."},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Releasing code and data","x":"Section 3 of 3 Releasing code and data Clean the repository: README, licence, requirements, a script that reproduces the main table. Archive a version with a DOI (Zenodo) and cite it in the thesis. Release models on Hugging Face if allowed. Remove personal data and secrets (API keys!) before publishing. Post a preprint on arXiv if the venue allows it, to share the work early."},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 3 Worked example 1 An examiner asks: “Why did you not compare with GPT-4?” How do you answer? Show the solution Directly and honestly: “We focused on models that can run locally and be fine-tuned on our data, because the target users cannot send private messages to an external API. We did not evaluate GPT-4; based on [ref], we expect it to be strong on standard Bangla but it would need to be tested on code-mixed text. That is a good direction for future work.”"},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 3 Worked example 2 Turn the slide title “Results” into a message title. Show the solution “Continued pre-training improves macro-F1 by 3 points on code-mixed comments” — the audience knows what to look for before they read the table."},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Worked example 3","x":"Worked example 3 of 3 Worked example 3 A reviewer writes: “The baselines are weak; the comparison with recent transformer models is missing.” Draft your response. Show the solution “We thank the reviewer. We have added XLM-R and BanglaBERT as baselines, tuned with the same budget as our model (Table 3, Section 5.1). Our method still improves macro-F1 by 2.4 points over the strongest baseline (p = 0.02).”"},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Name the four kinds of thesis presentation. Show the answer Proposal defence, progress presentation, final defence and poster session. 2. How long should each part of a 15-minute defence take? Show the answer About: problem and gap 3 min, method 3, results 5, analysis and limitations 2, conclusion 1, buffer 1. 3. What do you do if you cannot answer a viva question? Show the answer Say honestly that you do not know or did not test it, explain your best reasoning, and say how you would find out."},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. What makes a good poster? Show the answer A finding in the title, one clear message, large figures, little text (300–500 words), a logical why–how–what flow, readable from 2 metres, a QR code. 5. Conference or journal: give two differences. Show the answer Conferences: shorter papers, fixed deadlines, faster review, presented live, dominant in ML/AI. Journals: longer, several review rounds, slower, more weight in many fields. 6. What should be removed before releasing code publicly? Show the answer Personal data, credentials and API keys, large private datasets you may not share; and add a README and licence."},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"For your next supervisor meeting","x":"Bring this to your supervisor For your next supervisor meeting Bring this A 10-slide outline of your defence talk and written answers to the five viva questions you fear most. See an acceptable example Slides: title; problem; gap; RQ; data; method; main results; ablation and errors; limitations; contributions. Feared questions: Why not GPT-4? Is +3 F1 meaningful? How did you prevent leakage? Why only Facebook? What would you do with six more months? See this step in the worked thesis Milestone checklist"},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Guide How to give a great research talk — Simon Peyton Jones ↗ One idea, examples first, and how to handle questions. Deadlines AI conference deadlines ↗ Countdowns to ML, CV, NLP and robotics deadlines. Calls WikiCFP ↗ Calls for papers across computing. Review OpenReview ↗ Read real reviews and author responses from ICLR and other venues. Preprints arXiv submission guide ↗ How to post a preprint. Archive Zenodo ↗ Archive code and data with a DOI."},{"u":"topics/11-presenting-defending-publishing.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 11 · Presenting, Defending and Publishing","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary Proposal, progress, final defence and poster each have a different goal and focus. Structure the talk: hook, gap, questions, method, results, analysis, conclusion — about one slide per minute. One message per slide, big figures, message titles; rehearse with a timer. In the viva, answer directly, use evidence, admit what you do not know, accept fair criticism. Publish at a suitable conference or journal; respond to reviews point by point. Release clean, documented code and data with a DOI; remove personal data and secrets. Next: Machine Learning More practice All resources Milestone checklist All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Machine Learning","x":"12 Chapter 12 of 24 · Part B · Research fields Machine Learning What ML research studies, typical problems (few labels, robustness, efficiency, fairness), key methods, datasets, evaluation, tools, venues, thesis ideas and how to get started. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters “I trained a model and got 95%.” Every ML examiner has heard this a thousand times, and the next question is always the same: “So what did you learn?” An ML thesis is a careful question about learning, with fair baselines, honest evaluation and an explanation of why it works. Almost every field now uses ML; few students learn to research it properly. This chapter maps the field, its methods, data and venues, with thesis ideas at three levels."},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 12 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. The field Methods Data and evaluation Tools and venues Thesis ideas"},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"What machine learning research studies","x":"Section 1 of 5 What machine learning research studies Machine learning (ML) studies algorithms that learn patterns from data and improve with experience, instead of following hand-written rules. Research asks how to learn more accurately , from less data , faster , more robustly and more fairly — and why methods work. Rest on a branch of the map for a description."},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Typical research problems","x":"Section 1 of 5 Typical research problems Problem Example thesis question Learning from little labelled data Does semi-supervised learning with 10% labels match full supervision on a local dataset? Generalisation and robustness How much does accuracy drop under distribution shift (new hospital, new season), and which method reduces it? Efficiency Can knowledge distillation make a model 5× smaller with less than 1 point loss? Imbalanced and noisy data Which loss or sampling strategy works best when the rare class is 2% of the data? Interpretability Do SHAP explanations agree with domain experts on which features matter? Fairness Does a credit model treat applicants from different regions equally, and can re-weighting fix it? Tabular and time-series prediction Do gradient-boosted trees beat deep models for crop-yield forecasting?"},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"The ML research pipeline","x":"Section 2 of 5 The ML research pipeline Problem task, inputs, outputs, metric Data collect, clean, split Baselines simple models first Model the proposed idea Tuning on validation data only Evaluation test once, several seeds Analysis ablation, errors, fairness Most ML theses fail at the edges of this pipeline — leaky splits, untuned baselines, one random seed — not in the model. Chapter 8 covers these in detail."},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Key methods to know","x":"Section 2 of 5 Key methods to know Classical models Linear and logistic regression, SVMs, decision trees, random forests, gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM) — still the best for many tabular problems. Neural networks MLPs, CNNs for grids (images), RNNs and transformers for sequences; trained with gradient descent and back-propagation. Unsupervised learning Clustering (k-means, DBSCAN), dimensionality reduction (PCA, t-SNE, UMAP), autoencoders. Self-supervised and transfer learning Pre-train on unlabelled data, then fine-tune — the default for images and text today."},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Key methods to know (cont.)","x":"Section 2 of 5 Key methods to know (cont.) Ensembles and regularisation Bagging, boosting, dropout, weight decay, early stopping: ways to reduce overfitting. Explainability Feature importance, SHAP, LIME, saliency maps — to understand and debug predictions."},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Popular models in machine learning","x":"Section 2 of 5 Popular models in machine learning Model What it is Typical use Logistic regression, SVM linear classifiers; SVMs with kernels first baselines; small or text data Random forest many decision trees voting robust tabular baseline, feature importance XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost gradient-boosted trees the strongest choice for most tabular data MLP a plain feed-forward neural network deep-learning baseline for tables TabPFN a transformer pretrained to classify small tables in one pass small tabular datasets (thousands of rows) Transformer attention-based sequence model text, time series, and the basis of most modern models k-means, DBSCAN, Isolation Forest clustering and anomaly detection unlabelled data, outliers Always include a gradient-boosting model when your data is a table: in many studies it still beats deep models."},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Overfitting, live","x":"Interactive Overfitting, live"},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Datasets","x":"Section 3 of 5 Datasets Source What you find UCI ML Repository hundreds of classic tabular datasets OpenML datasets with benchmark results from many algorithms Kaggle Datasets competition and community datasets (check the licence and quality) Hugging Face Datasets text, image, audio datasets with loaders Google Dataset Search search engine across repositories Government open data e.g. agriculture, weather, health statistics — good for local problems"},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Evaluation","x":"Section 3 of 5 Evaluation Task Metrics Classification accuracy, macro-F1, AUC-ROC, AUC-PR Regression MAE, RMSE, R² Clustering silhouette, adjusted Rand index Calibration expected calibration error Efficiency parameters, latency, memory Use k-fold cross-validation for small data. Report mean ± sd over seeds or folds. Compare with strong, tuned baselines — including gradient boosting for tabular data. Test on a second dataset or a later time period for robustness."},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Tools","x":"Section 4 of 5 Tools scikit-learn Classical ML, pipelines, cross-validation and metrics. PyTorch The main deep-learning framework in research. XGBoost / LightGBM Gradient boosting for tabular data. Weights & Biases / MLflow Tracking experiments. Google Colab / Kaggle Notebooks Free GPUs for small and medium experiments. SHAP Explaining model predictions."},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Where ML research is published","x":"Section 4 of 5 Where ML research is published Type Venues Top conferences NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR Broad AI conferences AAAI, IJCAI Applied ML and data KDD, ECML-PKDD, ICDM Journals JMLR, IEEE TPAMI, IEEE TNNLS, Machine Learning (Springer), Pattern Recognition Good first venues workshops at the big conferences; regional IEEE conferences Check the current rank of any venue on the CORE portal and SCImago before submitting."},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Thesis ideas","x":"Section 5 of 5 Thesis ideas Starter Benchmark ML models on a local tabular dataset Compare logistic regression, random forest, XGBoost and an MLP on, for example, crop yield or student performance data, with proper CV and SHAP analysis. Standard Learning with few labels Measure how semi-supervised or self-training methods perform as labelled data shrinks from 100% to 5%. Standard Robustness to distribution shift Train on one year or region, test on another; evaluate domain-adaptation methods. Standard Fairness audit Measure group fairness metrics of a public model and test a mitigation method. Ambitious Efficient models for edge devices Distil or quantise a model to run on a phone; measure accuracy, latency and energy."},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Getting started in four weeks","x":"Section 5 of 5 Getting started in four weeks Week 1 refresh the basics: linear models, overfitting, cross-validation; reproduce one tutorial Week 2 pick a dataset; build a scikit-learn baseline end to end Week 3 read 5 recent papers on your problem; note their baselines and metrics Week 4 reproduce one paper’s main number; write a one-page idea Pitfalls: tuning on the test set, accuracy on imbalanced data, one random seed, comparing with untuned baselines, ignoring simple models."},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 2 Worked example 1 A student’s random forest gets 99% accuracy predicting loan default; 3% of loans default. What do you check? Show the solution Class imbalance (a model predicting “no default” always scores 97%), leakage (features recorded after the default, such as “collection calls”), the split (same customer in train and test). Report recall, precision and AUC-PR for the default class."},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 2 Worked example 2 Turn “use deep learning for crop yield prediction” into a research question. Show the solution “Do LSTM models using weekly weather data predict district-level rice yield more accurately (lower MAE) than gradient-boosted trees using seasonal aggregates, when tested on a held-out recent year?”"},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Name four typical ML research problems. Show the answer Learning from few labels, robustness to distribution shift, efficiency, imbalanced/noisy data, interpretability, fairness. 2. What is overfitting and how do you detect it? Show the answer The model fits the training data (including noise) but generalises poorly; training error is low while validation error is high or rising. 3. Which model family should always be a baseline for tabular data? Show the answer Gradient-boosted trees (XGBoost, LightGBM) — and a simple linear model."},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. Why use k-fold cross-validation? Show the answer With small data, it uses every example for testing once, giving a more reliable estimate with a variance. 5. Name three top ML conferences. Show the answer NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR. 6. What is transfer learning? Show the answer Reusing a model pre-trained on a large dataset and fine-tuning it on the target task."},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Course Stanford CS229: Machine Learning ↗ Lecture notes covering the core theory. Book Dive into Deep Learning ↗ Free interactive deep-learning book with code. Course fast.ai Practical Deep Learning ↗ Top-down, hands-on deep learning. Docs scikit-learn user guide ↗ Models, pipelines, evaluation. Data OpenML ↗ Datasets with benchmark results. Code Hugging Face Papers ↗ Trending ML papers with code. Book Pattern Recognition and Machine Learning — C. Bishop Springer, 2006. The classic probabilistic view of ML."},{"u":"topics/12-machine-learning.html#s=23","w":"Chapter 12 · Machine Learning","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary ML research asks how to learn more accurately, from less data, robustly, efficiently and fairly. Know classical models, neural networks, unsupervised, transfer and self-supervised learning. Use public repositories (UCI, OpenML, Hugging Face, Kaggle) or local data; describe and split it carefully. Evaluate with the right metrics, cross-validation, several seeds and strong baselines. Top venues: NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR (plus AAAI, IJCAI, KDD); start with workshops and regional conferences. Start by reproducing one paper’s main result. Next: Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI More practice All resources All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","x":"13 Chapter 13 of 24 · Part B · Research fields Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI Search, planning, reasoning, reinforcement learning, LLMs, retrieval-augmented generation and agents; evaluating AI systems reliably; tools, venues, thesis ideas and how to start. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters “My thesis is a ChatGPT chatbot.” “Lovely. What is the research question?” “…It chats.” Generative AI made it easy to build impressive demos that answer no question. Good AI research measures reliability, compares methods fairly and explains the failures. This chapter covers classical AI (search, planning, reasoning) and modern generative AI, with evaluation habits that make results credible."},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 13 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. The field Methods Data and evaluation Tools and venues Thesis ideas"},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"What AI research studies","x":"Section 1 of 5 What AI research studies Artificial intelligence builds systems that perceive, reason, plan, learn and act. Machine learning is one part; AI also covers search and planning , knowledge representation and reasoning , agents , and today generative AI — large language models (LLMs) and image generators — with their evaluation and safety. Rest on a branch of the map for a description."},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Typical research problems","x":"Section 1 of 5 Typical research problems Problem Example thesis question Reliability of LLMs How often does an LLM give wrong answers (hallucinate) on Bangla university-regulation questions, and does retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) reduce it? LLM evaluation Do automatic metrics agree with human judgement for summaries of Bangla news? Agents and tool use Can an LLM agent with a calculator and a database answer multi-step questions more accurately than the LLM alone? Search and planning Which heuristic gives the fastest optimal routes for delivery robots on a campus map? Knowledge graphs Does adding a medical knowledge graph improve question answering about drug interactions? Explainable and trustworthy AI Do users trust a recommendation more with a counterfactual explanation, and is the trust justified? Small, efficient models How well does a 1–3B-parameter open model, fine-tuned with LoRA, perform compared with a large API model on a narrow task?"},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Generative AI research, carefully","x":"Section 2 of 5 Generative AI research, carefully Prefer open models (e.g. Llama, Qwen, Mistral, Gemma families) you can run and fine-tune: results are reproducible. If you use an API model, record the exact model version and date ; outputs change over time. Fix prompts, decoding settings (temperature) and run several times. Build a test set with reference answers, written before you look at the outputs. Combine automatic scores with human evaluation using a rubric and two raters. A strong LLM thesis is a careful measurement or a clear improvement on a well-defined task — not a demo of a chatbot."},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Key methods to know","x":"Section 2 of 5 Key methods to know Search BFS, DFS, uniform-cost, A* with heuristics; local search; adversarial search (minimax). Planning and reasoning STRIPS/PDDL planners, constraint satisfaction, logic and probabilistic reasoning, Bayesian networks. Reinforcement learning Agents learning from rewards: Q-learning, policy gradients; also used to align LLMs (RLHF). Transformers and LLMs Pre-training, instruction tuning, prompting, in-context learning. Adapting LLMs Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), fine-tuning with LoRA/QLoRA, tool use and agents. Trustworthy AI Explainability, uncertainty, robustness, fairness and safety evaluation."},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Popular models in AI and generative AI","x":"Section 2 of 5 Popular models in AI and generative AI Model family What it is Typical use GPT, Claude, Gemini large commercial LLMs, used through an API strong reference systems; record the exact version and date Llama, Qwen, Mistral, Gemma, DeepSeek open-weight LLM families in many sizes run locally, fine-tune with LoRA, reproducible research Reasoning models (e.g. DeepSeek-R1, OpenAI o-series) LLMs trained to think step by step before answering maths, code and multi-step problems Embedding models (e.g. BGE, E5, sentence-transformers) turn text into vectors the retrieval step of RAG, semantic search DQN, PPO reinforcement-learning algorithms agents in games and simulations; PPO is also used to align LLMs AlphaZero, MuZero search + learned value networks planning in games; a classic example of search plus learning A*, MCTS classical search algorithms path-finding, planning, game play Model versions change every few months: name the family and the exact version you used, and prefer open models for reproducible results."},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Search in action: BFS versus A*","x":"Interactive Search in action: BFS versus A*"},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Data and benchmarks","x":"Section 3 of 5 Data and benchmarks Kind Examples Reasoning and knowledge MMLU, GSM8K (maths word problems), ARC, HellaSwag Question answering SQuAD, Natural Questions; build your own for local documents Agents WebArena, SWE-bench, tool-use benchmarks Planning International Planning Competition domains (PDDL) Reinforcement learning Gymnasium (formerly OpenAI Gym) environments Bangla / multilingual translated or native benchmarks — often missing: a gap you can fill Beware contamination : popular benchmarks may be in a model’s training data. A new, private test set is often more convincing."},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Evaluating AI systems","x":"Section 3 of 5 Evaluating AI systems What How Correctness exact match, F1 against reference answers Faithfulness of RAG is every claim supported by the retrieved text? Generation quality human rating with a rubric; LLM-as-judge only with validation Agents task success rate, steps, cost Search / planning solution cost, nodes expanded, time Safety refusals, harmful outputs, jailbreak success rate Report variance across runs and prompts. Measure inter-rater agreement for human evaluation. Report cost and latency — they matter in practice."},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Tools","x":"Section 4 of 5 Tools Hugging Face Transformers Load, run and fine-tune open models. PEFT / LoRA Fine-tune large models on a single GPU. Ollama / llama.cpp Run open models locally. Vector databases FAISS, Chroma — the retrieval part of RAG. Gymnasium Reinforcement-learning environments. Evaluation harnesses lm-evaluation-harness, Stanford HELM."},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Where AI research is published","x":"Section 4 of 5 Where AI research is published Type Venues Broad AI AAAI, IJCAI, ECAI ML and LLMs NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR Language models ACL, EMNLP, NAACL (see chapter 14) Planning and agents ICAPS, AAMAS Journals Artificial Intelligence (Elsevier), JAIR, IEEE TPAMI, ACM Computing Surveys Good first venues conference workshops; regional IEEE conferences"},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Thesis ideas","x":"Section 5 of 5 Thesis ideas Starter Hallucination audit Build a 300-question test set on a local topic and measure how often several open LLMs answer wrongly or invent facts. Standard RAG for university documents Retrieval-augmented question answering over course rules or policies; compare retrievers and measure faithfulness. Standard LoRA fine-tuning a small model Fine-tune a small open model for a narrow task (e.g. Bangla FAQ) and compare with a large model by accuracy and cost. Starter Heuristic search for real maps Compare A* heuristics for routing on an OpenStreetMap graph of Dhaka. Ambitious Tool-using agents An agent that uses a calculator, a database and search; measure task success and failure modes."},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Getting started in four weeks","x":"Section 5 of 5 Getting started in four weeks Week 1 AIMA chapters on search and agents; a Hugging Face tutorial Week 2 run an open model locally; try prompting on your task Week 3 build a small test set with reference answers Week 4 measure two models on it; write a one-page idea Pitfalls: evaluating on examples you looked at while designing prompts, unrecorded model versions, one run per prompt, trusting LLM-as-judge without checking it against humans."},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 2 Worked example 1 Turn “a chatbot for our department” into a research question. Show the solution “Does retrieval-augmented generation over the department’s official documents reduce unsupported answers of an open 8B-parameter LLM, compared with the LLM alone, on 300 student questions with reference answers?” — task, method, comparison and metric are now defined."},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 2 Worked example 2 Your LLM scores 92% on a public benchmark released two years ago. Why be careful? Show the solution The benchmark may be in the model’s training data (contamination), so the score may reflect memorisation. Test on a new, private set written after the model’s training cut-off, or check for overlap."},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Name four areas of AI that are not machine learning. Show the answer Classical search (BFS, A*); automated planning; knowledge representation and logical reasoning; constraint satisfaction; multi-agent systems and game theory. (Reinforcement learning and generative AI are machine learning, so they do not count here.) 2. Why prefer open models for a thesis? Show the answer They can be run and fine-tuned locally, versions are fixed, results are reproducible and data stays private. 3. What is RAG? Show the answer Retrieval-augmented generation: retrieve relevant documents for a question and give them to the LLM so it answers from them."},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. Why does A* expand fewer nodes than BFS? Show the answer It uses a heuristic estimate of the remaining cost to explore promising nodes first; with an admissible heuristic it still finds an optimal path. 5. What is benchmark contamination? Show the answer When test data appeared in the model’s training data, inflating its score. 6. How do you make human evaluation of generated text reliable? Show the answer A written rubric, at least two raters, blind to the system, and a measure of agreement between them."},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Book Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach — Russell & Norvig ↗ The standard AI textbook: search, reasoning, planning, learning. Course Hugging Face courses ↗ LLMs, agents and fine-tuning, free and hands-on. Paper Attention Is All You Need — Vaswani et al. ↗ The transformer paper (NeurIPS 2017). Benchmark Stanford HELM ↗ Holistic evaluation of language models. Report AI Index — Stanford HAI ↗ Yearly data on AI research and trends. Tool Gymnasium ↗ Reinforcement-learning environments."},{"u":"topics/13-ai-and-generative-ai.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 13 · Artificial Intelligence and Generative AI","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary AI includes search, planning, reasoning, agents, reinforcement learning and generative models — not only ML. Generative-AI theses need fixed model versions, private test sets, several runs and human evaluation. Know RAG, LoRA fine-tuning, tool-using agents and classical search such as A*. Watch for benchmark contamination and unvalidated LLM-as-judge scores. Venues: AAAI, IJCAI, NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR, ACL/EMNLP, ICAPS, AAMAS. A good AI thesis measures or improves something well defined — not a demo. Next: Natural Language Processing More practice All resources All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Natural Language Processing","x":"14 Chapter 14 of 24 · Part B · Research fields Natural Language Processing Text classification, NER, translation, QA, summarisation and speech; Bangla as a low-resource language; methods from TF-IDF to transformers and LLMs; datasets, metrics, venues and thesis ideas. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters Type “ami bhat khabo” into a translator and watch it panic. About 300 million people speak Bangla, yet the tools still stumble over Banglish. That gap is your opportunity. A careful Bangla dataset, a fair comparison of models on real Bangla text or a method for code-mixed Banglish is a real contribution — one the big labs are unlikely to make. This chapter maps NLP research, its methods, data and venues, with a focus on Bangla."},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 14 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. The field Methods Data and evaluation Tools and venues Thesis ideas"},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"What NLP research studies","x":"Section 1 of 5 What NLP research studies Natural language processing (NLP) builds methods that let computers understand and generate human language: classify texts, extract information, translate, answer questions, summarise and converse. For a Bangladeshi student, Bangla is a low-resource language — fewer datasets and tools than English — so careful data work in Bangla is itself a valuable contribution. Rest on a branch of the map for a description."},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Typical research problems","x":"Section 1 of 5 Typical research problems Task Example thesis question Text classification Can a model detect hate speech in Bangla YouTube comments, and which categories does it confuse? Named entity recognition Does a transformer recognise Bangla person and place names written in romanised Banglish? Machine translation How good is open MT for Bangla–English legal text, and does domain fine-tuning help? Question answering and RAG Can a QA system answer questions from Bangla textbooks? Summarisation Do summaries of Bangla news by small open models stay faithful to the article? Speech How accurate is open speech recognition on Bangla regional dialects? Low-resource methods Does cross-lingual transfer from Hindi help Bangla NER with 1,000 labelled sentences?"},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"The NLP pipeline","x":"Section 2 of 5 The NLP pipeline Collect texts with permission; remove personal data Normalise Unicode, punctuation, spelling variants Annotate guidelines, two annotators, κ Tokenise words or subwords Model baseline → pretrained transformer Evaluate macro-F1, BLEU/chrF, human checks Analyse errors by phenomenon Bangla needs attention at the first steps: Unicode normalisation (different code-point sequences for the same character), conjuncts, mixed scripts and romanised text."},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Key methods to know","x":"Section 2 of 5 Key methods to know Classical text features Bag of words, n-grams, TF-IDF with logistic regression or SVM — a fast, strong baseline. Word embeddings word2vec, fastText (good for Bangla morphology through subword units). Pretrained transformers BERT-style encoders (BanglaBERT, XLM-R, mBERT) for understanding; sequence-to-sequence models (mT5, BanglaT5) for generation. Large language models Prompting, few-shot learning, fine-tuning with LoRA, RAG."},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Key methods to know (cont.)","x":"Section 2 of 5 Key methods to know (cont.) Low-resource techniques Transfer from related languages, data augmentation, back-translation, active learning. Evaluation and analysis Error analysis by phenomenon, probing, robustness to spelling variation."},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Popular models in NLP","x":"Section 2 of 5 Popular models in NLP Model What it is Typical use BERT, RoBERTa pretrained transformer encoders classification, NER, question answering XLM-R, mBERT multilingual encoders covering Bangla cross-lingual transfer, Bangla baselines BanglaBERT encoder pretrained on Bangla text (BUET) strong baseline for Bangla understanding tasks mT5, BanglaT5 sequence-to-sequence transformers summarisation, translation, generation NLLB-200 Meta’s translation model for 200 languages Bangla ↔ English translation baselines Whisper, wav2vec 2.0 speech-recognition models Bangla and dialect speech-to-text Sentence-transformers sentence embedding models semantic similarity, clustering, retrieval Open LLMs (Llama, Qwen…) decoder-only generative models zero-shot baselines, instruction tuning, RAG A typical Bangla NLP thesis compares a TF-IDF baseline, a multilingual model (XLM-R) and a Bangla-specific one (BanglaBERT) before trying anything new."},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"TF-IDF: which words matter?","x":"Interactive TF-IDF: which words matter?"},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Datasets and resources","x":"Section 3 of 5 Datasets and resources Resource What it offers Hugging Face Datasets thousands of NLP datasets; filter by language “bn” BanglaBERT (CSE BUET NLP) a Bangla pretrained model and benchmark tasks ACL Anthology every ACL paper — and the datasets they introduce Universal Dependencies treebanks for part-of-speech and parsing in many languages OPUS parallel corpora for machine translation Your own collection news, social media or forms — with permission and anonymisation"},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Evaluation","x":"Section 3 of 5 Evaluation Task Metrics Classification macro-F1, per-class F1 NER entity-level F1 (seqeval) Translation chrF, BLEU, COMET + human Summarisation ROUGE, BERTScore + faithfulness check QA exact match, F1 Speech recognition word / character error rate Report inter-annotator agreement for any dataset you label. chrF suits Bangla better than BLEU because of rich morphology. Always add a small human evaluation for generation tasks."},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Tools","x":"Section 4 of 5 Tools Hugging Face Transformers Pretrained models, tokenisers, training scripts. spaCy / Stanza Pipelines for tokenisation, tagging and parsing. fastText Fast embeddings and classifiers. Label Studio / Doccano Free annotation tools for labelling text. bnlp / bangla tokenisers Bangla-specific normalisation and tokenisation. sacreBLEU, evaluate Standard, comparable metric implementations."},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Where NLP research is published","x":"Section 4 of 5 Where NLP research is published Type Venues Top conferences ACL, EMNLP, NAACL, EACL, COLING Regional / low-resource AACL-IJCNLP, workshops on low-resource and South Asian languages (e.g. BLP) Speech Interspeech, ICASSP Journals Computational Linguistics, TACL, ACM TALLIP Archive all ACL papers are free in the ACL Anthology"},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Thesis ideas","x":"Section 5 of 5 Thesis ideas Starter A new labelled Bangla dataset Collect and label 3,000–5,000 texts for an under-served task (e.g. cyberbullying, fake product reviews) with guidelines and agreement, plus baselines. Standard Banglish (code-mixed) understanding Measure how pretrained models handle romanised Bangla and test transliteration or continued pre-training. Standard Faithful summarisation Evaluate open models for Bangla news summarisation with a human faithfulness check. Standard Domain question answering RAG over Bangla textbooks or legal documents; compare retrievers. Ambitious Dialect speech recognition Collect regional-dialect speech, fine-tune an open ASR model and analyse errors."},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Getting started in four weeks","x":"Section 5 of 5 Getting started in four weeks Week 1 Jurafsky & Martin chapters on classification and transformers Week 2 fine-tune BanglaBERT or XLM-R on a public Bangla task Week 3 read 5 recent papers in the ACL Anthology on your task Week 4 label a pilot of 200 examples; measure agreement; write a one-page idea Pitfalls: no Unicode normalisation, duplicates across splits, accuracy on imbalanced classes, no annotation guide, personal data left in social-media text."},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 2 Worked example 1 You label 4,000 comments for hate speech with one annotator. Is that enough? Show the solution Not for a credible dataset. Write annotation guidelines, have a second annotator label a sample (e.g. 500), compute Cohen’s κ, resolve disagreements and refine the guide. Report κ and the class distribution."},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 2 Worked example 2 Your Bangla classifier is 91% accurate, but 85% of comments are “not hate”. What do you report? Show the solution Macro-F1 and per-class precision/recall, especially for the hate class, plus the confusion matrix. Accuracy alone hides poor recall on the minority class."},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Why is Bangla called low-resource? Show the answer It has far fewer labelled datasets, pretrained models and tools than English, relative to its number of speakers. 2. Name two Bangla-specific pre-processing issues. Show the answer Unicode normalisation of equivalent character sequences; conjuncts; mixed Bangla/English script; romanised (Banglish) text; spelling variation. 3. Which metric suits Bangla translation better than BLEU, and why? Show the answer chrF — character n-grams handle rich morphology better than word n-grams."},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. What is TF-IDF? Show the answer Term frequency × inverse document frequency: a word scores high if it is frequent in a document but rare across documents. 5. Name three NLP venues. Show the answer ACL, EMNLP, NAACL (also EACL, COLING, TACL). 6. What makes a new dataset credible? Show the answer Clear source and permission, annotation guidelines, two annotators with agreement, documented class balance, fixed splits, baselines, a datasheet."},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Book Speech and Language Processing — Jurafsky & Martin ↗ The standard NLP textbook, free online draft. Course Stanford CS224N: NLP with Deep Learning ↗ Lectures and assignments on modern NLP. Archive ACL Anthology ↗ All papers from ACL venues, free. Model BanglaBERT ↗ Bangla pretrained model and benchmarks from BUET. Course Hugging Face NLP course ↗ Transformers in practice. Paper BERT — Devlin et al. ↗ Pretraining bidirectional transformers (NAACL 2019)."},{"u":"topics/14-natural-language-processing.html#s=23","w":"Chapter 14 · Natural Language Processing","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary NLP covers classification, extraction, translation, QA, summarisation, dialogue and speech. Bangla is low-resource: careful datasets and evaluations are real contributions. Normalise Unicode, handle code-mixing, write annotation guidelines and measure agreement. Methods range from TF-IDF baselines to pretrained transformers and LLMs. Use task-appropriate metrics (macro-F1, entity F1, chrF, ROUGE, WER) plus human evaluation. Venues: ACL, EMNLP, NAACL, COLING; read them free in the ACL Anthology. Next: Computer Vision and Image Processing More practice All resources All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Computer Vision and Image Processing","x":"15 Chapter 15 of 24 · Part B · Research fields Computer Vision and Image Processing Image processing and computer vision tasks — classification, detection, segmentation, OCR, restoration, video — with CNNs and vision transformers, datasets, metrics, tools, venues and thesis ideas. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters A model scores 99% on plant-disease photos taken in a lab. On a farmer’s cracked phone, under the afternoon sun in the field, it scores 60%. That gap between benchmark images and the real world is where much useful vision research for local problems lies. Closing it, or even measuring it well, makes a strong thesis. This chapter introduces image processing and computer vision: tasks, methods, data, evaluation and ideas."},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 15 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. The field Methods Data and evaluation Tools and venues Thesis ideas"},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"What vision and image-processing research studies","x":"Section 1 of 5 What vision and image-processing research studies Image processing transforms images: denoising, enhancement, compression, edge detection. Computer vision extracts meaning: what is in the image, where, and what is happening in a video. Applications range from agriculture and medicine to traffic, document reading and robotics. Rest on a branch of the map for a description."},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Typical research problems","x":"Section 1 of 5 Typical research problems Task Example thesis question Classification Can a lightweight CNN identify five rice-leaf diseases from phone photos taken in the field? Object detection How accurately does YOLO detect rickshaws and CNG auto-rickshaws in Dhaka traffic video at night? Segmentation Does U-Net segment flood water in satellite images better than thresholding? OCR and documents How well does open OCR read handwritten Bangla forms, and does synthetic data help? Image restoration Can a model enhance low-light images from cheap CCTV cameras for later detection? Video understanding Can action recognition detect falls of elderly people from home cameras? Robustness and efficiency How much does accuracy drop with blur and rain, and can augmentation recover it?"},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"The vision pipeline","x":"Section 2 of 5 The vision pipeline Images collect, check consent and licences Annotate labels, boxes or masks Pre-process resize, normalise, augment Model pretrained backbone + task head Train transfer learning, then fine-tune Evaluate per-class metrics, held-out site Analyse failure images, Grad-CAM Split by source (camera, field, patient, video) — not by individual frame — or near-identical images will leak into the test set."},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Key methods to know","x":"Section 2 of 5 Key methods to know Classic image processing Filtering and convolution, histograms, edge detection (Sobel, Canny), morphology, thresholding. CNNs ResNet, EfficientNet, MobileNet: convolutional feature extractors. Vision transformers ViT, Swin; self-supervised models like DINO; image–text models like CLIP. Detection Two-stage (Faster R-CNN) and one-stage (YOLO family, DETR). Segmentation U-Net, Mask R-CNN, and promptable “segment anything” models. Generation GANs and diffusion models — for synthesis and data augmentation."},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Popular models in computer vision","x":"Section 2 of 5 Popular models in computer vision Model What it is Typical use ResNet, EfficientNet, ConvNeXt convolutional backbones image classification, transfer learning MobileNet lightweight CNN phones and edge devices ViT, Swin Transformer vision transformers classification and backbones when data is large or pretrained YOLO (Ultralytics YOLOv8, YOLO11) fast one-stage object detector real-time detection: traffic, crops, safety Faster R-CNN, DETR / RT-DETR two-stage and transformer detectors accurate detection; strong baselines for YOLO U-Net, DeepLabv3+, Mask R-CNN segmentation networks pixel masks in medical, satellite and street images SAM (Segment Anything) promptable segmentation foundation model fast labelling, zero-shot segmentation CLIP, DINOv2 image–text and self-supervised models zero-shot classification, strong features Stable Diffusion diffusion image generator synthetic training data, image editing Tesseract, TrOCR, PaddleOCR OCR engines and models reading printed and handwritten text"},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Popular models in computer vision (cont.)","x":"Section 2 of 5 Popular models in computer vision (cont.) For detection, compare YOLO with at least one other family (Faster R-CNN or RT-DETR) and report both accuracy (mAP) and speed (FPS)."},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Convolution: the core operation","x":"Interactive Convolution: the core operation"},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Datasets","x":"Section 3 of 5 Datasets Dataset Task ImageNet classification; the source of most pretrained backbones COCO detection, segmentation, captions PlantVillage, PlantDoc plant-disease classification (lab and field photos) Cityscapes urban street segmentation Roboflow Universe thousands of community detection datasets (check quality and licence) Your own images local problems: crops, traffic, documents — with consent where people appear"},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Evaluation","x":"Section 3 of 5 Evaluation Task Metrics Classification accuracy, macro-F1, top-k Detection mAP@0.5, mAP@0.5:0.95 Segmentation IoU, Dice Restoration PSNR, SSIM + human rating Efficiency FPS, latency, model size Test on images from a different source than training (another field, camera or hospital). Show qualitative examples: successes and failures. Use Grad-CAM or similar to check the model looks at the right region."},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Tools","x":"Section 4 of 5 Tools OpenCV Classic image processing and video I/O. PyTorch + torchvision / timm Backbones and training. Ultralytics YOLO Fast object detection training. Albumentations Image augmentation. CVAT / Label Studio Free annotation for boxes and masks. Grad-CAM Visual explanations of CNN predictions."},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Where vision research is published","x":"Section 4 of 5 Where vision research is published Type Venues Top conferences CVPR, ICCV, ECCV Other conferences WACV, BMVC, ICIP, ICPR Medical imaging MICCAI, ISBI (see chapter 17) Journals IEEE TPAMI, IJCV, IEEE TIP, Pattern Recognition, Computer Vision and Image Understanding Free papers CVF Open Access hosts CVPR/ICCV papers"},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Thesis ideas","x":"Section 5 of 5 Thesis ideas Starter Lab-to-field gap in plant disease detection Train on PlantVillage, test on your own field photos; measure the drop and test augmentation. Standard Local vehicle detection Annotate local vehicle types (rickshaw, CNG, leguna) and compare YOLO versions by mAP and speed. Standard Handwritten Bangla OCR Evaluate open OCR on handwritten forms; add synthetic training data. Standard Low-light enhancement for detection Does enhancing night images improve downstream detection? Ambitious Efficient on-device vision Quantise and deploy a detector on a phone; measure accuracy, FPS and battery."},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Getting started in four weeks","x":"Section 5 of 5 Getting started in four weeks Week 1 CS231n notes on CNNs; OpenCV basics Week 2 fine-tune a pretrained ResNet on a small dataset Week 3 read 5 recent papers on your task (CVF Open Access) Week 4 collect and label 200 pilot images; one-page idea Pitfalls: frames of one video in both train and test, near-duplicate images, reporting only accuracy, no test on a new source, people’s faces without consent."},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 2 Worked example 1 You have 40 videos of traffic and extract 20,000 frames for detection. How do you split? Show the solution By video: e.g. 28 videos for training, 6 for validation, 6 for testing. Random frame splits put near-identical frames from the same video in train and test, inflating mAP."},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 2 Worked example 2 A disease classifier is accurate but Grad-CAM shows it focuses on the background. What does that mean? Show the solution It may be using a shortcut (e.g. lab background or lighting that correlates with the label). Test on images with different backgrounds, crop or mask backgrounds, and add varied field images."},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Distinguish image processing from computer vision. Show the answer Image processing transforms images (enhance, denoise, detect edges); computer vision extracts meaning (what and where objects are, what happens). 2. Name the metric for detection and for segmentation. Show the answer Detection: mAP (e.g. mAP@0.5:0.95). Segmentation: IoU or Dice. 3. What does a 3×3 convolution kernel do? Show the answer Computes each output pixel as a weighted sum of the 3×3 neighbourhood; different weights blur, sharpen or detect edges."},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. Why use transfer learning in vision? Show the answer Pretrained backbones already detect general features, so small datasets reach good accuracy with less data and training. 5. Name three top vision conferences. Show the answer CVPR, ICCV, ECCV. 6. How do you test robustness of a vision model? Show the answer Evaluate on images from a different source, and with controlled corruptions (blur, noise, low light, rain)."},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Course Stanford CS231n: Deep Learning for Computer Vision ↗ Notes on CNNs, training and vision tasks. Book Computer Vision: Algorithms and Applications — R. Szeliski ↗ Free online textbook, 2nd edition. Tool OpenCV ↗ Classic image processing and video. Data COCO ↗ Detection and segmentation benchmark. Paper Deep Residual Learning (ResNet) — He et al. ↗ CVPR 2016; the backbone behind many models. Paper U-Net — Ronneberger et al. ↗ MICCAI 2015; segmentation with little data."},{"u":"topics/15-computer-vision-and-image-processing.html#s=23","w":"Chapter 15 · Computer Vision and Image Processing","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary Image processing transforms images; computer vision extracts meaning from them. Tasks: classification, detection, segmentation, OCR, restoration, video understanding. Methods: classic filters and convolution, CNNs, vision transformers, YOLO/DETR detectors, U-Net, diffusion models. Split by source, test on a new source, report mAP/IoU/Dice and show failures. Venues: CVPR, ICCV, ECCV (free on CVF Open Access), plus WACV, BMVC, ICIP. The lab-to-field gap is a rich source of thesis questions. Next: Cybersecurity More practice All resources All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Cybersecurity","x":"16 Chapter 16 of 24 · Part B · Research fields Cybersecurity Intrusion and malware detection, phishing, web and software security, cryptography, privacy, usable security and adversarial ML; threat models, legal and ethical limits, data, metrics, venues and thesis ideas. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters An intrusion detector scores 99.9% on a public dataset. On a real network it raises 5,000 false alarms a day, and the admin switches it off by Friday. Security research is adversarial and practical: realistic threat models, recent data, few false alarms and robustness to attackers who adapt. It also has strict legal and ethical limits. This chapter maps the field and shows how to do security research that is credible and responsible."},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 16 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. The field Methods Data and evaluation Tools and venues Thesis ideas"},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"What security research studies","x":"Section 1 of 5 What security research studies Cybersecurity research studies how systems are attacked and how to defend them: finding vulnerabilities, detecting intrusions and malware, designing secure protocols and cryptography, protecting privacy, and understanding the human side of security. It is adversarial : a defence is only as good as the strongest realistic attack against it. Rest on a branch of the map for a description."},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Typical research problems","x":"Section 1 of 5 Typical research problems Area Example thesis question Intrusion detection Does a model trained on one network’s traffic detect attacks on another network, and what reduces false alarms? Malware analysis Can static features of Android apps detect malware families not seen during training? Phishing and fraud Do URL and page features detect Bangla-language phishing sites better than global blocklists? Web and software security How common are OWASP Top 10 vulnerabilities in local government websites (with permission)? Cryptography in practice What is the performance cost of post-quantum key exchange on low-power IoT devices? Privacy Which personal data do popular Bangladeshi apps send to third parties? Usable security Why do users ignore SMS fraud warnings, and which wording works better? Adversarial ML How easily can small changes fool a malware or spam classifier?"},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Thinking like a security researcher","x":"Section 2 of 5 Thinking like a security researcher Start with a threat model : who is the attacker, what can they do, what do they want, what is trusted? Evaluate against adaptive attackers , not only past attacks. Measure false positives : a detector that floods analysts with alarms is useless. Test on realistic, recent data; old benchmarks have known flaws. Work only on systems you own or have written permission to test. Unauthorised scanning or exploitation is illegal, even for research. Use labs, virtual machines and capture-the-flag environments."},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Key methods to know","x":"Section 2 of 5 Key methods to know Cryptography Hashes, symmetric and public-key encryption, signatures, TLS; post-quantum algorithms. Network monitoring Packet capture, flow features, signature IDS (Snort, Suricata, Zeek) and anomaly detection. Static and dynamic analysis Inspect code or binaries without running them; run in a sandbox and observe behaviour. Fuzzing Feed random or mutated inputs to find crashes and bugs automatically. ML for security Classifiers for malware, phishing and intrusion — plus their robustness to evasion. Measurement studies Scanning and crawling (with permission) to measure how common a weakness is."},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Popular models and algorithms in security","x":"Section 2 of 5 Popular models and algorithms in security Model / algorithm What it is Typical use Random forest, XGBoost on flow features classic ML on network-flow statistics intrusion-detection baselines (e.g. CIC-IDS datasets) Autoencoders, Isolation Forest anomaly detectors detect unseen attacks without labels LSTM / CNN on traffic sequences deep sequence models attack and botnet detection over time MalConv, CNNs on byte images deep models reading raw executables malware detection BERT-based classifiers transformers on URLs, emails and messages phishing and spam detection Graph neural networks learn from call graphs and provenance graphs malware and advanced-attack detection Code LLMs models that read and write code vulnerability detection and repair (evaluate carefully) AES, RSA, ECC; ML-KEM, ML-DSA standard and post-quantum cryptography secure communication; NIST’s post-quantum standards (2024) Simple tree models on good features are hard to beat in intrusion detection; deep models must earn their place against them, especially on new data."},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Hashes and the avalanche effect","x":"Interactive Hashes and the avalanche effect"},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Datasets and environments","x":"Section 3 of 5 Datasets and environments Resource Use CIC datasets (UNB) CIC-IDS2017, CIC-DDoS2019 and others for intrusion detection NSL-KDD a classic but outdated IDS benchmark — use only with newer data PhishTank, OpenPhish phishing URLs AndroZoo millions of Android apps for malware research (on request) VirusTotal malware labels from many engines Capture-the-flag (CTF) platforms legal practice environments for attacks and defences"},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Evaluation","x":"Section 3 of 5 Evaluation What How Detection precision, recall, F1, false-positive rate, ROC/AUC at realistic base rates Generalisation train on one dataset or time period, test on another Robustness success rate of evasion attacks Performance throughput, latency, overhead Usable security user studies: success, errors, time Attacks are rare : a 1% false-positive rate on a million flows is 10,000 false alarms. Avoid random splits of attack traffic recorded in one session — split by time."},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Tools","x":"Section 4 of 5 Tools Wireshark / Zeek Packet capture and network analysis. Suricata / Snort Signature-based intrusion detection. Burp Suite / OWASP ZAP Web application security testing. Ghidra Reverse engineering (NSA, free). AFL++ / libFuzzer Fuzzing. Kali Linux, VirtualBox A safe lab environment."},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Where security research is published","x":"Section 4 of 5 Where security research is published Type Venues Top conferences (“big four”) IEEE S&P, ACM CCS, USENIX Security, NDSS Other conferences ACSAC, RAID, ESORICS, AsiaCCS, Euro S&P Journals IEEE TIFS, IEEE TDSC, Computers & Security, ACM TOPS Good first venues workshops and regional IEEE security conferences"},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Thesis ideas","x":"Section 5 of 5 Thesis ideas Starter Phishing detection for local sites Collect Bangla phishing and legitimate URLs; compare URL-only and page-content models. Standard Cross-dataset intrusion detection Train on CIC-IDS2017, test on a newer dataset; measure the drop and test domain adaptation. Standard Android app privacy measurement Analyse permissions and third-party trackers in popular local apps. Standard Robustness of ML detectors Craft evasion attacks against a malware or spam classifier and test defences. Ambitious Post-quantum on IoT Benchmark post-quantum key exchange on microcontrollers: time, memory, energy."},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Getting started in four weeks","x":"Section 5 of 5 Getting started in four weeks Week 1 OWASP Top 10 and MITRE ATT&CK overview Week 2 set up a lab VM; capture and inspect traffic with Wireshark Week 3 read 5 recent papers from the big four on your topic Week 4 reproduce a simple detector on a public dataset; write a threat model and one-page idea Pitfalls: testing without permission, only old datasets (KDD99), random splits of session traffic, ignoring false positives, publishing a live vulnerability before disclosure."},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 2 Worked example 1 An IDS has 99% detection rate and 1% false-positive rate. The network sees 1,000,000 benign flows and 100 attacks a day. How many alerts are real? Show the solution True alerts ≈ 0.99 × 100 = 99. False alerts = 0.01 × 1,000,000 = 10,000. Precision ≈ 99 ÷ 10,099 ≈ 1%. Analysts would see almost only false alarms — false-positive rate matters more than accuracy."},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 2 Worked example 2 Write a threat model for a university exam-results portal. Show the solution Assets: grades, student personal data, admin accounts. Attackers: students wanting to change grades; outsiders seeking data. Capabilities: internet access, phishing, stolen passwords; not physical server access. Trusted: the hosting provider and the database server. Goals of defence: integrity of grades, confidentiality of data, availability during result publication."},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. What is a threat model? Show the answer A statement of who the attacker is, their goals and capabilities, what is trusted, and what must be protected. 2. Why is the false-positive rate critical in intrusion detection? Show the answer Attacks are rare, so even a small false-positive rate creates huge numbers of false alarms that hide real attacks. 3. What legal rule applies to security testing? Show the answer Only test systems you own or have explicit written permission to test; use labs, VMs and CTFs."},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. What is the avalanche effect? Show the answer A small change in the input (one bit) changes about half of the output bits of a good hash or cipher. 5. Name the “big four” security conferences. Show the answer IEEE S&P;, ACM CCS, USENIX Security, NDSS. 6. Why is NSL-KDD alone a weak choice of dataset? Show the answer It is old and has known artefacts; modern attacks and traffic differ, so results may not generalise. Use it only alongside newer datasets."},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Guide OWASP Top 10 ↗ The most critical web application risks. Knowledge base MITRE ATT&CK ↗ Adversary tactics and techniques. Data CIC datasets (University of New Brunswick) ↗ Intrusion, DDoS, malware and other datasets. Framework NIST Cybersecurity Framework ↗ Identify, protect, detect, respond, recover. Book Security Engineering, 3rd ed. — Ross Anderson ↗ Free chapters on building dependable secure systems. Practice picoCTF ↗ Free, legal capture-the-flag challenges."},{"u":"topics/16-cybersecurity.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 16 · Cybersecurity","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary Security research studies attacks and defences: detection, software and web security, cryptography, privacy, usable security, adversarial ML. Start every study with a threat model and evaluate against adaptive attackers. Rare attacks make false-positive rate and precision more important than accuracy. Use recent data, split by time, and test across datasets. Test only with permission; disclose vulnerabilities responsibly. Venues: IEEE S&P;, ACM CCS, USENIX Security, NDSS, plus ACSAC, RAID, ESORICS. Next: Biomedical and Health Informatics More practice All resources All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Biomedical and Health Informatics","x":"17 Chapter 17 of 24 · Part B · Research fields Biomedical and Health Informatics Medical imaging, biosignals, clinical prediction, clinical text, bioinformatics and digital health; clinical collaboration, patient-level evaluation, ethics, datasets, metrics, venues and thesis ideas. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters A test is 95% sensitive and 95% specific, and it says you have a rare disease. Should you panic? Surprisingly, you are probably fine. When a disease affects 1 person in 100, most positive results from that test are wrong. Health research rewards students who understand both the computing and the clinical meaning of their numbers — and who treat patient data with care. This chapter covers problems, methods, data, the right metrics, ethics and venues in biomedical informatics."},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 17 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. The field Methods Data and evaluation Tools and venues Thesis ideas"},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"What biomedical informatics research studies","x":"Section 1 of 5 What biomedical informatics research studies Biomedical and health informatics applies computing to medicine and biology: analysing medical images and signals, predicting disease risk from records, processing clinical text, analysing genes and proteins, and building digital-health tools. Errors can harm patients, so evaluation and ethics matter even more than in other fields. Rest on a branch of the map for a description."},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Typical research problems","x":"Section 1 of 5 Typical research problems Area Example thesis question Medical imaging Can a CNN grade diabetic retinopathy from fundus images, and does it hold on images from a local hospital? Biosignals Can arrhythmia be detected from a single-lead ECG of a cheap wearable? Clinical prediction Which features predict dengue severity on admission, and is the model calibrated? Clinical and health text Can symptoms be extracted from Bangla patient messages to a telemedicine service? Bioinformatics Which gene-expression features distinguish two cancer subtypes? Digital health and mHealth Does an SMS reminder system improve vaccination follow-up in a pilot community? Fairness and generalisation Does a skin-lesion model perform equally on darker skin tones?"},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Working with clinical problems","x":"Section 2 of 5 Working with clinical problems Find a clinical collaborator (doctor, public-health expert): they define what matters and check plausibility. Decide the clinical use : screening, diagnosis support, triage, prognosis? It decides the metric and threshold. Use patient-level splits and, ideally, an external test set from another hospital. Report sensitivity and specificity at a clinically chosen threshold, and calibration. Patient data needs ethics approval, de-identification and a data-use agreement. Never put identifiable patient data on a public cloud or into a public AI tool."},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Key methods to know","x":"Section 2 of 5 Key methods to know Image models CNNs and U-Net for classification and segmentation of X-ray, CT, MRI, fundus and histopathology images. Signal processing Filtering, R-peak detection, spectral features; 1-D CNNs for ECG and EEG. Tabular prediction Logistic regression and gradient boosting with calibration; survival analysis (Cox models). Clinical NLP Named-entity recognition for symptoms, drugs and diseases; domain-specific language models. Bioinformatics Sequence alignment, gene-expression analysis, feature selection, protein structure tools. Interpretability Saliency maps, SHAP and simple models clinicians can check."},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Popular models in biomedical informatics","x":"Section 2 of 5 Popular models in biomedical informatics Model What it is Typical use U-Net, nnU-Net segmentation networks; nnU-Net configures itself automatically organ and lesion segmentation — nnU-Net is the standard baseline DenseNet, ResNet, EfficientNet CNN classifiers X-ray, skin-lesion and fundus classification (e.g. CheXNet uses DenseNet-121) MedSAM, BiomedCLIP medical foundation models zero-shot segmentation and image–text tasks 1-D CNN / ResNet for signals deep models on ECG and EEG arrhythmia and seizure detection BioBERT, PubMedBERT, ClinicalBERT language models trained on biomedical text clinical NER, literature mining Logistic regression, XGBoost, Cox model interpretable risk and survival models clinical prediction with calibration AlphaFold protein-structure prediction structural biology and drug discovery Clinicians trust what they can check: always compare a deep model with a simple, interpretable one."},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Screening tests: why prevalence matters","x":"Interactive Screening tests: why prevalence matters"},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Datasets","x":"Section 3 of 5 Datasets Resource What it offers PhysioNet ECG, EEG and clinical databases (MIMIC needs credentialed access and training) The Cancer Imaging Archive CT, MRI and pathology images Grand Challenge medical-imaging challenges with data and leaderboards HAM10000, ISIC skin-lesion images NIH ChestX-ray14, CheXpert chest X-rays NCBI GEO gene-expression data"},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Evaluation","x":"Section 3 of 5 Evaluation Metric Clinical meaning Sensitivity (recall) share of sick patients detected Specificity share of healthy patients correctly cleared PPV / NPV chance a positive / negative result is right — depends on prevalence AUC-ROC ranking quality over all thresholds Calibration do predicted risks match observed rates? Dice / IoU segmentation overlap Report confidence intervals (bootstrap). Test on an external site. Analyse subgroups (age, sex, skin tone). Follow reporting guidelines such as TRIPOD+AI for prediction models."},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Tools","x":"Section 4 of 5 Tools MONAI PyTorch framework for medical imaging. 3D Slicer Viewing and annotating medical images. NeuroKit2 / WFDB Biosignal processing for ECG and EEG. lifelines / scikit-survival Survival analysis in Python. Biopython Sequence analysis. PubMed Search the biomedical literature."},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Where biomedical informatics is published","x":"Section 4 of 5 Where biomedical informatics is published Type Venues Medical imaging MICCAI, ISBI, IEEE TMI, Medical Image Analysis Health informatics AMIA, MEDINFO, JAMIA, JBI (Journal of Biomedical Informatics) ML for health ML4H, CHIL, MLHC Signals Computing in Cardiology, IEEE EMBC, IEEE JBHI Bioinformatics ISMB, Bioinformatics (Oxford), BMC Bioinformatics"},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Thesis ideas","x":"Section 5 of 5 Thesis ideas Starter External validation of a published model Test a public chest X-ray or skin-lesion model on another dataset; measure the drop and subgroup performance. Standard Wearable ECG arrhythmia detection Single-lead ECG classification on PhysioNet data; test on noisy recordings. Standard Calibrated risk prediction Predict severity of a local disease from admission data with a doctor; report calibration and decision thresholds. Standard Bangla health-message NLP Extract symptoms from anonymised telemedicine messages. Ambitious Federated learning across hospitals Simulate training across sites without sharing data; compare with central training."},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Getting started in four weeks","x":"Section 5 of 5 Getting started in four weeks Week 1 basics: sensitivity, specificity, PPV, ROC, calibration Week 2 get access to a public dataset (PhysioNet training if needed) Week 3 read 5 papers via PubMed and MICCAI; meet a clinician Week 4 baseline model with patient-level split; one-page idea Pitfalls: image-level instead of patient-level splits, accuracy on rare diseases, no external test, models learning hospital or scanner artefacts, identifiable data on public tools."},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 2 Worked example 1 A test has 95% sensitivity and 95% specificity; prevalence is 1%. Out of 10,000 people, what is the PPV? Show the solution Sick: 100; detected: 95. Healthy: 9,900; false positives: 5% = 495. PPV = 95 ÷ (95 + 495) ≈ 16%. Only one in six positives is truly sick — so this test is for screening followed by confirmation, not diagnosis."},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 2 Worked example 2 A pneumonia model trained in hospital A has AUC 0.93 internally but 0.78 at hospital B. What could cause it, and what do you do? Show the solution Differences in scanners, patient populations, labelling practices or shortcut features (e.g. text markers on images). Report both results honestly, analyse errors, try normalisation, augmentation or fine-tuning with a small local sample, and discuss limitations."},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Define sensitivity and specificity. Show the answer Sensitivity: share of truly sick patients the test detects. Specificity: share of truly healthy patients the test clears. 2. Why does PPV depend on prevalence? Show the answer With a rare disease, even a small false-positive rate among the many healthy people produces more false than true positives. 3. Why split by patient? Show the answer Several images or records from one patient in train and test let the model recognise the patient, inflating results."},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. What approvals does patient data need? Show the answer Ethics approval, de-identification, and a data-use agreement or credentialed access (e.g. PhysioNet for MIMIC). 5. Name two medical-imaging venues. Show the answer MICCAI, ISBI (journals: IEEE TMI, Medical Image Analysis). 6. What is calibration and why does it matter clinically? Show the answer Whether predicted probabilities match observed frequencies; doctors act on risk estimates, so a 30% prediction must mean about 30% risk."},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Data PhysioNet ↗ Biosignal and clinical databases, with training for restricted data. Data The Cancer Imaging Archive ↗ Public medical images. Challenges Grand Challenge ↗ Medical-imaging challenges with data. Tool MONAI ↗ Medical imaging deep learning. Guideline TRIPOD+AI ↗ Reporting guideline for clinical prediction models using AI. Search PubMed ↗ The biomedical literature."},{"u":"topics/17-biomedical-and-health-informatics.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 17 · Biomedical and Health Informatics","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary Biomedical informatics covers imaging, biosignals, clinical prediction, clinical text, bioinformatics and digital health. Work with a clinician; define the clinical use, which decides the metric and threshold. Split by patient, validate externally, report sensitivity, specificity, PPV/NPV, calibration and confidence intervals. Prevalence changes PPV dramatically: understand it before claiming a test is useful. Patient data needs approval, de-identification and agreements; never use public AI tools on it. Venues: MICCAI, ISBI, AMIA, JAMIA, ML4H, CHIL, Computing in Cardiology. Next: Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval More practice All resources All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","x":"18 Chapter 18 of 24 · Part B · Research fields Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval Clustering, association rules, recommender systems, search and ranking, anomaly detection, forecasting and graph mining; the CRISP-DM process, datasets, metrics, tools, venues and thesis ideas. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters A shop owner has ten years of sales records on a dusty hard drive. “There must be gold in here,” he says. There is — mixed with a lot of sand. Data-science research turns raw data into knowledge you can trust: patterns that hold up, rankings that serve users and forecasts tested honestly. Local data — sales, health, agriculture, transport — is full of good questions. This chapter covers data mining, recommender systems and information retrieval."},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 18 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. The field Methods Data and evaluation Tools and venues Thesis ideas"},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"What data science research studies","x":"Section 1 of 5 What data science research studies Data science and data mining discover useful patterns in large datasets: clusters, associations, anomalies, trends and predictions. Information retrieval (IR) finds and ranks relevant items — web pages, products, documents — and powers search engines and recommender systems. Rest on a branch of the map for a description."},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Typical research problems","x":"Section 1 of 5 Typical research problems Area Example thesis question Clustering and segmentation Which customer segments emerge from a local e-commerce dataset, and are they stable over time? Recommender systems Does adding review text improve book recommendations for users with few ratings (cold start)? Search and ranking How well do neural retrievers rank Bangla news for user queries compared with BM25? Anomaly detection Can unusual electricity-meter readings reveal theft or faults? Time-series forecasting Which model forecasts daily dengue cases best from weather and past cases? Graph mining Can community detection on a co-authorship network reveal research groups? Data quality and visual analytics How do missing-data methods change conclusions from a health survey?"},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"The data-mining process (CRISP-DM)","x":"Section 2 of 5 The data-mining process (CRISP-DM) Business understanding what decision will the result support? Data understanding explore, visualise, check quality Data preparation clean, join, engineer features Modelling try several methods Evaluation against the goal, not only a metric Deployment report, dashboard or system CRISP-DM is the most widely used process model for data projects. For a thesis, add a clear research question and a comparison with baselines at the modelling step."},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Key methods to know","x":"Section 2 of 5 Key methods to know Exploratory analysis Summaries, distributions, correlations and visualisation before any model. Clustering k-means, hierarchical clustering, DBSCAN; choosing k with the elbow or silhouette. Association rules Apriori, FP-growth: “customers who buy X also buy Y”. Recommendation Collaborative filtering, matrix factorisation, neural and sequence-aware recommenders. Retrieval and ranking BM25, dense retrieval with embeddings, learning to rank, re-ranking. Time series and anomalies ARIMA, Prophet, gradient boosting with lags; isolation forest, autoencoders."},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Popular models in data science and IR","x":"Section 2 of 5 Popular models in data science and IR Model What it is Typical use k-means, DBSCAN, HDBSCAN clustering algorithms segmentation, grouping, exploration Apriori, FP-growth frequent-pattern mining association rules, market baskets BM25 classic ranking function the retrieval baseline every IR study needs DPR, ColBERT, BGE / E5 embeddings dense neural retrievers semantic search, RAG Cross-encoder re-rankers (monoBERT, monoT5) score query–document pairs together second-stage re-ranking Matrix factorisation (ALS), LightGCN collaborative-filtering models recommendations from interactions SASRec, two-tower models sequential and scalable recommenders next-item and large-scale recommendation ARIMA, Prophet, Temporal Fusion Transformer, N-BEATS forecasting models sales, demand, disease forecasting Strong old baselines (BM25, ARIMA, popularity) often beat new models on small data — report them."},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"k-means step by step","x":"Interactive k-means step by step"},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Datasets","x":"Section 3 of 5 Datasets Dataset Use MovieLens recommender systems MS MARCO, BEIR retrieval and ranking benchmarks Kaggle e-commerce, finance and public-data sets (check quality) UCI / OpenML tabular and time-series data Open government data local statistics: agriculture, health, transport, education SNAP (Stanford) large network datasets for graph mining"},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Evaluation","x":"Section 3 of 5 Evaluation Task Metrics Clustering silhouette, Davies–Bouldin; adjusted Rand index if labels exist Recommendation precision@k, recall@k, nDCG, hit rate; coverage, diversity Retrieval MRR, nDCG@10, recall@100 Forecasting MAE, RMSE, MAPE on a rolling time split Anomaly detection precision/recall at the alert budget Clusters need interpretation : describe each with real features. Split recommender data by time to avoid using the future. Compare with simple baselines: most-popular items, BM25, last-value forecast."},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Tools","x":"Section 4 of 5 Tools pandas / Polars Data manipulation. scikit-learn Clustering, anomaly detection, pipelines. Matplotlib / Plotly / Seaborn Visualisation. Apache Spark Data too large for one machine. Pyserini / FAISS Retrieval: BM25 and dense vector search. RecBole / implicit Recommender-system libraries."},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Where this research is published","x":"Section 4 of 5 Where this research is published Type Venues Data mining KDD, ICDM, SDM, PAKDD, CIKM Web and IR SIGIR, WWW (The Web Conference), WSDM, ECIR Recommender systems RecSys Journals IEEE TKDE, Data Mining and Knowledge Discovery, ACM TOIS, Information Processing & Management"},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Thesis ideas","x":"Section 5 of 5 Thesis ideas Starter Customer or student segmentation Cluster a real dataset, validate clusters, and describe them for decision makers. Standard Cold-start recommendation Combine content features with collaborative filtering for new users or items; evaluate with a time split. Standard Bangla search Build a test collection of Bangla queries and compare BM25 with dense retrievers. Standard Disease or demand forecasting Forecast weekly cases or sales with weather and holidays; compare statistical and ML models. Ambitious Graph anomaly detection Detect fraud rings in a transaction graph with graph neural networks."},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Getting started in four weeks","x":"Section 5 of 5 Getting started in four weeks Week 1 pandas and visualisation refresher; CRISP-DM Week 2 explore a dataset: quality report and 10 plots Week 3 read 5 recent KDD/SIGIR/RecSys papers on your task Week 4 baseline with a time-aware split; one-page idea Pitfalls: skipping exploratory analysis, random splits of time-ordered data, uninterpretable clusters, comparing only against weak baselines, ignoring data quality."},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 2 Worked example 1 You cluster 5,000 customers with k-means and get 4 clusters. How do you make this research rather than a demo? Show the solution Justify k (silhouette, elbow, stability across seeds), describe each cluster with its feature profile, check stability over time (e.g. two years), compare with another method (hierarchical, GMM), and test whether the segments predict something useful (churn, spending) better than a baseline segmentation."},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 2 Worked example 2 A recommender is evaluated by randomly hiding 20% of ratings. What is wrong? Show the solution Random hiding lets the model use future interactions to predict past ones. Split by time (train on earlier interactions, test on later ones) to match real use."},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Name the six phases of CRISP-DM. Show the answer Business understanding, data understanding, data preparation, modelling, evaluation, deployment. 2. How do you choose k in k-means? Show the answer Elbow method on within-cluster error, silhouette score, stability across runs, and interpretability. 3. What is BM25? Show the answer A classic ranking function scoring documents by term frequency, inverse document frequency and document length — a strong retrieval baseline."},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. Name two metrics for ranking quality. Show the answer nDCG, MRR (also precision@k, recall@k). 5. What is the cold-start problem? Show the answer Recommending for new users or items with little or no interaction history. 6. Name two data-mining and two IR venues. Show the answer Data mining: KDD, ICDM (also SDM, PAKDD, CIKM). IR: SIGIR, WWW (also WSDM, ECIR, RecSys)."},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Book Mining of Massive Datasets — Leskovec, Rajaraman & Ullman ↗ Free book: similarity, clustering, recommendation, graphs. Book Introduction to Information Retrieval — Manning, Raghavan & Schütze ↗ Free online IR textbook. Data MovieLens ↗ Standard recommender datasets. Data SNAP datasets ↗ Large network datasets. Course Kaggle Learn ↗ Short practical courses on pandas, visualisation and ML. Tool Pyserini ↗ Reproducible retrieval baselines (BM25, dense)."},{"u":"topics/18-data-science-and-information-retrieval.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 18 · Data Science, Data Mining and Information Retrieval","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary Data science discovers patterns; information retrieval finds and ranks relevant items. Follow CRISP-DM, with a research question and baselines at the modelling step. Methods: clustering, association rules, recommenders, BM25 and dense retrieval, forecasting, anomaly and graph mining. Evaluate with task metrics (silhouette, nDCG, MRR, MAE) and time-aware splits. Interpret results for decision makers: clusters and patterns must make sense. Venues: KDD, ICDM, CIKM, SIGIR, WWW, WSDM, RecSys. Next: IoT, Networks and Edge Computing More practice All resources All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","x":"19 Chapter 19 of 24 · Part B · Research fields IoT, Networks and Edge Computing IoT systems, energy efficiency, edge AI, wireless and mobile networks, SDN and network measurement; simulation versus testbeds, metrics such as latency, delivery ratio and energy, tools, venues and thesis ideas. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters A flood sensor that warns the village ten minutes after the water arrives is not a sensor. It is a very expensive diary. Networking and IoT research is about real limits — delay, bandwidth, energy, cost — measured in simulation and on real devices. Agriculture, water and transport give many local problems to solve. This chapter introduces networking, IoT and edge computing research: methods, tools, metrics, venues and ideas."},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 19 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. The field Methods Data and evaluation Tools and venues Thesis ideas"},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"What networking and IoT research studies","x":"Section 1 of 5 What networking and IoT research studies Networking research studies how data moves: protocols, wireless and mobile networks, performance and reliability. The Internet of Things (IoT) connects sensors and devices; edge computing processes their data close to where it is produced instead of in a distant cloud. Research balances latency, bandwidth, energy, cost and reliability . Rest on a branch of the map for a description."},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Typical research problems","x":"Section 1 of 5 Typical research problems Area Example thesis question IoT systems Can low-cost soil sensors with LoRa report moisture reliably across a 2 km farm for a whole season? Energy efficiency Which duty-cycling scheme extends battery life of sensor nodes most without losing events? Edge AI Is it better to run a detection model on the camera, on an edge box or in the cloud, for latency and cost? Wireless and mobile How does Wi-Fi throughput degrade in crowded university halls, and does channel assignment help? Routing and SDN Can software-defined networking reroute traffic faster after a link failure? Network measurement What latency and packet loss do mobile users in different districts experience? IoT security How many consumer IoT devices on a campus network use default passwords? (with permission)"},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"How networking research is done","x":"Section 2 of 5 How networking research is done Simulation and emulation ns-3, OMNeT++, Cooja (for IoT), Mininet (for SDN) large, repeatable scenarios cheaply results only as good as the model of reality Testbeds and real deployments real devices: ESP32, Raspberry Pi, LoRa modules convincing, captures real noise and failures small scale; harder to repeat A strong thesis often does both: simulate to explore many settings, then validate the key result on a small real testbed."},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Key methods to know","x":"Section 2 of 5 Key methods to know Protocols TCP/IP, MQTT and CoAP for IoT, HTTP/3/QUIC; how they behave under loss and delay. Wireless technologies Wi-Fi, Bluetooth Low Energy, Zigbee, LoRaWAN, NB-IoT, 5G — range, rate and energy trade-offs. Queueing and performance models Latency, throughput, utilisation; Little’s law. SDN and virtualisation Programmable control of networks; network functions in software. Edge and fog computing Task offloading, placement and scheduling between device, edge and cloud. TinyML Running small ML models on microcontrollers."},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Popular models, platforms and standards in IoT and networking","x":"Section 2 of 5 Popular models, platforms and standards in IoT and networking Model / platform What it is Typical use MQTT, CoAP lightweight IoT messaging protocols sensor data to brokers and servers LoRaWAN, BLE, Zigbee, NB-IoT low-power wireless standards long-range or short-range sensor links Log-distance path loss, Okumura–Hata radio propagation models predicting range and signal strength in simulation M/M/1 and other queueing models mathematical models of delay analysing latency and capacity MobileNet, YOLO-nano, DS-CNN tiny neural networks vision and keyword spotting on edge devices TensorFlow Lite Micro ML runtime for microcontrollers TinyML deployment DQN / PPO for offloading reinforcement learning deciding where to run tasks: device, edge or cloud Say which propagation and traffic models your simulator uses — results depend heavily on them."},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Edge or cloud? A latency calculator","x":"Interactive Edge or cloud? A latency calculator"},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Data, testbeds and simulators","x":"Section 3 of 5 Data, testbeds and simulators Resource Use ns-3 discrete-event network simulator for research Mininet emulate SDN networks on a laptop Cooja (Contiki-NG) simulate IoT sensor networks FIT IoT-LAB large open IoT testbed in Europe (remote access) CRAWDAD archive of wireless network traces Your own testbed ESP32 / Raspberry Pi nodes, LoRa gateways — a few hundred dollars"},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Evaluation","x":"Section 3 of 5 Evaluation Metric Meaning Latency (and its tail, e.g. 99th percentile) delay from send to receive Throughput data delivered per second Packet delivery ratio / loss reliability Energy per message, battery lifetime crucial for IoT Jitter variation in delay Cost and scalability devices, bandwidth, cloud bills Report distributions (CDFs, percentiles), not only averages. Repeat measurements at different times; networks vary. State the full setup: hardware, firmware, distances, interference."},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Tools","x":"Section 4 of 5 Tools ns-3 / OMNeT++ Network simulation. Mininet / Ryu / ONOS SDN experiments. Wireshark / iperf3 Capture traffic; measure throughput. ESP32, Arduino, Raspberry Pi Cheap IoT hardware. MQTT brokers (Mosquitto) IoT messaging. TensorFlow Lite Micro TinyML on microcontrollers."},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Where this research is published","x":"Section 4 of 5 Where this research is published Type Venues Networking ACM SIGCOMM, USENIX NSDI, IEEE INFOCOM, ACM CoNEXT Mobile and wireless ACM MobiCom, MobiSys, IEEE ICC, IEEE GLOBECOM IoT and sensors ACM SenSys, IPSN, EWSN Journals IEEE/ACM Transactions on Networking, IEEE Internet of Things Journal, IEEE TMC, Computer Networks"},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Thesis ideas","x":"Section 5 of 5 Thesis ideas Starter Smart-agriculture sensor network Deploy LoRa soil sensors; measure delivery ratio, range and battery life over weeks. Starter MQTT versus CoAP under poor networks Compare protocols for latency, overhead and energy with emulated loss. Standard Edge versus cloud inference Measure latency, bandwidth and cost of running a vision model at device, edge and cloud. Standard SDN fast failover Design and evaluate a recovery scheme in Mininet. Ambitious TinyML anomaly detection Detect machine faults from vibration data on a microcontroller; measure accuracy and energy."},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Getting started in four weeks","x":"Section 5 of 5 Getting started in four weeks Week 1 refresh TCP/IP, wireless basics, MQTT Week 2 run an ns-3 or Mininet tutorial; build an ESP32 sensor sending MQTT data Week 3 read 5 recent papers (IEEE IoT Journal, SenSys) Week 4 measure one real metric (latency or battery) end to end; one-page idea Pitfalls: only averages (no tail latency), simulation settings unstated, a single measurement run, unrealistic radio models, ignoring energy in IoT."},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 2 Worked example 1 A camera sends 1 MB images for detection. Uplink is 2 Mbps; cloud round trip 80 ms; cloud inference 30 ms; on-device inference 400 ms. Which is faster per image? Show the solution Cloud: transfer 8 Mbit ÷ 2 Mbps = 4 s, + 0.08 + 0.03 ≈ 4.11 s. Device: 0.4 s. On-device is about 10× faster here; the cloud wins only with much faster uplink or a much slower device — exactly the trade-off to measure in an edge-computing thesis."},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 2 Worked example 2 Your simulation shows 99% packet delivery; a field test shows 82%. What do you report? Show the solution Both, and the difference is a finding: the simulator’s radio model missed real interference, obstacles or weather. Analyse the field losses (distance, time of day), and adjust the simulation or discuss the limitation."},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Name three trade-offs IoT research balances. Show the answer Latency, bandwidth, energy, cost, reliability, range. 2. Simulation or testbed: one advantage of each. Show the answer Simulation: large, cheap, repeatable scenarios. Testbed: real behaviour, noise and failures. 3. Why report the 99th-percentile latency? Show the answer Averages hide rare long delays that matter for real-time applications."},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. Name two IoT messaging protocols. Show the answer MQTT and CoAP. 5. What is edge computing? Show the answer Processing data near its source (on the device or a nearby server) instead of in a distant cloud, to cut latency and bandwidth. 6. Name two networking and two IoT venues. Show the answer Networking: SIGCOMM, INFOCOM (also NSDI, CoNEXT). IoT: SenSys, IPSN (also IEEE Internet of Things Journal)."},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Tool ns-3 ↗ Network simulator with tutorials. Tool Mininet ↗ SDN network emulation. Tool Contiki-NG and Cooja ↗ IoT operating system and simulator. Book Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach — Kurose & Ross Pearson, 8th ed. The standard networking textbook. Course TinyML (Harvard edX) ↗ Machine learning on microcontrollers. Testbed FIT IoT-LAB ↗ Open large-scale IoT testbed."},{"u":"topics/19-iot-networks-and-edge-computing.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 19 · IoT, Networks and Edge Computing","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary Networking and IoT research balances latency, bandwidth, energy, cost and reliability. Edge computing moves processing close to the data to cut delay and bandwidth. Use simulation (ns-3, Mininet, Cooja) to explore and real testbeds (ESP32, LoRa, Raspberry Pi) to validate. Report distributions and tail latency, energy and delivery ratio, with the full setup. Local problems — agriculture, water, transport — make strong, practical theses. Venues: SIGCOMM, NSDI, INFOCOM, MobiCom, SenSys, IEEE IoT Journal. Next: Software Engineering Research More practice All resources All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Software Engineering Research","x":"20 Chapter 20 of 24 · Part B · Research fields Software Engineering Research Testing, defect prediction, code quality, AI for code, mining software repositories and developer studies; experiments, mining, tool building, surveys and case studies; data, metrics, tools, venues and thesis ideas. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters Every developer swears by a favourite practice — tests first, pair programming, AI assistants, tabs over spaces. Ask for evidence and the room gets loud. Software engineering research settles such arguments with tools and data from real projects and real developers. With GitHub and AI coding assistants, there are more open questions than ever. This chapter introduces SE research: problems, methods, data, metrics, tools and venues."},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 20 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. The field Methods Data and evaluation Tools and venues Thesis ideas"},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"What software engineering research studies","x":"Section 1 of 5 What software engineering research studies Software engineering (SE) research studies how software is built, tested, maintained and used by teams — and how to do it better. It mixes tools (program analysis, test generation, AI coding assistants) with empirical studies of real projects and developers. Rest on a branch of the map for a description."},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Typical research problems","x":"Section 1 of 5 Typical research problems Area Example thesis question Testing Do automatically generated unit tests find as many seeded bugs as student-written tests? Defect prediction Can code metrics from previous releases predict which files will have bugs in the next release? Code quality How common are code smells in open-source Android apps, and are they linked to more bug fixes? AI for code How often is code suggested by AI assistants insecure, and which prompts reduce it? Mining software repositories How long do pull requests wait for review in popular projects, and what predicts delay? Requirements and process How do small Bangladeshi software companies manage requirements changes? Program repair Can an LLM fix failing tests in Defects4J bugs, and how often are its fixes correct rather than test-passing?"},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"How SE research is done","x":"Section 2 of 5 How SE research is done Method Use for Example Controlled experiment does tool or technique X help? two groups of students debug with and without a tool Mining software repositories what happens in real projects, at scale analyse commits, issues and pull requests from GitHub Tool building + benchmark a new technique a test generator evaluated on a bug benchmark Survey and interviews practice and opinions of developers questionnaire of 100 local developers Case study one company or project in depth adopting code review in one team These are the designs of chapter 7 applied to software; most SE theses combine two of them."},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Which SE method fits?","x":"Interactive Which SE method fits?"},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Key techniques to know","x":"Section 2 of 5 Key techniques to know Static analysis Inspect code without running it: linters, SonarQube, type checkers, data-flow analysis. Testing techniques Unit, integration and mutation testing; coverage; fuzzing; property-based tests. Repository mining GitHub API, commit histories, issue trackers; linking commits to bugs (SZZ algorithm). Software metrics Size, complexity, churn, coupling — features for prediction. AI for code Code LLMs for generation, summarisation, review and repair — and their evaluation. Qualitative analysis Coding interview transcripts into themes (thematic analysis)."},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Popular models and tools in software-engineering research","x":"Section 2 of 5 Popular models and tools in software-engineering research Model / tool What it is Typical use CodeBERT, CodeT5 pretrained models of code code search, summarisation, defect prediction StarCoder 2, Code Llama, DeepSeek-Coder, Qwen-Coder open code LLMs code generation and repair experiments GitHub Copilot and chat assistants commercial AI coding tools studies of developer use and code quality SWE-agent and similar agents LLM agents that edit repositories issue fixing on SWE-bench EvoSuite, Randoop search-based and random test generators baselines for test generation Random forest on code metrics classic defect-prediction model baseline for bug prediction SZZ algorithm links bug fixes to the commits that caused them building defect datasets from history When you evaluate AI for code, record the model version, the prompt and whether results are checked beyond passing tests."},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Data and benchmarks","x":"Section 3 of 5 Data and benchmarks Resource Use GitHub REST API repositories, commits, issues, pull requests Defects4J real Java bugs with tests: testing and repair research HumanEval, MBPP, SWE-bench code-generation and issue-fixing benchmarks for AI Software Heritage / GHTorrent-style datasets large archives of source code and history Stack Overflow data dump developer questions and answers PROMISE / defect datasets defect prediction"},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Evaluation","x":"Section 3 of 5 Evaluation Study Measures Testing tools bugs found, mutation score, coverage, time Prediction precision, recall, AUC; effort-aware metrics AI code pass@k, correctness beyond tests, security issues Experiments with people task time, correctness, perceived workload Surveys response rate, representative sample, validated questions For mining studies, state how repositories were selected (stars, activity, language) and filter out forks and toy projects. For AI code, check that test-passing fixes are really correct ."},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Tools","x":"Section 4 of 5 Tools PyDriller / GitPython Mine Git histories in Python. SonarQube Code-quality and smell analysis. EvoSuite / Randoop Automatic Java test generation. PIT / mutmut Mutation testing for Java / Python. JaCoCo / coverage.py Code coverage. NVivo / Taguette Qualitative coding of interviews."},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Where SE research is published","x":"Section 4 of 5 Where SE research is published Type Venues Top conferences ICSE, ESEC/FSE, ASE, ISSTA Repository mining and maintenance MSR, ICSME, SANER Empirical SE ESEM, EASE Journals IEEE TSE, ACM TOSEM, Empirical Software Engineering, JSS, IST"},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Thesis ideas","x":"Section 5 of 5 Thesis ideas Starter Code-smell study of local projects Analyse open-source projects by Bangladeshi developers with SonarQube; relate smells to bug fixes. Standard Security of AI-generated code Generate solutions to tasks with AI assistants and scan them for vulnerabilities; test safer prompting. Standard Generated vs human tests Compare EvoSuite tests with developer tests by mutation score on real projects. Standard Developer practices survey Survey and interview local developers on testing or code review; thematic analysis. Ambitious LLM program repair, verified Evaluate LLM fixes on Defects4J with manual correctness checks beyond tests."},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Getting started in four weeks","x":"Section 5 of 5 Getting started in four weeks Week 1 read two MSR or ICSE papers close to your interest Week 2 mine one repository with PyDriller; plot commit activity Week 3 run a tool (SonarQube, EvoSuite) on a real project Week 4 define the study design and sample; one-page idea Pitfalls: biased repository samples (only famous projects), forks and bots in the data, survey questions that lead the answer, trusting tests as proof that AI code is correct."},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 2 Worked example 1 Design a mining study: “Do projects with continuous integration have fewer bugs?” Show the solution Sample: 500 active GitHub Java projects (≥50 stars, ≥2 years of history, no forks), half adopting CI during their life. Measure bug-fix commits per KLOC before and after CI adoption (a before/after design within each project), control for size and activity, and discuss confounders (mature teams adopt CI)."},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 2 Worked example 2 An LLM repairs 40% of Defects4J bugs because the tests now pass. Why is that not the full answer? Show the solution Tests are incomplete: a patch can pass them but be wrong (overfitting patches). Manually check a sample of patches for semantic correctness against the developer fix, and report the correct-fix rate separately."},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Name four research methods used in SE. Show the answer Controlled experiments, mining software repositories, tool building with benchmarks, surveys and interviews, case studies. 2. What is mutation testing? Show the answer Seeding small artificial faults (mutants) into code and measuring how many the test suite detects — the mutation score. 3. What is Defects4J used for? Show the answer A benchmark of real Java bugs with tests, used for testing, fault localisation and program-repair research."},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. Why filter forks and toy projects when mining GitHub? Show the answer They duplicate data or do not represent real development, biasing the results. 5. Name three top SE venues. Show the answer ICSE, ESEC/FSE, ASE (also ISSTA, MSR; journals TSE, TOSEM). 6. Give one risk in evaluating AI-generated code. Show the answer Passing tests does not prove correctness or security; benchmarks may be in training data; prompts and model versions must be recorded."},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Conference MSR — Mining Software Repositories ↗ The main venue for repository mining, with data showcases. Data Defects4J ↗ Real Java bugs for testing and repair research. Tool PyDriller ↗ Python framework for mining Git. API GitHub REST API ↗ Access repositories, issues and pull requests. Book Experimentation in Software Engineering — Wohlin et al. Springer, 2nd ed. 2024. Experiments, validity and analysis. Tool SonarQube ↗ Code-quality analysis."},{"u":"topics/20-software-engineering-research.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 20 · Software Engineering Research","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary SE research studies how software is built, tested and maintained, with tools and empirical evidence. Methods: experiments, repository mining, tool building with benchmarks, surveys/interviews, case studies. Know static analysis, testing (mutation, coverage), metrics, repository mining and AI-for-code evaluation. Select repositories and participants carefully; filter forks, bots and toy projects. Passing tests is not proof of correctness — check AI fixes manually. Venues: ICSE, ESEC/FSE, ASE, ISSTA, MSR, ICSME; journals TSE, TOSEM, EMSE. Next: Human–Computer Interaction More practice All resources All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Human–Computer Interaction","x":"21 Chapter 21 of 24 · Part B · Research fields Human–Computer Interaction Usability, accessibility, interaction techniques, ICTD, human–AI interaction and education technology; user-centred research methods, participants and ethics, SUS and other measures, tools, venues and thesis ideas. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters A mobile-banking app passes every test. Then a real user sends ৳5,000 to the wrong number, because the “Confirm” button sat exactly where her thumb rests. HCI research finds out how people really use technology and designs it to fit them — with evidence from observation, experiments and interviews, not opinions. This chapter introduces HCI methods, measures, ethics, tools and venues, with ideas for local users."},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 21 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. The field Methods Data and evaluation Tools and venues Thesis ideas"},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"What HCI research studies","x":"Section 1 of 5 What HCI research studies Human–computer interaction (HCI) studies how people use technology and designs technology that fits people: usable interfaces, accessible apps, new interaction techniques, and how technology affects communities. Its evidence comes mostly from people — observations, interviews, experiments and surveys. Rest on a branch of the map for a description."},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Typical research problems","x":"Section 1 of 5 Typical research problems Area Example thesis question Usability Which design of a mobile-banking transfer screen leads to fewer errors among first-time users? Accessibility How do visually impaired users in Bangladesh use smartphones, and which barriers are most common in local apps? Interaction techniques Is voice input faster than typing for Bangla text on low-cost phones? Information and communication technology for development (ICTD) How do rural farmers use mobile information services, and what makes them trust advice? Human–AI interaction Do explanations make users rely on an AI suggestion more appropriately — or just more? Education technology Does a visual step-through tool help students understand recursion? Health and well-being What makes students keep using a mental-health app beyond the first week?"},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"The user-centred research process","x":"Section 2 of 5 The user-centred research process Understand interviews, observation, surveys Define personas, tasks, requirements Ideate sketches, alternatives Prototype paper → Figma → working app Evaluate usability tests, experiments Iterate improve and test again For research, the evaluation must be planned like an experiment: a question, participants, tasks, measures and analysis — not just “users liked it”."},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Key methods to know","x":"Section 2 of 5 Key methods to know Interviews and observation Semi-structured interviews, contextual inquiry; analysed with thematic analysis. Surveys Validated questionnaires (SUS, NASA-TLX, UEQ) and good question design. Usability testing Users perform tasks while thinking aloud; measure success, time and errors. Controlled experiments Compare designs A and B with enough participants; within- or between-subjects. Heuristic evaluation Experts check an interface against principles such as Nielsen’s ten heuristics. Field deployment Use in real life over weeks: logs plus interviews."},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Popular models and instruments in HCI","x":"Section 2 of 5 Popular models and instruments in HCI Model / instrument What it is Typical use Fitts’s law predicts the time to hit a target from its size and distance button and touch-target design Hick–Hyman law decision time grows with the number of choices menu design GOMS / Keystroke-Level Model predicts expert task time from basic actions comparing interface designs before testing Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), UTAUT models of why people adopt technology survey studies of adoption SUS, UEQ standard usability and experience questionnaires measuring perceived usability NASA-TLX workload questionnaire how demanding a task feels WCAG 2.2 accessibility guidelines auditing and designing accessible interfaces In HCI, “models” are often theories of human behaviour; using a validated questionnaire is better than inventing your own."},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"System Usability Scale calculator","x":"Interactive System Usability Scale calculator"},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Participants and ethics","x":"Section 3 of 5 Participants and ethics Recruit participants who match the real users (not only classmates). How many? 5–8 users find most usability problems in a formative test; comparisons need more — often 20+ per condition, or a power analysis. Within-subjects designs: counterbalance the order of conditions. Pilot your study with two people before running it. People are your data: get informed consent, pay or thank participants fairly, protect recordings, and check whether ethics approval is required (chapter 9)."},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Evaluation","x":"Section 3 of 5 Evaluation Measure How Effectiveness task success rate, errors Efficiency task time, steps Satisfaction SUS score, UEQ, interviews Workload NASA-TLX Accessibility WCAG checks plus tests with disabled users Long-term use retention, logs, follow-up interviews Combine numbers with why : quotes and observed problems explain the results. Use appropriate statistics for small samples (e.g. Wilcoxon, Mann–Whitney)."},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Tools","x":"Section 4 of 5 Tools Figma Prototypes for testing before coding. Maze / Lookback / OBS Remote testing and screen recording. Google Forms / LimeSurvey Questionnaires. Taguette / NVivo Coding interview transcripts. R / Python (SciPy) Statistics for experiments. Accessibility checkers Lighthouse, axe, screen readers (TalkBack, VoiceOver)."},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Where HCI research is published","x":"Section 4 of 5 Where HCI research is published Type Venues Top conference ACM CHI Other ACM conferences UIST, CSCW, DIS, IUI, ASSETS (accessibility), MobileHCI ICTD ACM COMPASS, ICTD Journals ACM TOCHI, International Journal of Human-Computer Studies, Behaviour & Information Technology"},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Thesis ideas","x":"Section 5 of 5 Thesis ideas Starter Usability study of a local service Test a popular local app or website with 8–10 users; report problems, SUS and redesign suggestions. Starter Accessibility audit Check local apps against WCAG and with screen-reader users; categorise barriers. Standard Bangla text-entry experiment Compare keyboard layouts or voice input for speed and errors in a controlled study. Standard Trust in AI advice Experiment on how explanations change reliance on an AI assistant’s suggestions. Ambitious Field deployment Design, deploy and study a tool with a community (farmers, patients) over several weeks."},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Getting started in four weeks","x":"Section 5 of 5 Getting started in four weeks Week 1 Nielsen’s heuristics, SUS, thematic analysis basics Week 2 observe or interview 3 target users Week 3 read 5 recent CHI papers on your topic Week 4 paper prototype + pilot test; one-page idea Pitfalls: testing only with friends, leading questions, “users liked it” as the only result, too few participants for a comparison, no consent."},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 2 Worked example 1 Design a study comparing two layouts (A and B) of a payment screen. Show the solution Within-subjects experiment with 24 participants who use mobile banking; each completes 5 transfers with A and 5 with B, order counterbalanced. Measures: errors, task time, SUS for each layout, short interview. Analysis: Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on time and errors, thematic analysis of interviews. Consent and anonymised recordings."},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 2 Worked example 2 Ten users give SUS answers; the mean SUS score is 58. What does it mean? Show the solution About 68 is the commonly cited average, so 58 is below average usability. Combine with observed problems (where users failed or hesitated) to explain why, and prioritise fixes."},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Name four HCI research methods. Show the answer Interviews and observation, surveys, usability testing, controlled experiments, heuristic evaluation, field deployments. 2. How many users find most usability problems in a formative test? Show the answer About 5–8, tested in rounds; comparison experiments need more. 3. What does counterbalancing prevent? Show the answer Order effects (learning or fatigue) in within-subjects designs."},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. What is the SUS? Show the answer A 10-item questionnaire with 1–5 answers giving a usability score from 0 to 100. 5. Name two HCI venues. Show the answer ACM CHI, UIST (also CSCW, ASSETS, TOCHI). 6. Why is “users liked it” a weak result? Show the answer It is subjective, unmeasured and possibly biased; a study needs defined tasks, measures and analysis."},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Guide Nielsen Norman Group articles ↗ Usability methods and research findings. Guide Interaction Design Foundation ↗ Free articles on user research and design. Guide Measuring U: System Usability Scale ↗ How to score and interpret SUS. Standard WCAG overview (W3C) ↗ Web accessibility guidelines. Book Research Methods in Human-Computer Interaction — Lazar, Feng & Hochheiser Morgan Kaufmann, 2nd ed. 2017. Experiments, surveys, interviews, analysis. Book The Design of Everyday Things — Don Norman Basic Books, revised ed. 2013. Foundations of user-centred design."},{"u":"topics/21-human-computer-interaction.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 21 · Human–Computer Interaction","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary HCI studies how people use technology and designs technology that fits them. Methods: interviews, observation, surveys, usability tests, experiments, heuristic evaluation, field deployments. Plan studies like experiments: question, participants, tasks, measures, analysis; pilot first. Measure effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction (SUS), and explain the numbers with observations. Participants need consent, fair treatment and privacy; recruit real target users. Venues: CHI, UIST, CSCW, ASSETS, COMPASS; journals TOCHI, IJHCS. Next: Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design More practice All resources All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","x":"22 Chapter 22 of 24 · Part B · Research fields Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design Caches and memory, processors, digital logic and arithmetic circuits, accelerators on FPGAs, RISC-V, verification, low-power design and hardware security; the hardware design flow, simulation versus FPGA, fair experiments, benchmarks, metrics, tools, venues and thesis ideas. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters Doubling the cache made one program 10× faster and another not faster at all. Same chip, same cache. Explaining why is a thesis. Architecture research asks how hardware should be organised for the programs people actually run. You can do serious work with a simulator on a laptop or a low-cost FPGA board. It also shows how to design a rigorous experiment outside the familiar “model and dataset” format: one variable, everything else held fixed, several workloads."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 22 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. The field Methods Data and evaluation Tools and venues Thesis ideas"},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"What architecture and hardware research studies","x":"Section 1 of 5 What architecture and hardware research studies Computer architecture studies how processors, memory and accelerators are organised so that programs run fast and use little energy. Digital hardware design studies the logic circuits they are made of — arithmetic units, controllers, memories — and how they are described in a hardware language, verified and synthesised. Research trades off performance, energy, area, cost and correctness , and most of it is evaluated by simulation or on an FPGA — no chip factory needed. Rest on a branch of the map for a description."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Typical research problems","x":"Section 1 of 5 Typical research problems Area Example thesis question Caches and memory How does L1 data-cache size affect the miss rate of three workloads with different access patterns? Prefetching Does a simple stride prefetcher reduce memory stalls for image-processing kernels? Branch prediction How much does a TAGE-style predictor improve over a 2-bit counter on embedded benchmarks? Accelerators Can a small CNN accelerator on a low-cost FPGA beat a Raspberry Pi in images per joule? RISC-V and open hardware Does a custom instruction for Bangla text processing speed up a RISC-V core enough to justify its area? Low-power design How much energy does clock gating save in a sensor-node processor? Digital logic and arithmetic Can an approximate 8-bit multiplier cut FPGA area by 30% while keeping image quality (PSNR) above 40 dB? Verification and testing Does constrained-random testing find more bugs than directed tests in a RISC-V ALU? Hardware security Can cache-timing leakage be measured on a classroom FPGA design, and does partitioning remove it? How it differs from IoT and networks (chapter 19): those study how devices communicate ; this field studies what is inside the processor and the circuits themselves."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"How architecture research is done","x":"Section 2 of 5 How architecture research is done Simulation gem5 (full system), ChampSim (caches and prefetchers), small trace-driven simulators explore many designs cheaply and repeatably slow, and only as accurate as the model Hardware FPGA prototypes in Verilog or VHDL, or high-level synthesis from C real performance counters on your own CPU ( perf ) convincing numbers for area, frequency and energy; fewer designs tried A strong undergraduate thesis often simulates many configurations, then builds or measures the best one on real hardware."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"The digital hardware design flow","x":"Section 2 of 5 The digital hardware design flow Specify inputs, outputs, timing and the target: FPGA or ASIC Design (RTL) describe the circuit in Verilog or VHDL, or in C with HLS Verify testbenches, a reference model, coverage, assertions Synthesise map to gates or FPGA cells; area and power reports Timing static timing analysis gives the maximum clock frequency Prototype and measure run on the board; measure speed, power, correctness Each step produces evidence for the thesis: the design, the verification results, the area / timing / power reports and the measurements. Verification usually takes more time than design: plan for it. Compare with a baseline circuit (e.g. an exact multiplier, a vendor IP core) synthesised with the same tool, device and settings . State the tool versions, the device and the constraints: synthesis results depend on all three."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Designing a fair simulation experiment","x":"Section 2 of 5 Designing a fair simulation experiment Question How does L1 data-cache size affect miss rate? Baseline a stated configuration, e.g. 32 KB, 2-way, 64-byte blocks, LRU One variable change only the size: 1 to 64 KB Held fixed block size, associativity, replacement, L2, core model Workloads several, with different access patterns; warm the cache up first Put the full configuration in a table: an examiner must be able to rerun it. Use standard benchmark suites , not one program you wrote. Report results per workload as well as the average: the interesting finding is usually why workloads differ. For speed-ups across benchmarks, use the geometric mean ."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Try it: a cache-size experiment","x":"Interactive Try it: a cache-size experiment"},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Key methods to know","x":"Section 2 of 5 Key methods to know Memory hierarchy Caches, the three Cs of misses (compulsory, capacity, conflict), locality. Pipelines and prediction Hazards, out-of-order execution, branch predictors. Performance models CPI and IPC, Amdahl’s law, the roofline model. HDL and HLS Verilog / SystemVerilog, VHDL, high-level synthesis from C/C++. Power and area models McPAT and CACTI estimates; FPGA reports of LUTs, DSPs and power. Verification Testbenches, coverage, assertions, formal checks."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Benchmarks, simulators and boards","x":"Section 3 of 5 Benchmarks, simulators and boards Resource Use SPEC CPU 2017 standard CPU benchmarks (licensed; ask your department) Embench , MiBench free embedded benchmarks PARSEC, Rodinia parallel CPU and GPU workloads gem5 full-system simulator used in most architecture papers ChampSim fast simulator for caches, prefetchers and branch predictors Verilator, Icarus Verilog free HDL simulators FPGA boards (Basys 3, PYNQ-Z2, DE10-Lite) build and measure real hardware RISC-V cores (PicoRV32, VexRiscv) open processors to modify"},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Evaluation","x":"Section 3 of 5 Evaluation Metric Meaning Execution time, speed-up the measure that matters to users IPC / CPI instructions per cycle, or cycles per instruction Miss rate, MPKI cache misses per access, or per thousand instructions Energy, power joules per task; watts Area LUTs, flip-flops, DSPs on an FPGA; mm² on a chip Maximum clock frequency from timing analysis Report energy per task , not only power: a faster design may use more power but less energy. Miss rate alone can mislead: a bigger cache may be slower to access. Simulators are deterministic, but workloads and inputs vary: use several and report each."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Tools","x":"Section 4 of 5 Tools gem5 / ChampSim Architecture simulation. Verilog, Verilator Design and simulate circuits. Vivado / Quartus Synthesise for Xilinx or Intel FPGAs; area, timing and power reports. Yosys, OpenROAD Open-source synthesis and chip layout. perf, PAPI Hardware performance counters on a real CPU. Python + Matplotlib Sweep configurations and plot results by script."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Where this research is published","x":"Section 4 of 5 Where this research is published Type Venues Architecture ISCA, MICRO, HPCA, ASPLOS FPGAs and reconfigurable computing ACM/SIGDA FPGA, FCCM, FPL Design automation DAC, ICCAD, DATE Embedded systems ESWEEK (CASES, CODES+ISSS), LCTES Journals IEEE Transactions on Computers, IEEE Computer Architecture Letters, ACM TACO, IEEE TCAD"},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Thesis ideas","x":"Section 5 of 5 Thesis ideas Starter An approximate multiplier Design exact and approximate 8-bit multipliers in Verilog; compare area, delay and power on an FPGA, and image quality when used in a blur filter. Starter Cache size and workloads Sweep cache size, block size and associativity in a simulator for three benchmark kernels; explain each curve with the three Cs. Starter Branch predictors compared Implement 2-bit, gshare and a perceptron predictor in ChampSim; compare accuracy on embedded benchmarks. Standard A tiny CNN accelerator on an FPGA Build a convolution engine with HLS; compare images per second and per joule with a CPU. Standard A custom RISC-V instruction Add one instruction to an open core; measure speed-up against area and frequency. Ambitious A prefetcher for image kernels Design a prefetcher in ChampSim or gem5 and evaluate it against standard prefetchers."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Getting started in four weeks","x":"Section 5 of 5 Getting started in four weeks Week 1 refresh caches and pipelines (Harris & Harris, Hennessy & Patterson) Week 2 run the gem5 or ChampSim tutorial; write a small cache simulator Week 3 read 5 recent papers (IEEE CAL is short and readable) Week 4 reproduce one published result on a small scale; one-page idea Pitfalls: changing two parameters at once, one workload only, configuration not stated, no warm-up, power reported without energy, simulated numbers presented as if measured on silicon."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 2 Worked example 1 A simulator gives these L1 miss rates (2-way, 64-byte blocks) for cache sizes 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64 KB. Matrix multiply: 42.5, 38.6, 36.6, 4.8, 2.9, 2.9, 2.9%. Streaming sum: 12.5% at every size. Linked list: 49.5, 48.7, 47.1, 43.6, 35.3, 6.3, 6.3%. Explain the results. Show the solution Each workload has a different working set. Matrix multiply re-reads matrix B (8 KB) for every row of A: once B fits, at 8 KB, capacity misses almost disappear. The stream reads 512 KB once per pass, which never fits, so every block is a compulsory or capacity miss: one miss per 64-byte block of 8-byte values = 1 ÷ 8 = 12.5%, whatever the size. The list’s nodes occupy 32 KB in random order, so it improves slowly and then sharply when they all fit; 6.3% is left over from the first pass. Conclusion for the thesis: “a larger L1 helps only when the working set fits; streaming code needs prefetching instead” — and report each workload, because the average hides all of this."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 2 Worked example 2 Your FPGA accelerator makes the convolution part of a program 10× faster. Convolution was 40% of the run time. What is the overall speed-up? Show the solution Amdahl’s law: new time = 0.60 + 0.40 ÷ 10 = 0.64 of the old time, so speed-up = 1 ÷ 0.64 ≈ 1.56×. Even an infinitely fast accelerator gives at most 1 ÷ 0.60 ≈ 1.67×. Report the end-to-end speed-up, not only the kernel’s."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Name the three Cs of cache misses. Show the answer Compulsory (first access to a block), capacity (the cache is too small for the working set) and conflict (blocks map to the same set). 2. Simulation or FPGA: one advantage of each. Show the answer Simulation: many configurations quickly and repeatably. FPGA: real area, frequency and energy numbers. 3. In a cache-size experiment, what must be held fixed? Show the answer Block size, associativity, replacement policy, the rest of the memory hierarchy, the core model, the workloads and their inputs — everything except the size."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. Why report energy per task and not only power? Show the answer A faster design can draw more power yet finish sooner and use less energy overall. 5. List the steps of the digital hardware design flow. Show the answer Specify, design at register-transfer level (Verilog/VHDL or HLS), verify with testbenches, synthesise, run timing analysis, then prototype and measure on the board. 6. Why use the geometric mean for speed-ups? Show the answer Speed-ups are ratios; the geometric mean treats a 2× gain and a 2× loss symmetrically and does not depend on which design is the reference."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Tool gem5 simulator ↗ Documentation and the Learning gem5 tutorial. Tool ChampSim ↗ Trace-based simulator for caches, prefetchers and branch predictors. Benchmark Embench ↗ Free, modern benchmark suite for embedded processors. Tool Verilator ↗ Fast open-source Verilog simulator. Book Computer Architecture: A Quantitative Approach — Hennessy & Patterson Morgan Kaufmann, 6th ed. The reference for architecture research methods. Book Digital Design and Computer Architecture, RISC-V Edition — Harris & Harris Morgan Kaufmann, 2021. From logic gates to a RISC-V processor."},{"u":"topics/22-computer-architecture-and-hardware-design.html#s=23","w":"Chapter 22 · Computer Architecture and Digital Hardware Design","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary Architecture and hardware research balances performance, energy, area, cost and correctness; it studies what is inside the processor, not how devices communicate. Hardware work follows a design flow: specify, design (RTL), verify, synthesise, analyse timing, prototype and measure. Most undergraduate work uses simulators (gem5, ChampSim) or FPGA boards. Design experiments like a scientist: a stated baseline, one variable changed, everything else fixed, several workloads. Report per-workload results, energy per task and the full configuration; use geometric means for speed-ups. Explain results with models: the three Cs, Amdahl’s law, the roofline. Venues: ISCA, MICRO, HPCA, ASPLOS, FPGA, FCCM, DAC. Next: Qualitative and Mixed Methods More practice All resources All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Qualitative and Mixed Methods","x":"23 Chapter 23 of 24 · Part C · Research skills Qualitative and Mixed Methods When to use qualitative research, interviews, focus groups, observation and diary studies, sampling and saturation, interview guides, transcription, thematic analysis, rigour, surveys, mixed-methods designs and reporting findings. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters Your app’s logs show that 70% of farmers stopped using it after a week. You have the number. You have no idea why. Twelve conversations would tell you. Many good theses ask questions about people: why they adopt or reject technology, how they work, what they need. Answering them well takes methods as rigorous as any experiment — just different ones. This chapter covers interviews, observation and surveys, thematic analysis and how to combine numbers and words in a mixed-methods thesis."},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 23 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. Why qualitative Collecting data Analysing data Surveys and mixed methods"},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"When numbers are not enough","x":"Section 1 of 4 When numbers are not enough Qualitative research studies experiences, reasons and practices through words, observations and artefacts. It answers how and why questions that a metric cannot: why developers skip tests, how farmers use a weather app, what makes students distrust an AI tutor. In computer science it is central to HCI, software engineering, ICTD, security (usable security) and computing education — and useful in any thesis that builds something for people. Approach Answers Typical data Quantitative how much, how many, which is better metrics, measurements, closed survey answers Qualitative how, why, what is it like interviews, observation notes, open answers, documents Mixed methods both, with each explaining the other a survey plus interviews, an experiment plus observation"},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Choosing a method","x":"Section 1 of 4 Choosing a method Method Use it when you want… Typical size Semi-structured interviews people’s experiences and reasons, in their own words 8–20 people Focus groups shared views and the discussion between people 3–5 groups of 4–8 Observation / contextual inquiry what people actually do, not what they say they do several sessions Diary study behaviour over days or weeks 10–20 people, 1–4 weeks Open survey questions many short explanations alongside closed questions as many as answer Document and artefact analysis code reviews, forum posts, app reviews, policies a defined sample Choose the method from the research question, not from what is easiest. “How do…?” and “Why do…?” questions about people usually need interviews or observation."},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Participants and sampling","x":"Section 2 of 4 Participants and sampling Purposive sampling : choose people who have the experience you study (e.g. students who used AI assistants this term). Aim for variety : years, genders, backgrounds, skill levels. Stop when new interviews add few new ideas ( saturation ); for a focused question this is often 9–15 interviews. Describe every participant in a table: P1…P12, role, experience — never names. Before you recruit ethics approval or your department’s process (chapter 9) an information sheet and consent form a plan for storing recordings safely a short pilot interview"},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Writing an interview guide","x":"Section 2 of 4 Writing an interview guide Warm-up background, easy questions Main questions 4–6 open questions on the research question Probes “Can you give an example?” “What happened next?” Close “Is there anything I should have asked?” Instead of… ask… Do you find the app useful? Tell me about the last time you used the app. Don’t you think the forum is confusing? How do you find information on the forum? Why and how often do you use Git? How do you use Git? (then, separately) How often?"},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Interview or not? Judge the questions","x":"Interactive Interview or not? Judge the questions"},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Recording, transcribing and translating","x":"Section 2 of 4 Recording, transcribing and translating Record audio with consent; keep files encrypted and off shared drives. Transcribe verbatim ; remove names, places and other identifying details as you go. Interviews in Bangla: analyse in Bangla where possible; translate only the quotes you report, and say who translated them. Automatic transcription (e.g. Whisper) saves time, but check every line — and never upload sensitive recordings to an online service without consent. Keep a research diary : what you noticed after each interview, and how your ideas changed."},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Thematic analysis in six phases","x":"Section 3 of 4 Thematic analysis in six phases 1. Familiarise read every transcript; note first ideas 2. Code label meaningful segments with short codes 3. Generate themes group codes into candidate themes 4. Review check themes against the codes and the whole data 5. Define and name write what each theme is and is not 6. Write up tell the story with quotes After Braun and Clarke (2006), the most widely used approach for an undergraduate thesis. A free tool such as Taguette or QualCoder, or a spreadsheet, is enough."},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"From quotes to codes to themes","x":"Section 3 of 4 From quotes to codes to themes Quote (anonymised) Codes Theme “I ask ChatGPT only after I’ve been stuck for an hour.” (P3) AI as last resort; time pressure Rules students set for themselves “If the code runs, I submit it. I don’t always know why it works.” (P7) working code over understanding Understanding traded for speed “My teacher never said what is allowed, so I just don’t mention it.” (P11) unclear rules; hiding use Uncertainty about what is allowed “I rewrite its answer in my own way so I can explain it in the viva.” (P2) rewriting; preparing to explain Rules students set for themselves A theme is a pattern of meaning across participants, not a topic heading. “AI” is a topic; “students set their own rules because the official ones are unclear” is a theme."},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Making qualitative work rigorous","x":"Section 3 of 4 Making qualitative work rigorous Audit trail Keep the guide, codebook, memos and every version of the themes. Reflexivity Write down how your own position (a fellow student, a TA) may shape what people tell you. Second coder A peer codes some transcripts; discuss disagreements. Some report Cohen’s κ, others prefer discussion — say which and why. Negative cases Look for participants who do not fit a theme and report them. Thick description Describe the setting and participants so readers can judge where the findings apply. Member checking Show a summary to some participants and ask whether it is fair."},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Surveys that measure something","x":"Section 4 of 4 Surveys that measure something Use validated scales where they exist (SUS, NASA-TLX, UMUX); cite them and do not change the wording. One idea per question; neutral wording; a balanced Likert scale (e.g. 1–5). Pilot with 3–5 people; time it; fix unclear questions. Report the response rate and who responded — a convenience sample of friends cannot speak for “Bangladeshi students”. Analysing Likert answers Single Likert items are ordinal: report medians and distributions (stacked bars), and compare groups with Mann–Whitney or Wilcoxon tests. Multi-item validated scales such as SUS can be scored and averaged as their authors specify."},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Mixed-methods designs","x":"Section 4 of 4 Mixed-methods designs Design Order Example Explanatory sequential numbers first, then interviews to explain them Log data shows 60% never open the LMS forum; interviews find out why. Exploratory sequential interviews first, then a survey to measure how common the themes are Interviews with 12 students produce themes; a survey of 300 counts them. Convergent both at once, compared at the end A usability test records task times and think-aloud comments together. Say in the methodology which design you use and where the two strands are combined — mixing is more than having both a survey and interviews."},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Reporting qualitative findings","x":"Section 4 of 4 Reporting qualitative findings One section per theme: define it, explain it, support it with 2–3 short quotes from different participants. Attribute quotes to IDs (P4), never names. Use counts with care (“most participants”, “7 of 12”) — the aim is understanding, not percentages. Discuss transferability : to which people and settings the findings may apply. Put the interview guide and the codebook in an appendix. A findings sentence “Students set their own rules for using AI assistants because the official rules were unclear. Seven of twelve described a personal rule, such as asking only after being stuck for an hour (P3) or rewriting every answer so they could explain it (P2).”"},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 2 Worked example 1 Three excerpts from interviews with developers at local start-ups: (a) “We have no time for tests; the client wants features yesterday.” (b) “Tests? Nobody here knows how to write good ones.” (c) “We tried, but the tests broke every week, so we stopped.” Code them and suggest a theme. Show the solution Codes: (a) deadline pressure, client priorities; (b) lack of testing skills; (c) brittle tests, abandoned practice. Possible themes: “Testing loses to delivery pressure” (a), and “Testing is attempted but not sustained without skills” (b, c). Check each theme against all transcripts, look for developers who do test, and give each theme a definition before writing it up."},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 2 Worked example 2 Research question: “Why do students rarely use the university LMS discussion forum?” Design a mixed-methods study. Show the solution Explanatory sequential design. Strand 1 (quantitative): LMS logs for one term — share of students who read or post, by year and course (with permission and anonymised). Strand 2 (qualitative): semi-structured interviews with 12–15 students chosen to include readers, posters and non-users; thematic analysis. Integration: use the log patterns to choose interviewees, and use the themes to explain the patterns. Ethics: approval, consent, anonymised data. Output: the reasons, how common the usage patterns are, and design recommendations."},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. Give two research questions that need qualitative methods and one that needs quantitative methods. Show the answer Qualitative: “Why do developers in local start-ups skip unit testing?”, “How do visually impaired students use smartphones for study?” Quantitative: “Does dark mode reduce reading speed on a news app?” 2. What is saturation? Show the answer The point at which new interviews add few or no new codes or themes, so more data is unlikely to change the findings. 3. Rewrite the leading question “Don’t you think the app is hard to use?” Show the answer “Tell me about the last time you used the app.” followed by probes such as “What was difficult, if anything?”"},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. What is the difference between a code and a theme? Show the answer A code labels one meaningful segment of data; a theme is a pattern of shared meaning across many codes and participants that answers the research question. 5. Name three ways to make qualitative analysis rigorous. Show the answer An audit trail; reflexive notes; a second coder on part of the data; reporting negative cases; thick description; member checking. 6. In an exploratory sequential design, which comes first? Show the answer The qualitative strand (e.g. interviews); its themes then shape a quantitative instrument (e.g. a survey) that measures how common they are."},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"For your next supervisor meeting","x":"Bring this to your supervisor For your next supervisor meeting Bring this A one-page plan for a qualitative or mixed-methods strand: the research question, the method, who you will recruit and how many, three main interview or survey questions, and how you will analyse the data. See an acceptable example RQ: Why do students rarely post in the LMS forum? Method: semi-structured interviews (explanatory sequential, after LMS log analysis). Participants: 12–15 students, chosen to include regular posters, readers only and non-users, across years. Main questions: “Tell me about the last time you had a question about a course.” “How do you decide where to ask it?” “What happened the last time you read or posted on the forum?” Analysis: thematic analysis in Taguette; a second coder on 3 transcripts. Ethics: consent form and anonymised transcripts. Milestone checklist"},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Paper Using Thematic Analysis in Psychology — V. Braun and V. Clarke ↗ Qualitative Research in Psychology, 2006. The six-phase method. Paper How Many Interviews Are Enough? — G. Guest, A. Bunce and L. Johnson ↗ Field Methods, 2006. An early study of saturation. Paper Qualitative Methods in Empirical Studies of Software Engineering — C. Seaman ↗ IEEE TSE, 1999. Qualitative methods for SE research. Tool Taguette ↗ Free, open-source tool for coding interview transcripts."},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=23","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Further learning (cont.)","x":"Resources Further learning (cont.) Book Research Methods in Human–Computer Interaction — Lazar, Feng and Hochheiser Morgan Kaufmann, 2nd ed. Interviews, surveys, diaries and analysis. Book Designing and Conducting Mixed Methods Research — Creswell and Plano Clark SAGE, 3rd ed. The standard guide to mixed-methods designs."},{"u":"topics/23-qualitative-and-mixed-methods.html#s=24","w":"Chapter 23 · Qualitative and Mixed Methods","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary Qualitative research answers how and why questions with words, observations and artefacts. Choose the method from the question: interviews, focus groups, observation, diaries or documents. Sample purposively, aim for variety, stop near saturation; get consent and protect recordings. Use open, neutral interview questions with probes; pilot the guide. Analyse with thematic analysis and make it rigorous: audit trail, reflexivity, second coder, negative cases. Combine numbers and words with a named mixed-methods design, and report themes with quotes. Next: Managing Your Thesis Year More practice All resources Milestone checklist All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=1","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Managing Your Thesis Year","x":"24 Chapter 24 of 24 · Part C · Research skills Managing Your Thesis Year Why theses run late and how to plan backwards, weekly habits, meetings and difficult supervision, group theses, debugging failed experiments, reporting negative results, deciding when to change direction, and looking after yourself. Start All chapters"},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=2","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Why this matters","x":"Before we start Why this matters Week 30. The method still loses to the baseline, your supervisor is at a conference, your group partner is quiet, and the deadline has not moved. Every thesis has a week like this. The research chapters teach what to do. This one is about getting it done: planning time, working with a supervisor and a group, and handling the experiments that fail. None of it is a sign that you are doing badly. It is what research is like — and there are good ways through it."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=3","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"In this chapter","x":"Outline · chapter 24 In this chapter Select a section to jump to it. Planning your time Working with your supervisor When things go wrong Looking after yourself"},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=4","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Why theses run late","x":"Section 1 of 4 Why theses run late People underestimate how long their own tasks take, even when they know past tasks ran late — the planning fallacy . Data collection, cleaning and debugging take two to three times longer than planned. The last 10% (writing, figures, formatting, references) takes far more than 10% of the time. Waiting — for data access, ethics approval, supervisor feedback — is invisible in most plans. Plan for it: add a 20–30% buffer to every phase, and start the tasks that involve waiting for other people first."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=5","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Plan backwards from the deadline","x":"Section 1 of 4 Plan backwards from the deadline Fix the dates submission, defence, department deadlines Work back final draft → full draft → results → experiments → data Add buffers 2–3 weeks before submission Weekly goals one concrete output per week Review monthly with your supervisor; re-plan honestly Define a minimum thesis Write down the smallest result that still answers your research question — e.g. one baseline, your method and one ablation on one dataset. Finish that first; everything else is a bonus. Use the planner in chapter 1 and the milestones ."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=6","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"A working week","x":"Section 1 of 4 A working week Protected blocks Two or three blocks of 2–3 hours a week for thesis work only, with the phone away. Write every week Even half a page: method notes, a results paragraph. Writing reveals gaps early. A weekly log Three lines: done, blocked, next. It becomes your meeting agenda. Small first steps When stuck, choose a 20-minute task: plot one result, read one section."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=7","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Meetings that work","x":"Section 2 of 4 Meetings that work Send a short agenda a day before: what you did, results (with a figure), questions, what you plan next. Bring decisions to make , not only updates: “Should I add dataset B or do error analysis?” Write the agreed actions at the end and email them the same day. Keep a meeting log : date, decisions, actions, deadlines. An agenda email Subject: Thesis meeting Tuesday — agenda 1. Done: baseline F1 0.68 ± 0.01 (figure attached). 2. Problem: BanglaBERT runs out of GPU memory at batch size 32. 3. Decision needed: smaller batch, or a smaller model? 4. Next week: ablation without augmentation."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=8","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"When the supervision relationship is hard","x":"Section 2 of 4 When the supervision relationship is hard Situation What usually helps No reply for weeks one short email with a single question and a proposed time; then ask in person after class or office hours Conflicting advice from two supervisors write both options down and ask them to decide together, in one meeting or one email thread You disagree with the direction bring evidence (a pilot result, two papers) and propose an alternative; accept the decision or ask why Feedback feels harsh separate the content from the tone; ask “what would a good version look like?” Real problems persist talk to the thesis coordinator or head of department early, calmly and with your meeting log Most supervisors supervise many students. Clear, short, prepared communication is the single most effective thing you control."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=9","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Thesis in a group","x":"Section 2 of 4 Thesis in a group Many departments run theses in groups of two or three: agree who does what in writing at the start. One shared Git repository, one shared reference library, one shared document. Meet as a group weekly; rotate who writes the agenda. If a member stops contributing, raise it with them first, then with the supervisor — early, not at the defence. In the thesis, state each member’s contribution if your department asks for it."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=10","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"When experiments fail","x":"Section 3 of 4 When experiments fail Can the model overfit 20 examples ? If not, the pipeline has a bug. Do you reproduce a known result (a baseline from a paper) with your code? Look at the data : labels, shapes, a few raw examples after every step. Change one thing at a time and log it. Ask: is the task actually learnable from this data? Debugging is part of research. Record what you tried — failed attempts often become a useful paragraph in the methodology or the limitations."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=11","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Negative results are results","x":"Section 3 of 4 Negative results are results Your method loses to the baseline. The thesis has not failed: your research question asked whether it would help, and the answer is “no” — now explain why. Analyse where it loses: classes, data sizes, input lengths. Find conditions where it helps, if any (small data? noisy labels?). Check it was a fair fight: same tuning budget, no bugs, strong baseline. Report it honestly; the examiners will judge the quality of the investigation. Writing it up “Contrary to our hypothesis, continued pre-training did not improve macro-F1 (−0.4 points, 95% CI −1.3 to 0.5). Error analysis suggests the unlabelled corpus was too formal: gains appeared only on comments written in Bangla script.” Negative results are welcomed by workshops such as “I Can’t Believe It’s Not Better!”."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=12","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Helpful or not? Judge the responses","x":"Interactive Helpful or not? Judge the responses"},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=13","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Persist or change direction?","x":"Section 3 of 4 Persist or change direction? Signal Usually means Data you need will not arrive in time change the data or the question now — waiting rarely helps The method works, but less than hoped persist: analyse, ablate, report A paper appears that did your idea not the end: different data, a local setting, a deeper analysis or a replication are still contributions Nothing learnable after the sanity checks discuss a pivot with your supervisor; keep what you built Time already spent is not a reason to continue (the sunk-cost trap). Decide on what the remaining months can still achieve."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=14","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Looking after yourself","x":"Section 4 of 4 Looking after yourself A thesis is long: sleep, exercise and time with people keep your thinking sharp. Talk to peers in the same stage; a weekly writing group helps many students. Stuck and anxious for weeks is common — and a reason to talk to your supervisor early, not to hide. Your university’s student counselling service is there for exactly this. The thesis is one piece of work. It does not measure your worth."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=15","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Worked example 1","x":"Worked example 1 of 2 Worked example 1 Six weeks to submission. Left: two experiments (one week each), error analysis (one week), and writing the methodology, results and discussion chapters (about three weeks). What do you do? Show the solution Seven weeks of work in six, with no buffer. Agree a minimum thesis with your supervisor: keep the experiment that answers the main research question, drop or shorten the other (mention it as future work). Write the methodology now, while the experiment runs. Do a smaller error analysis (50 errors). Plan: weeks 1–2 experiment + methodology; week 3 error analysis + results; weeks 4–5 discussion, introduction, abstract; week 6 buffer and proofreading."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=16","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Worked example 2","x":"Worked example 2 of 2 Worked example 2 Your method scores 0.776 ± 0.010 macro-F1; the baseline 0.791 ± 0.008 (5 seeds). How do you present this in the results and discussion? Show the solution Report it directly: the method is 1.5 points worse, consistently over seeds. Then analyse: per-class results, performance by input length or domain, and whether any subset benefits. Check fairness: the same tuning budget, a bug-free pipeline (both reproduce published numbers?). Discussion: answer the research question (“No — under these conditions it does not help”), give the most likely reasons with evidence, and suggest what would test them. This is a complete, honest thesis."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=17","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Practice (1 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (1 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 1. What is the planning fallacy, and how do you plan around it? Show the answer The tendency to underestimate how long your own tasks will take. Plan backwards from the deadline, add 20–30% buffers, start tasks that depend on others first, and re-plan monthly. 2. What is a minimum thesis? Show the answer The smallest result that still answers the research question, finished first so the rest is a bonus. 3. What should an agenda for a supervisor meeting contain? Show the answer What you did, results (ideally a figure), problems, the decisions you need, and the plan for next week."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=18","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Practice (2 of 2)","x":"Practice Practice (2 of 2) Try each one yourself first, then check the answer. 4. Your model does not learn. Give three checks. Show the answer Overfit a tiny subset; reproduce a known baseline result; inspect the data and labels after each step; change one thing at a time. 5. Why is a well-analysed negative result still a thesis? Show the answer It answers the research question with evidence and explains why; the examiners judge the quality of the investigation, not whether the hypothesis was right. 6. A paper with your idea appears in month 5. What can you still contribute? Show the answer Different or local data, a different setting or language, a deeper analysis, a replication, or a comparison with that paper."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=19","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Quiz","x":"Check yourself Quiz"},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=20","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"For your next supervisor meeting","x":"Bring this to your supervisor For your next supervisor meeting Bring this Your plan for the rest of the year: the fixed deadlines, a backwards plan with buffers, your minimum thesis in two sentences, and the next three weekly goals. See an acceptable example Deadlines: proposal defence 15 Feb, progress 10 May, submission 20 Aug, defence early Sep. Minimum thesis: BanglaBERT with and without continued pre-training on the 5,000-comment dataset, with one ablation and error analysis. Backwards plan: writing from 1 Jul (with a 2-week buffer), experiments done by 30 Jun, data and baselines by 15 Apr. Next three weeks: finish labelling guide; label 500 comments with a second annotator; run the TF-IDF baseline end to end. Milestone checklist"},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=21","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Further learning","x":"Resources Further learning Hand-picked material to go deeper. Links open in a new tab. Paper Exploring the “Planning Fallacy” — R. Buehler, D. Griffin and M. Ross ↗ Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 1994. Why we underestimate our own tasks. Blog The Thesis Whisperer ↗ Practical advice on supervision, writing and wellbeing during research degrees. Workshop I Can’t Believe It’s Not Better! ↗ A workshop series for negative and unexpected results in machine learning. Book Deep Work — Cal Newport Grand Central, 2016. Protecting time for focused work. Book Writing Your Dissertation in Fifteen Minutes a Day — Joan Bolker Holt, 1998. Building a daily writing habit."},{"u":"topics/24-managing-your-thesis.html#s=22","w":"Chapter 24 · Managing Your Thesis Year","t":"Summary","x":"Summary Summary Theses run late for predictable reasons: plan backwards, add buffers, start tasks that need other people first. Define a minimum thesis and finish it first; write every week. Prepare meetings: an agenda, results, decisions needed; record actions in a meeting log. Handle supervision and group problems early, calmly and in writing. Debug failed experiments systematically; a well-analysed negative result is still a thesis. Look after yourself and use the people and services around you. Thesis milestones More practice All resources Milestone checklist All chapters Found a mistake or something unclear? Send feedback ."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-ablation-study","w":"Glossary","t":"Ablation study","x":"Removing or replacing one component of a method at a time and measuring the change, to show which part gives the improvement."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-abstract","w":"Glossary","t":"Abstract","x":"A 150–300-word summary of the whole thesis: context, problem, approach, results with numbers, and conclusion. Written last."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-amdahl-s-law","w":"Glossary","t":"Amdahl’s law","x":"The overall speed-up from making part of a program faster is limited by the part that is not sped up."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-audit-trail","w":"Glossary","t":"Audit trail","x":"The record of how qualitative analysis developed: interview guide, codebook, memos and every version of the themes."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-baseline","w":"Glossary","t":"Baseline","x":"A simple or established method your proposed method is compared with. A fair baseline gets the same data and tuning budget."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-boolean-search","w":"Glossary","t":"Boolean search","x":"A search string that combines terms with AND, OR and NOT, with quotation marks for phrases."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-bootstrap-confidence-interval","w":"Glossary","t":"Bootstrap confidence interval","x":"An interval found by resampling the test items with replacement many times and recomputing the result each time; the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles give a 95% interval."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-cache-miss","w":"Glossary","t":"Cache miss","x":"An access to data that is not in the cache. Misses are compulsory (first access), capacity (cache too small) or conflict (blocks compete for one set)."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-code-qualitative","w":"Glossary","t":"Code (qualitative)","x":"A short label attached to a meaningful segment of interview or text data."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-cohen-s-d","w":"Glossary","t":"Cohen’s d","x":"An effect size: the difference between two means divided by a standard deviation."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-cohen-s-kappa","w":"Glossary","t":"Cohen’s κ (kappa)","x":"Agreement between two annotators, corrected for the agreement expected by chance. Above about 0.6 is usually called substantial."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-confidence-interval","w":"Glossary","t":"Confidence interval","x":"The range of values for a result (such as a difference between two methods) that is compatible with the data, at a stated level such as 95%."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-confusion-matrix","w":"Glossary","t":"Confusion matrix","x":"A table of true positives, false positives, false negatives and true negatives, from which precision, recall and other metrics are computed."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-contribution","w":"Glossary","t":"Contribution","x":"What your thesis adds that did not exist before: a new method, dataset, tool, empirical finding, replication or analysis."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-core-ranking","w":"Glossary","t":"CORE ranking","x":"A ranking of computer science conferences into A*, A, B and C."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-corrected-resampled-t-test","w":"Glossary","t":"Corrected resampled t-test","x":"A version of the paired t-test for cross-validation folds (Nadeau and Bengio) that allows for the training data the folds share."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-cross-validation-k-fold","w":"Glossary","t":"Cross-validation (k-fold)","x":"Splitting data into k parts and testing on each part once while training on the rest; every item is tested exactly once."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-data-leakage","w":"Glossary","t":"Data leakage","x":"Information from the test data reaching the model during training or tuning, which makes results look better than they are."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-effect-size","w":"Glossary","t":"Effect size","x":"How large a difference is, in the metric’s units (“+2 F1 points”) or in standardised form (Cohen’s d)."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-error-analysis","w":"Glossary","t":"Error analysis","x":"Studying a sample of a model’s mistakes, grouping them by cause and counting each group, to explain why the method fails."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-external-validity","w":"Glossary","t":"External validity","x":"Whether results hold beyond the data, people and settings that were studied."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-fabrication-and-falsification","w":"Glossary","t":"Fabrication and falsification","x":"Inventing data or results (fabrication); changing, selecting or hiding data or results (falsification). Both are research misconduct."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-finer","w":"Glossary","t":"FINER","x":"A check for research questions: Feasible, Interesting, Novel, Ethical, Relevant."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-gap","w":"Glossary","t":"Gap","x":"What existing research has not done, which your thesis addresses. The literature review ends by stating it."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-hypothesis","w":"Glossary","t":"Hypothesis","x":"A testable prediction, for example “continued pre-training improves macro-F1 on code-mixed comments”."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-inclusion-and-exclusion-criteria","w":"Glossary","t":"Inclusion and exclusion criteria","x":"Written rules for which papers (or participants) are included in a review (or study)."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-informed-consent","w":"Glossary","t":"Informed consent","x":"A participant’s voluntary agreement to take part after being told the purpose, procedure, risks and the right to stop."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-internal-validity","w":"Glossary","t":"Internal validity","x":"Whether an observed effect is really caused by what you changed, and not by something else."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-literature-matrix","w":"Glossary","t":"Literature matrix","x":"A table with one row per paper and columns such as problem, data, method, results and limitations."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-macro-f1","w":"Glossary","t":"Macro-F1","x":"The F1 score computed for each class and then averaged, so that small classes count as much as large ones."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-mcnemar-s-test","w":"Glossary","t":"McNemar’s test","x":"A test for two classifiers on the same test set, based on the items on which they disagree."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-mixed-methods","w":"Glossary","t":"Mixed methods","x":"A study design that combines quantitative and qualitative data, such as a survey followed by interviews, and integrates the results."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-multiple-comparisons","w":"Glossary","t":"Multiple comparisons","x":"Running many tests raises the chance of a false positive; corrections such as Holm–Bonferroni adjust for it."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-negative-result","w":"Glossary","t":"Negative result","x":"A finding that a method does not help or a hypothesis does not hold. Well analysed, it is a valid answer to a research question."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-p-value","w":"Glossary","t":"p-value","x":"If there were really no difference, the probability of seeing a difference at least as large as the one observed. It is not the probability that a hypothesis is true."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-paired-t-test","w":"Glossary","t":"Paired t-test","x":"A test of whether the mean difference between paired results (same seeds, same items) is zero. Assumes independent pairs and roughly normal differences."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-peer-review","w":"Glossary","t":"Peer review","x":"Evaluation of a paper by independent experts before it is published."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-plagiarism","w":"Glossary","t":"Plagiarism","x":"Presenting someone else’s words, ideas, code or figures as your own without citation."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-planning-fallacy","w":"Glossary","t":"Planning fallacy","x":"The tendency to underestimate how long your own tasks will take."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-precision-and-recall","w":"Glossary","t":"Precision and recall","x":"Precision: the share of predicted positives that are correct. Recall: the share of real positives that are found."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-predatory-venue","w":"Glossary","t":"Predatory venue","x":"A journal or conference that charges authors but does no real peer review."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-prisma","w":"Glossary","t":"PRISMA","x":"A standard flow diagram reporting how many papers were found, screened, read in full and included."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-purposive-sampling","w":"Glossary","t":"Purposive sampling","x":"Choosing participants because they have the experience the study is about."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-reproducibility","w":"Glossary","t":"Reproducibility","x":"Whether someone else can obtain your results again from your code, data and description."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-research-question-rq","w":"Glossary","t":"Research question (RQ)","x":"The precise question a thesis answers with evidence."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-saturation","w":"Glossary","t":"Saturation","x":"The point in interviews at which new data adds few or no new ideas."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-seed-random","w":"Glossary","t":"Seed (random)","x":"The number that fixes a run’s random choices (initialisation, data order). Several seeds show how much results vary between runs."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-snowballing","w":"Glossary","t":"Snowballing","x":"Finding papers through the references of a paper (backward) and the papers that cite it (forward)."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-stratified-split","w":"Glossary","t":"Stratified split","x":"A split that keeps the same class proportions in train, validation and test."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-systematic-review","w":"Glossary","t":"Systematic review","x":"A literature review that follows a written, repeatable search and selection protocol."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-thematic-analysis","w":"Glossary","t":"Thematic analysis","x":"A method for finding patterns of meaning (themes) in qualitative data, usually in six phases."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-threats-to-validity","w":"Glossary","t":"Threats to validity","x":"Reasons your conclusions might be wrong, and what you did to reduce them."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-three-pass-reading","w":"Glossary","t":"Three-pass reading","x":"Reading a paper in three passes of increasing depth: overview, content, then full detail."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-train-validation-and-test-sets","w":"Glossary","t":"Train, validation and test sets","x":"Data to fit the model, to tune and choose models, and to report the final result once."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-viva","w":"Glossary","t":"Viva","x":"The oral examination in which examiners question you about your thesis."},{"u":"glossary.html#g-wilcoxon-signed-rank-test","w":"Glossary","t":"Wilcoxon signed-rank test","x":"A paired test that uses ranks instead of values. With 5 pairs it can never reach p < 0.05; use at least 6."},{"u":"reference.html","w":"Quick reference","t":"Research question templates","x":"Effect: Does X improve Y for Z compared with W ? Comparison: Which of A , B or C performs best for task on data , measured by metric ? Design: How can we build a system that does T under constraint C ? Explanation: Why does method M fail on case K ? Exploration: How do users U currently do task T , and what problems do they face? Check it with FINER : Feasible, Interesting, Novel, Ethical, Relevant."},{"u":"reference.html","w":"Quick reference","t":"From topic to objectives","x":"Area → topic → problem → research question(s) → hypothesis → objectives → contributions. Each step narrows the one before it."},{"u":"reference.html","w":"Quick reference","t":"Boolean search cheat sheet","x":"Write Means AND both terms (narrower) OR either term: use for synonyms (wider) NOT / - exclude a term \"exact phrase\" words in this order ( … ) group synonyms: (Bangla OR Bengali) * truncation in most databases: classif* intitle: Google Scholar: term in the title author: Google Scholar: by an author"},{"u":"reference.html","w":"Quick reference","t":"Three-pass reading","x":"Pass 1 (5–10 min): title, abstract, introduction, headings, conclusion, figures. Decide: read on or drop? Pass 2 (about 1 hour): figures, tables and the method; mark unknown references. Summarise in your own words. Pass 3 (hours): re-derive or re-implement; question every assumption; list strengths, weaknesses and ideas."},{"u":"reference.html","w":"Quick reference","t":"Questions to ask of every paper","x":"What problem, and why does it matter? What is new compared with earlier work? What data, method, baselines and metrics? Do the results support the claims? Any statistics? What are the limitations and threats to validity? What would I do next?"},{"u":"reference.html","w":"Quick reference","t":"Venue quality signals","x":"Signal Where to check Conference rank A*/A/B/C CORE portal Journal quartile Q1–Q4 SCImago (SJR), Scopus, Web of Science Peer review and editorial board the venue’s website Indexed in Scopus / Web of Science / DBLP the index itself Warning signs fast “guaranteed” acceptance, fees before review, spam emails, fake impact factors"},{"u":"reference.html","w":"Quick reference","t":"Thesis structure","x":"Front matter: title, declaration, approval, acknowledgements, abstract, contents, lists of figures and tables Introduction Literature review Methodology Results and discussion Conclusion and future work References Appendices"},{"u":"reference.html","w":"Quick reference","t":"Abstract in five sentences","x":"Context: the field and why it matters. Problem: the gap or difficulty. Approach: what you did. Results: the main numbers. Conclusion: what it means."},{"u":"reference.html","w":"Quick reference","t":"Classification metrics","x":"Metric Formula Accuracy (TP + TN) ÷ all Precision TP ÷ (TP + FP) Recall (sensitivity) TP ÷ (TP + FN) Specificity TN ÷ (TN + FP) F1 2 · P · R ÷ (P + R) Macro-F1 mean of per-class F1"},{"u":"reference.html","w":"Quick reference","t":"Choosing a statistical test","x":"What one pair or group is Test Two methods, seeds on one fixed split paired t-test; Wilcoxon signed-rank (6+ pairs) if far from normal — covers training randomness only Two methods, folds of one (repeated) cross-validation corrected resampled t-test (Nadeau–Bengio) or 5×2 cv test — not the ordinary paired t-test Two classifiers, one test set McNemar’s test; bootstrap confidence interval over test items Several methods on several datasets Friedman test + post-hoc (Nemenyi or Holm) Two independent groups of people Welch’s t-test; Mann–Whitney U Same people, two conditions paired t-test; Wilcoxon signed-rank Counts in categories chi-square test; Fisher’s exact test for small counts p is the probability of a difference at least this large if there were really no difference . Always report the effect size and a 95% confidence interval with it; correct for many comparisons (Holm)."},{"u":"reference.html","w":"Quick reference","t":"Email to a prospective supervisor","x":"Subject: Thesis supervision request — topic Dear Name , I am name, ID, programme . I am interested in area , and I read your paper title . I would like to work on one-line idea . I have done relevant course / project . Could I meet you this week to discuss it? I attach a one-page summary. Regards, name"},{"u":"reference.html","w":"Quick reference","t":"Weekly progress report","x":"Done this week (with evidence: numbers, plots, pages) Problems and what I tried Plan for next week Questions for my supervisor"},{"u":"reference.html","w":"Quick reference","t":"IEEE reference format","x":"[1] A. Author, B. Author, and C. Author, “Title of paper,” in Proc. Conference Name (ABBR) , City, Country, 2024, pp. 1–10, doi: 10.xxxx/xxxxx. [2] A. Author and B. Author, “Title of article,” Journal Name , vol. 12, no. 3, pp. 45–67, 2023."},{"u":"worked-thesis.html#stage1","w":"Worked thesis","t":"From an idea to a research question","x":"“I want to do sentiment analysis of Bangla using deep learning.” “Which Bangla? Formal news text is well studied. What is new here, what data will you use, and how will you know you succeeded? Bring me a question, not an area.” “Does continued pre-training of BanglaBERT on unlabelled code-mixed comments improve sentiment classification of Banglish Facebook comments, compared with fine-tuning alone, measured by macro-F1?” Scope: three classes; 5,000 comments to be labelled; one platform. The draft named an area and a technique. The revision names the data (Banglish comments), the method (continued pre-training), the comparison (fine-tuning alone) and the outcome (macro-F1) — it can now be answered and checked."},{"u":"worked-thesis.html#stage2","w":"Worked thesis","t":"Searching and screening","x":"Searched Google Scholar for “Bangla sentiment analysis”; saved the first 30 results. “Thirty papers from one query on one site is a sample, not a search. You missed the code-mixed work entirely. Keep a log so you can describe it in the thesis.” A search log with three databases (Google Scholar, IEEE Xplore, ACL Anthology), a Boolean string — (“code-mixed” OR “code-switched” OR Banglish) AND sentiment AND (Bangla OR Bengali) — dates and counts; written inclusion and exclusion criteria; 549 found → 61 screened in → 24 read in full → 17 included. A logged, repeatable search with explicit criteria can be defended in the viva (“how do you know you did not miss important work?”). It also found the code-mixed papers that the first query missed."},{"u":"worked-thesis.html#stage3","w":"Worked thesis","t":"Reading and comparing three papers","x":"One paragraph summarising each paper, in the order they were found. “Summaries do not show me what you learned. Put the papers side by side: what data, what method, what result, and what did each one not do?” Literature-matrix rows: Paper 1 (2021) formal Bangla news comments, BiLSTM, F1 0.71, formal script only. Paper 2 (2022) code-mixed hate speech, mBERT, F1 0.76, no sentiment labels. Paper 3 (2023) BanglaBERT on six tasks, strong on formal text, not tested on Latin-script Bangla. The matrix makes the pattern visible: no paper combines code-mixed Latin-script Bangla, sentiment and a Bangla-specific pre-trained model. That pattern becomes the gap."},{"u":"worked-thesis.html#stage4","w":"Worked thesis","t":"Stating the gap","x":"“There is very little work on Banglish sentiment analysis, so this research is important.” “‘Little work’ is not a gap, and ‘important’ is not an argument. What exactly has not been done, and why does it matter that it has not?” “Models pre-trained on formal Bangla perform well on news and reviews [3], and domain-adaptive pre-training improves other languages’ social-media tasks, yet no study has tested whether it helps sentiment classification of Latin-script Banglish, and no labelled Banglish sentiment dataset is public. This thesis addresses both.” The revision states precisely what is missing, supports each claim with a citation, and ties the gap directly to the research question and the contributions."},{"u":"worked-thesis.html#stage5","w":"Worked thesis","t":"The evaluation plan","x":"Random 80/20 split; compare BanglaBERT with and without continued pre-training; report accuracy. “Accuracy on imbalanced classes will flatter you. Comments from the same page are similar — a random split leaks. Where is a simple baseline? How will you know a difference is real?” 70/15/15 split, stratified by class and grouped by Facebook page. Baselines: majority class, TF-IDF + logistic regression, BanglaBERT fine-tuned. Metric: macro-F1. Five seeds per model; mean ± sd; paired t-test over seeds plus a bootstrap confidence interval on the test set. Same tuning budget for every model. Each change answers a likely examiner question: leakage (grouped split), metric choice (macro-F1), whether the task is easy (simple baselines) and whether the gain is real (seeds, test and interval)."},{"u":"worked-thesis.html#stage6","w":"Worked thesis","t":"Results, ablation and errors","x":"“Our model achieved 0.83 macro-F1, outperforming all baselines.” (best seed of five) “Reporting your best seed is selective reporting. Show all runs. Which part of the method helps? Where does it still fail?” Main table, mean ± sd over 5 seeds: majority 0.21, TF-IDF + LR 0.68 ± 0.01, BanglaBERT 0.78 ± 0.02, ours 0.81 ± 0.01 (+3.0 points; 95% bootstrap CI 1 to 5 points; paired t-test p = 0.004). Ablation: without continued pre-training 0.781. Error analysis of 100 errors: sarcasm 31, mixed sentiment 24, spelling variants 18, label noise 9, other 18. The honest mean over seeds is lower than the best seed, but it is defensible. The ablation shows which part matters; the error analysis explains the remaining failures and gives future work (sarcasm)."},{"u":"worked-thesis.html#stage7","w":"Worked thesis","t":"Ethics and data","x":"Comments scraped with a script and shared in full on GitHub. “These are people’s words, with their names. Check the platform’s terms, remove identifiers and think about what you can release.” Comments collected from public pages with permitted tools; usernames and links removed before labelling; stored on a university drive; only text and labels released, for research use; AI-tool use (grammar checking only) declared. Public is not the same as free to republish. The revision protects the people in the data and keeps the dataset usable for others."},{"u":"worked-thesis.html#stage8","w":"Worked thesis","t":"The abstract and the final claim","x":"“In this thesis we propose a novel deep-learning approach that achieves state-of-the-art performance on Banglish sentiment analysis.” “Novel? State of the art compared with what? An abstract needs the problem, what you did and the actual numbers.” “Social-media comments in Bangladesh often mix Bangla and English in Latin script. Models trained on formal Bangla perform poorly on such text, and no labelled dataset exists. We labelled 5,000 Facebook comments and compared our model with three baselines. Continued pre-training of BanglaBERT on one million unlabelled code-mixed comments raised macro-F1 from 0.78 to 0.81 (95% CI +0.01 to +0.05; five seeds), and sarcasm caused 31% of the remaining errors. We release the dataset and code.” Every claim in the revision is specific and supported by a number from the results; the inflated words (“novel”, “state of the art”) are gone."},{"u":"worked-thesis.html#stage9","w":"Worked thesis","t":"The defence","x":"Planned answer to “Is +3 F1 meaningful?”: “Yes, it is significant.” “They will ask what significant means. Give the size, the uncertainty and what it means for a user.” “It is 3 points of macro-F1, consistent over all five seeds; the 95% interval on the test set is 1 to 5 points. Accuracy rose from 0.81 to 0.84, so about three more comments in every hundred are classified correctly — mostly negative comments in Latin script, the class that matters most for moderation.” A strong viva answer gives the number, its uncertainty and its practical meaning, and admits the limits (one platform, one test set)."}];
